WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.240
Andrew Huberman: [Opening theme music]

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.640
Welcome to the Huberman Lab Guest
series, where I and an expert

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:07.180
guest discuss s cience and science
based tools for everyday life.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.080
I'm Andrew Huberman, and I'm a professor
of neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:12.349 --> 00:00:13.730
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:18.349
Today's episode marks the second episode
in our four episode series with Dr.

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.509
Paul Conti about mental health.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:23.830
The first episode in the series
dealt with how to understand and

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.510
assess your level of mental health.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.019
Today's episode is about how
to improve your mental health.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.230
I do want to emphasize that you do not
need to have heard or seen the first

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.339
episode in order to understand or glean
important information from today's episode

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.900
about how to improve your mental health.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.769
But I do encourage you to go and
listen to the first episode at

00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:43.379
some point if you have not already.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.500
Today's episode deals with several
topics important to all of us, as well as

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.459
protocols to improve one's mental health.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:53.190
For instance, you will learn how to
guide yourself through a process of self

00:00:53.209 --> 00:00:57.930
inquiry in which you address certain key
questions about your drives, your level

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.750
of aggressive drive, pleasure drive,
and the so called generative drive.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.920
These are essential things to understand
about oneself if you want to guide

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.839
yourself toward your aspirations, and
if you want to understand how your

00:01:09.849 --> 00:01:13.679
subconscious processing is influencing
your thoughts and your behaviors and

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.450
your feelings in ways that sometimes
serve your aspirations, and in other

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:19.740
ways that can hinder your aspirations.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:20.200
Dr.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.130
Conti shares with us a way of assessing
our internal narratives, as well as

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:28.649
a way of creating a constructive self
awareness and an understanding of

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:32.330
where those narratives and that self
awareness stem from in our childhood,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.620
so that we can navigate forward
with the greatest sense of agency.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.000
We also talk about how to move
past common hindrances to improving

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
one's mental health, such as
overcoming intrusive thoughts.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.709
And perhaps most importantly, today's
episode provides information and

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:49.229
protocols that anyone can use to
cultivate their generative drive,

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.240
which is a hallmark of mental health.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:52.560
Just a reminder that Dr.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.610
Paul Conti has generously provided
a few diagrams that we include as

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.110
PDFs in the show note captions.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:01.729
They are completely zero cost to access,
and they can help you understand some

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.380
of the material that was discussed
in t he first episode of this series,

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:07.440
as well as the current episode about
how to improve your mental health.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:11.450
And while those simple PDF diagrams
are certainly not necessary in order

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:14.900
to understand the material in today's
discussion or in the other discussions

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.200
of this series, many people find them
useful, so I encourage you to check out

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.839
those links in the show note captions.

00:02:19.969 --> 00:02:23.260
Before we begin, I'd like to emphasize
that this podcast is separate from my

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.840
teaching and research roles at Stanford.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.690
It is, however, part of my desire and
effort to bring zero cost to consumer

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:32.080
information about science and science
related tools to the general public.

