00:05:23.049 --> 00:05:25.550
And now for my discussion
about mental health with Dr.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:25.960
Paul Conti.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:27.200
Dr.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.400
Conti, welcome back.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:28.880
Paul Conti: Thank you.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:32.390
Andrew Huberman: In the first episode
of this series, you laid out for us

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:37.589
in a very structured way what true
mental health looks like, essentially

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.330
what we should all be aspiring to.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:45.550
And you touched on these themes of
agency and gratitude as verb states,

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:51.490
really, ways of being in the world
that allow everybody to have some sense

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:56.129
of well being, to have some sense of
themselves in a way that is kind to

00:05:56.130 --> 00:06:00.650
themselves and to others, and really
to feel good and do good in their life.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.570
Without question, this is
what people want, right?

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:08.410
You also spelled out for us these two
pillars, the structure of self and

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:12.969
the function of self, that consist
of a number of different things that

00:06:13.059 --> 00:06:17.630
from which geyser up or kind of give
rise to these feelings of empowerment,

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:19.599
humility, agency, and gratitude.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:25.520
And reminded us several times that when
we are challenged, when we're not doing

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.289
as well as we would like, that we need
to look back to the structure of self

00:06:29.289 --> 00:06:35.659
and the function of self and ask specific
questions in order to arrive or re arrive

00:06:35.879 --> 00:06:37.880
at this sense of agency and gratitude.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:42.950
Yes, I think it would be wonderful for
us if you could just recap the overall

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.830
model because it has the components that
I just mentioned, but some subtlety and

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:54.019
some really key aspects of these pillars
of structure of self and function of self.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.540
I think if people keep in mind for
today's episode, which is about

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:02.320
challenges that people commonly face,
and even, if you will, phenotypes

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:03.540
that we see commonly out there.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.620
For people that haven't heard of
phenotypes, phenotypes are the

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:08.640
typical appearance of something.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:13.990
So there is the phenotype of the anxious
person, the phenotype of the person who

00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:16.020
just can't seem to get out of a rut.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:19.560
There's the phenotype of the traumatized
person and these things play out

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.659
differently in different individuals,
men and women, boys and girls.

00:07:23.059 --> 00:07:28.469
But we're going to visit many of the most
common phenotypes out there and think

00:07:28.469 --> 00:07:33.719
about how to do better, be better, feel
better through the lens of the model

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.880
that we spelled out in episode one.

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:40.830
And of course, if people have not seen
or heard episode one, today's discussion

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:42.480
will still be entirely accessible to them.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:47.510
So in keeping with that, if you could just
give us an overview of what this structure

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:51.969
of the healthy self looks like as a
roadmap for where we're all headed today.

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:52.870
Paul Conti: Thank you.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:53.630
Thanks very much.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:59.280
Revisiting the pillars is, I think,
the best place to start, because

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.150
there really are routes to understand.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:06.160
And if we understand, then we can
strategize, we can make change, right?

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:07.219
We can make things better.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:12.630
So the first pillar, the structure of
self, starts with the unconscious mind.

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:18.040
This incredibly complicated biological
supercomputer that's firing a mile a

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:23.210
minute underneath the surface in us and
is throwing up to the surface all sorts

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:28.020
of thoughts and ideas and states that
then the conscious mind apprehends.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.980
Then our awareness comes into play, and
then we have defense mechanisms that

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:37.340
sort of rise up from the unconscious
mind, and they circle and sort of gird

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.610
themselves around the conscious mind,
which they can do in an unhealthy way or

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.680
in a healthy way or anything in between.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.570
And then the character structure is
sort of the nest around all of that.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:54.420
And it's from the character structure
that we are engaging in the world

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.099
in the ways that we're engaging.

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:58.539
It's our active engagement
with the world around us.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:01.273
And the idea is that the
self grows out of that.

00:09:01.273 --> 00:09:05.050
It grows out of that nest sitting on
top of the unconscious mind to the

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:08.629
conscious mind rising above the defense
mechanisms and the character structure.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:12.710
And if we go back to that, when
we're trying to understand ourselves,

00:09:13.429 --> 00:09:16.939
trying to understand states of health
as well as states of unhappiness or

00:09:17.299 --> 00:09:21.830
states that aren't healthy, by going
back and looking at the structure,

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.650
we can learn a tremendous amount.

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:28.540
And the other side, the other
pillar, is the function of self.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:30.669
And it really starts
with a self awareness.

00:09:30.679 --> 00:09:34.059
The awareness that, hey, there
is an I, I am in the world.

00:09:34.059 --> 00:09:37.005
This is 24 hours in the day are
going to pass today, and I'm going

00:09:37.005 --> 00:09:38.319
to be doing one thing or another.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.120
I'm to some very significant extent
deciding how am I going to engage in

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:44.670
the world around me during that time.

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:48.530
So on top of that are the
defense mechanisms in action.

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:50.849
So defense mechanisms,
remember, are unconscious.

00:09:51.089 --> 00:09:54.609
So there's a lot then going on
inside of us that's determining

00:09:54.609 --> 00:09:56.220
sort of the field set of options.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:01.270
There may be a lot of automaticity
that narrows down the set of options

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:05.079
of what we may entertain, what we
may be aware of, what we may decide.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:08.219
And that could happen for better
or for worse, depending upon the

00:10:08.219 --> 00:10:09.819
health of the defense mechanisms.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:12.800
But on top of that lies salient.

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:16.890
So the idea then we would next visit,
okay, what are we paying attention to.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.100
What's coming from inside,
what's coming from outside.

00:10:20.309 --> 00:10:24.359
And we have to not pay attention to
many, many things in order to pay

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.930
attention to whatever our attention
has alighted on at the moment.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:33.270
So it's a complex process, and it's
worth looking at very closely if

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:34.660
we want to understand ourselves.

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:39.449
So after thinking about the defense
mechanisms in action, the unconscious

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:44.260
aspects of how we're engaging with
the world, then next to consider

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.619
is salience, which is sort of
where does the mind arrive at rest?

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:50.599
Where does the mind trend towards?

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:51.910
Is it something internal?

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:53.140
Is it something external?

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:55.690
What are all the things we're
not paying attention to in order

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.170
to pay attention to something?

00:10:57.809 --> 00:10:59.780
And is that thing healthy?

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:00.640
Is it not healthy?

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:01.810
Is it serving us well?

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.510
So there's so much to
understand about salience.

00:11:04.959 --> 00:11:07.979
And then the next step beyond
that is understanding behavior.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:10.910
How are we engaging with
the world around us?

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:12.310
What are our behavioral choices?

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:14.460
What are our automatic behaviors?

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:17.710
And then sitting on top of
all of that are our strivings.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:22.610
So we have a sense of wanting something
in the world around us, and what is

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:25.560
that, and how are we trying to get
to it, and how does it make us feel?

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:31.285
So if we look at the ten elements, the
five under the structure of self and

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.990
the five under the function of self,
then what we're really looking at is

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:36.900
sort of like looking at ten cabinets.

00:11:37.599 --> 00:11:40.740
And if we're trying to understand
ourselves, whether we're trying to just

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:44.829
generally understand ourselves or we're
trying to get at a problem, then looking

00:11:44.829 --> 00:11:46.870
in all ten of those cabinets makes sense.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:51.079
Some of them will be bare, meaning that
they may seem to have very little to

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:53.010
do with the problem we're bringing.

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:54.530
And we kind of maintain an open mind.

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:57.660
We may be led back to that cabinet,
and there may be something there.

00:11:57.889 --> 00:12:01.990
But what usually happens is if we look
in all ten places, we find a couple

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:06.540
where there's some rich material to
explore, sort of the X marks the spot,

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:10.689
and then we go and we dig there to sort
of mix metaphors we dig in the cabinet

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:11.840
where we're going to find something.

00:12:12.349 --> 00:12:15.930
And then it leads forward
a process of understanding.

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:20.110
And if we're bringing those things into
line, where we have a healthy structure

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:23.569
of self and a healthy function of self,
and we're aware of all of this and we're

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:27.109
working on it, we're self aware, and
we're paying attention to everything

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:32.410
built on top of that, then what we end
up with is a sense of humility, because

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:38.390
one cannot be anything but respectful,
compassionate understanding the complexity

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:42.745
of all of this and understanding,
how does it manifest itself in us?

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:45.510
And just the very fact that
we can make our ways in the

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:48.579
world, right, is so impressive.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:52.800
And in a way, I think it brings to
us a respect, just a respect for

00:12:53.039 --> 00:12:54.980
being here, navigating the world.

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:57.790
And I think of that
respect is born humility.

00:12:57.790 --> 00:13:01.480
The complexity of us, the fact that
millions of things are going on underneath

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:06.970
the surface, millions of neurotransmission
and endocrinological function.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.289
All of this is going on under the surface.

00:13:08.289 --> 00:13:09.389
I'm not even aware of it.

00:13:09.389 --> 00:13:13.040
And then it kicks up to the surface,
generates a tremendous amount of

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.640
respect for the complexity and also
the diligence and perseverance it

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:19.410
takes us to navigate through the world.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:22.400
And I think built upon that
understanding is a sense of

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:24.789
humility and a sense of empowerment.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:27.900
And the humility and
empowerment in action, right?

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:32.040
So expressed, become agency and gratitude.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:36.310
And agency and gratitude, as you said
at the beginning, we're seeing as verbs.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:39.210
That's how we're living life.

00:13:39.220 --> 00:13:42.749
It's through the lens, so to
speak, of agency and gratitude

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:44.860
that we're actively living.

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:47.980
And again, I would put forth that
when we look at measures of human

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:53.590
happiness across disciplines and across
time, this is always what we see is

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:57.840
some way of describing how agency
and gratitude, together, as verbs,

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.309
manifest and then create happiness.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:05.319
It's the state that we're seeking
to be in, because from that state of

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:09.650
active agency and active gratitude,
we achieve what it is that I

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:11.180
think we're really searching for.

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:16.790
And there are infinite words throughout
human history to describe what that is.

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:22.299
We might choose to use words like
peacefulness, a sense of peace, a sense

00:14:22.299 --> 00:14:27.530
of contentment, being delighted by things,
like just being amazed and impressed

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.490
by things in the world around us.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:32.400
Like, this is a state
that we're striving for.

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:34.890
And I think when people talk about
happiness and what we're really

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:36.819
trying to get to, it's this.

00:14:37.509 --> 00:14:40.030
But it's not that these
things are passive.

00:14:40.559 --> 00:14:45.089
These things are coming from the
active agency, the active gratitude.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:49.760
And they're then interacting with
a generative drive within us.

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:51.419
We have an aggressive drive.

00:14:51.429 --> 00:14:54.390
We have a pleasure drive like this
has been thought about now for a

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:58.449
long, long time within mental health
and validated in a lot of ways.

00:14:58.699 --> 00:15:01.830
But what hasn't been validated is
that they're the only things, right?

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:03.910
We see human beings striving.

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:07.819
We see human beings wanting better for
themselves and for the world around them.

00:15:08.059 --> 00:15:11.459
We see acts of kindness that
seem to be rooted to nothing

00:15:11.500 --> 00:15:13.579
other than the act of kindness.

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:19.469
We have within us a drive to know, to
understand, to learn, to make better.

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:23.020
And that has been described as many,
many things across human history.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:26.089
But I think the words we might
choose are a generative drive, a

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:28.150
drive to create and to make better.

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:35.359
And it's the generative drive as something
active within us that is then aligning

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:40.500
with agency and gratitude, the active
ways in which we express ourselves.

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:44.829
And then that altogether brings
us the peace, the contentment,

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:45.880
the sense of delight.

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:48.970
Sometimes that may exist
in us in a state of rest.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:53.189
But very often it's existing
in us in a state of activity.

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:57.570
And that's why people find the quote
unquote happiness, like what people

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:00.860
are seeking, not just in meditation.

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:05.179
Sometimes we can find it there, but
people also find it in action, right?

00:16:05.179 --> 00:16:08.470
They find it in doing that thing that
they love to do, or in taking care

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:10.070
of someone and learning something.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:16.140
So when we look at all of this, we
can then have a route of understanding

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:20.690
what is going on inside of us and
how we can make the changes that

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:25.270
let us be in this state, which is
really the state that we are seeking.

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:28.119
Andrew Huberman: I really appreciate
that you highlight that agency and

00:16:28.119 --> 00:16:34.010
gratitude are verb states from which
peace, contentment and delight emerge.

00:16:34.030 --> 00:16:39.180
And also the way that you explain
the generative drive that is distinct

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:43.240
from aggressive drives and pleasure
drives that exist in all of us.

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:49.510
I'm smiling because a number of
examples of peace, contentment, and

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:51.720
delight while in action come to mind.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:56.130
I mean, for me, podcasting, and in
particular, preparing for a podcast,

00:16:56.130 --> 00:17:00.829
trying to mine the literature and
figure out where the gems reside and

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:02.970
where the confusion could emerge.

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:06.510
And all of that brings about such
peace, contentment and delight for

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:08.240
me, but it's anything but passive.

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:14.910
Likewise, yesterday had the
experience of running into a puppy.

00:17:15.059 --> 00:17:18.579
It's been a while since I've owned
a dog, and dogs are delightful.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.310
Puppies are particularly delightful.

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:22.909
Paul Conti: I had the experience of seeing
you light up when you ran into the puppy.

00:17:22.969 --> 00:17:27.210
Andrew Huberman: You did, and
I'm still buzzing from that

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:28.860
short interaction with the puppy.

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:30.630
Downstairs the way Mariner puppy.

00:17:32.639 --> 00:17:36.699
I don't know why, but I just delight
in animals of most all kinds.

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:41.960
Not a fan of reptiles, sorry, reptile fans
so much, but I just drive so much energy

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:43.540
from it, and it felt like life energy.

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:47.180
And the way the animal is sort of
attentionally scattered is amusing

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:50.729
to me as compared to the dog that he
will eventually be, which is going

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:52.119
to be more linear in his thinking.

00:17:52.119 --> 00:17:55.219
It encapsulates so much of
the other things I love, like

00:17:55.219 --> 00:17:56.419
brain development, et cetera.

00:17:56.639 --> 00:18:01.580
Anyway, I highlight those examples
because there's nothing passive about it.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:08.609
It's pure delight and joy for me, and it
intersects with other delights and joys.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:12.989
And I think that as you describe agency
and gratitude, peace, contentment and

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:18.030
delight in these generative forces as
well as other forces that exist in us,

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:23.300
I think it's really critical that people
understand that these are not states

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:27.539
that you sit down and place yourself
into, although perhaps one could through

00:18:28.269 --> 00:18:31.860
reflection or meditation or waking
up from a really great night's sleep,

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:36.609
things of that sort, but that these are
things that we can find ourselves awash

00:18:36.750 --> 00:18:39.340
in if we are doing the right things.

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:41.840
And those things can
oftentimes be very challenging.

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:48.530
So assuming I understand the way the
model is spelled out correctly, I'm

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:52.769
more and more delighted at the fact
that this is not just accessible in

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:57.429
one domain, but is accessible in many,
many different domains for everybody.

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.280
This is not something unique to my
experience, even though I give examples

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:07.439
from my own life, but that we really all
do have access to this if we're looking

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:11.980
in those cupboards, those ten cupboards,
and asking the right questions and.

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:14.490
Paul Conti: To maybe comment
even a little further on the

00:19:14.490 --> 00:19:16.760
experience of you and the dog.

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:21.070
So it was an experience of delight.

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:25.559
And you enjoyed it, and it brought
a sense of peace and contentment,

00:19:25.599 --> 00:19:26.989
like, all of that happens.

00:19:27.679 --> 00:19:29.180
But think about what that's linked to.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:33.860
I believe there's a strong sense of
agency in you that you are enacting.

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:36.340
There's a strong gratitude
in you that you're enacting.

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:38.320
You're handling your life in a way.

00:19:38.630 --> 00:19:43.030
And also for all of us, good things always
come with good fortune, but it comes with

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:46.740
our strivings and our achievements that
you're in a place to delight in that.

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:50.159
If you are unhappy, like, I don't
like what I'm doing, I'm angry,

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:54.220
I'm frustrated, then there's no
room in you to find the delight.

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:59.920
And the delight that you find is also
very much linked to the generative drive.

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:03.830
It makes me think of how you
loved and nurtured Costello.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:08.220
So you have it in you to love and
nurture a dog, and you have done

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:09.800
that in a really wonderful way.

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:13.430
And that generative drive is part
and parcel of the delight you

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:17.039
feel when you see a dog, because
you love dogs and you think about

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:18.979
nurturing, and it all comes together.

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:24.600
The agency and the gratitude expressed
as verbs puts you in a position to

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:27.640
have that sense of delight which is
so intertwined with your generative

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:33.480
drive, with a sense of caretaking,
a sense of creating the beyond self.

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:36.340
Because although you enjoyed
and loved Casello, you enjoyed

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:38.250
and loved his happiness, right?

00:20:38.259 --> 00:20:39.749
So it all comes together.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.179
And I think it's interesting
because in some ways it's a

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:44.330
simple example, but that's life.

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:49.320
Life has its big moments, but so
much of our lives are the smaller

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:50.580
moments that link together.

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:53.979
And I think that smaller
moment becomes a big example.

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.149
Andrew Huberman: I appreciate
that you mentioned Costello.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:01.380
For listeners of this podcast that have
tuned into early episodes, Costello was

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:03.420
the source of the background snoring.

00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:04.850
For those of you that
haven't, you can go check.

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:09.930
He was a 90 pound English bulldog
mastiff who had many skills,

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:11.940
the best of which was snoring.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:17.199
So in addition to the generative
drive, which is something that we

00:21:17.199 --> 00:21:21.580
certainly want to talk more about
today, you mentioned these other drives,

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:23.870
aggressive drives and pleasure drives.

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:28.300
And much of what we're talking about
today is going to be where people can

00:21:28.300 --> 00:21:30.489
go wrong or where people struggle.

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:35.980
We are also, of course, going to go
deeply into where people succeed and

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:40.939
in particular where people can ask
questions of themselves, in particular

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:47.500
what is working for them and why, as
a route to understanding how to sift

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:52.170
through those cupboards and understand
what's not working and why, and come

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:56.300
up with real actionable answers and
then the ability to move forward.

00:21:56.310 --> 00:22:01.600
So if you would, could you tell
us a little bit more about drives?

00:22:01.600 --> 00:22:06.370
Generally, when I hear drives, I
can't help as a neuroscientist,

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:11.059
but default to, okay, the dopamine
circuit, or the endogenous opioid

00:22:11.059 --> 00:22:13.449
circuit, or the serotonergic circuit.

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:17.200
But how do you conceptualize
drives within us?

00:22:18.490 --> 00:22:23.130
And then perhaps you could tell us what
the nature of aggressive drives and

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:25.879
pleasure drives and generative drives.

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:30.459
Paul Conti: So the concept of a
drive, the definition of a drive, is

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:33.330
something that's intrinsic to humans.

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:36.860
So we could look at it as a motivation.

00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:43.789
I mean, we don't just lie on the ground
and do nothing until we passively die.

00:22:44.270 --> 00:22:48.930
So something is going on inside
of us that is driving us to

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:50.980
do something other than that.

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:55.580
And historically, the thinking in the
field arising from early psychodynamic

00:22:56.050 --> 00:23:01.209
principles, the theory in the field that
has really dominated the field, either

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:06.559
directly or indirectly, in so many ways,
has been that there are two drives within

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:09.920
us, that there's aggression and pleasure.

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:12.000
And again, these are just words, right?

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.564
So we could apply many, many
words, which is why, of course,

00:23:14.564 --> 00:23:16.210
we want to define what that means.

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:20.189
So aggression, even though we're using
that word for it, because the word

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:28.169
for it is commonly used, but it means
sort of forward active engagement.

00:23:28.590 --> 00:23:33.749
So a good, healthy amount of aggression
using that word for the drive

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:35.789
would be a strong sense of agency.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:41.770
Too little aggression can be a
problem, then the person isn't

00:23:41.779 --> 00:23:43.280
bringing themselves to bear.

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:47.960
So there's too little in the way
of self determination, forward

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:50.620
movement, empowerment, agency, right.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:56.149
And in the same way, too much of
this drive becomes actual aggression.

00:23:56.160 --> 00:24:00.780
So the idea that I want more,
and if I can't get it in certain

00:24:00.789 --> 00:24:02.190
ways, I'll just take it, right.

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:07.929
So it starts to become what we more
map to the word aggression, which would

00:24:07.930 --> 00:24:10.400
be something negative in most cases.

00:24:10.599 --> 00:24:12.900
Andrew Huberman: Like a
desire or a tendency to harm.

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:14.310
Paul Conti: Sure.

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:18.299
As aggressive drives get higher,
which you see why they're in us,

00:24:18.300 --> 00:24:22.120
because let's say we're defending
ourselves, or you're defending a

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:24.780
family member or like an entire family.

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:29.940
Then it makes sense to have high levels of
aggression if your family is threatened.

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:34.980
So those drives are in us at potentially
those high levels for a reason.

00:24:35.299 --> 00:24:39.730
But we certainly access very high
levels of aggression without the

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:43.840
indication of preservation of
life or preservation of safety.

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:49.109
The thought is, that's a drive
in us, and that gets us up and

00:24:49.110 --> 00:24:50.749
off the ground, so to speak.

00:24:51.329 --> 00:24:55.510
And that the other drive, then, is
pleasure, which, again, doesn't just

00:24:55.510 --> 00:24:57.710
mean that we all want to be hedonists.

00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:03.359
So pleasure could be even the
pleasure of relief and safety.

00:25:04.599 --> 00:25:07.869
We're all back in the cave together, and
we roll the stone in front of the door.

00:25:08.549 --> 00:25:09.209
We're safe.

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:13.139
Throughout human development,
pleasure comes in a lot of ways.

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:18.550
It can come through the pleasure of food
or other people, friendship, romance, sex.

00:25:18.590 --> 00:25:20.990
There are a lot of ways
we can achieve pleasure.

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:24.619
It can be relief of things that
are unpleasant, relief of pain.

00:25:24.849 --> 00:25:27.900
But there's a drive towards
this in humans, which, again,

00:25:27.910 --> 00:25:29.820
really does make sense.

00:25:29.859 --> 00:25:34.070
And too little of it, again, can be
problematic because the person, then isn't

00:25:34.430 --> 00:25:38.570
motivated to sort of seek things because
they're not anticipating or don't receive

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:44.200
gratification and too much of a drive
for pleasure can also create problems.

00:25:45.219 --> 00:25:49.320
We can kind of see how these two
drives, like, okay, they get us up

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:51.280
and off the ground, so to speak.

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:54.250
But the question is, do
they explain everything?

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:58.550
And it's a very important question,
because if they explain everything,

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:09.140
then there's not room for behaviors
and choices that are beyond the self.

00:26:09.590 --> 00:26:09.850
Right?

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:12.250
There's not an explanation
for the person who.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:18.230
I'll give you an example of a person
I've taken care of, who's just a very

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:22.569
strong swimmer, knows how to swim,
has swimmed throughout his life, who

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:25.929
was in a place, I saw video of it,
where there had been a hurricane,

00:26:25.930 --> 00:26:28.600
and the waves were so frightening.

00:26:29.190 --> 00:26:35.349
They were just this huge surf, and there
were people who had gotten dragged out.

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:39.390
And you just see him,
he runs into the water.

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:40.990
He runs in and he goes.

00:26:40.990 --> 00:26:43.050
And he was really at risk.

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:45.950
He needed to be saved
himself, but he saved them.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:50.879
And I do not believe you can
explain that through these drives.

00:26:51.080 --> 00:26:52.969
I don't think you can say,
well, he was aggressive.

00:26:52.969 --> 00:26:56.480
He wanted to go and do something that
was imposing himself on the world,

00:26:56.480 --> 00:27:00.570
or he got pleasure in thinking,
I'm strong enough to go do this.

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:05.090
I mean, I think we're really gyrating,
we're contorting ourselves, right,

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:07.240
in order to explain it that way.

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:09.980
If we think there's a goodness
in that man's heart, like, I

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:11.960
know there's a goodness in that
man's heart, I know him, right?

00:27:11.960 --> 00:27:17.280
And that goodness sees him in the
moment, and he knows that maybe

00:27:17.330 --> 00:27:19.230
he can save them, maybe he can.

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:20.760
He's not sure, but maybe he can.

00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:22.690
So the next thing you
know, he's in the water.

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:27.600
And I think things like the love and
nurturing of other people, of children,

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:29.879
love and nurturing of animals, of plants.

00:27:30.219 --> 00:27:33.360
There are things inside of us that we
can't explain with those two drives.

00:27:33.770 --> 00:27:38.030
And I think they have led to
a very sort of darker way of

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:39.320
just conceiving of humans.

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:46.129
I think it's a reason why now you look
at us in the modern day and age, we come

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:48.000
at humans through the lens of pathology.

00:27:48.009 --> 00:27:53.029
I mean, there's a very, very thick
book that if a person is assessing,

00:27:53.029 --> 00:27:56.899
another person is thinking about, like,
okay, what numbers in that book apply.

00:27:57.410 --> 00:28:01.060
Which is like, that's not the way
to go about understanding humans.

00:28:01.060 --> 00:28:03.730
And I think if we just think
there are those two drives, we're

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:05.430
not doing justice to humans.

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:07.030
One, I think it's not true.

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:08.799
I think it's evident that it's not true.

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:11.579
And then if we're framing
it in a way that's not true.

00:28:11.859 --> 00:28:14.780
We are not appropriately
respectful of humans.

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:19.440
And if we come from what I believe
to be the truth, that there is a

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:24.320
generative drive in us, a drive for
the beyond self, a drive to make

00:28:24.320 --> 00:28:28.219
things better, whether it has anything
really directly to do with me or not.

00:28:28.219 --> 00:28:32.129
And as with the other drives, there
can be more or less, in people, a

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:37.440
combination of nature and nurture what
genetically is in us, a predisposition

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:41.180
based upon the genetic lineage that
comes down to us and the recombination.

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:44.679
And now we're a unique person with
a unique set of drives, but they are

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:50.190
impacted by the genetics and then they're
impacted by life experience, a more

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:52.440
