00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:35.700
learnings, and I derive value and
learnings from other accounts as well.

00:43:36.260 --> 00:43:40.779
But there are these milestones,
if you will, in life.

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:45.830
I mean, not that everyone has to go to
college and get married and have a family.

00:43:45.830 --> 00:43:49.230
There are a lot of different paths through
life that I would consider successful.

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:54.290
But in some sense, there are milestones,
like we want to move forward.

00:43:54.290 --> 00:43:58.810
There's this phenomenon nowadays of a
lot of young people so called failure

00:43:58.810 --> 00:44:03.419
to launch, like they're not leaving
home or they're not finding a vocation.

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:05.430
They're not feeling as if
they're good at anything.

00:44:06.360 --> 00:44:10.460
Or they have the sense that unless
you're going to be top 1% in

00:44:10.460 --> 00:44:11.950
something, it's not worth trying.

00:44:12.290 --> 00:44:16.879
But they can still find what most
people would describe as pleasures.

00:44:16.889 --> 00:44:20.120
Like they might enjoy food
maybe a little too much.

00:44:20.410 --> 00:44:22.919
They enjoy alcohol
maybe a little too much.

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:25.980
They enjoy social media or video
games, maybe a little too much.

00:44:25.980 --> 00:44:29.110
And I say a little too much because
it's providing more or less a

00:44:30.370 --> 00:44:34.560
sink or a reservoir for their
aggressive and pleasure drives.

00:44:34.809 --> 00:44:39.650
That's not moving them forward in
the standard milestones of life.

00:44:40.740 --> 00:44:42.019
I hear about that a lot.

00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:43.990
I see that a lot.

00:44:45.030 --> 00:44:50.470
So it's a slightly more complex phenotype
than described before as just simply

00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:53.270
the a motivated or non motivated person.

00:44:53.590 --> 00:44:59.530
But what do you think of the
phenotype I just described?

00:45:00.350 --> 00:45:02.560
Paul Conti: Because we're unique.

00:45:02.640 --> 00:45:05.400
Each person is unique,
although we fit categories.

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:09.299
So there are categories a person
there could fit that could be

00:45:09.300 --> 00:45:10.849
different from what I'm saying.

00:45:11.509 --> 00:45:15.020
But I think most people,
they say on balance.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:17.070
What is most prominent?

00:45:17.090 --> 00:45:23.200
I think what is most prominent in that
situation is there's something out

00:45:23.200 --> 00:45:25.460
of balance in the generative drive

00:45:25.650 --> 00:45:29.530
.
 And what you see a lot of times is
the person has a generative drive

00:45:30.219 --> 00:45:35.030
in them that's higher than their
ability to realize that drive.

00:45:35.039 --> 00:45:37.170
The generative drive then is frustrated.

00:45:37.170 --> 00:45:38.600
So I'll give an example.

00:45:38.600 --> 00:45:42.750
And it's a real true story of a person
who had worked very, very hard, gone

00:45:42.760 --> 00:45:46.129
to school for a long time, and had
achieved a very high paying job.

00:45:46.150 --> 00:45:47.310
And that was the goal.

00:45:47.660 --> 00:45:50.059
It's a prestigious job,
it's a high paying job.

00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:59.090
And the person for a while was doing quite
well at it, and things went relatively

00:45:59.090 --> 00:46:00.720
rapidly in a negative direction.

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:02.730
So maybe for a little while,
the person's doing okay.

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:07.399
Then the person becomes very negligent
of themselves and their environment

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:09.250
when they're not at the job.

00:46:09.259 --> 00:46:14.440
So the house is a mess, things are dirty,
the person is wasting time with things.

00:46:14.450 --> 00:46:18.190
So this is a person who enjoys
it wasn't exactly video games.

00:46:18.840 --> 00:46:20.229
Let's say it could have been.

00:46:20.389 --> 00:46:24.290
Well, it enjoys them to a certain
degree and can really gain pleasure

00:46:24.290 --> 00:46:26.639
and feel good about the time spent

00:46:27.049 --> 00:46:28.529
.
 But starts spending too much time.

00:46:28.920 --> 00:46:33.580
Now what was pleasurable starts
becoming a distraction mechanism.

00:46:34.160 --> 00:46:37.410
And then what that transitioned
to was overuse of alcohol.

00:46:37.870 --> 00:46:42.760
So now you have either something that
is actually destructive and was negative

00:46:42.760 --> 00:46:45.320
to job performance towards the person.

00:46:45.330 --> 00:46:48.630
This wasn't a person who was drinking
a lot before, and this is a person who

00:46:48.630 --> 00:46:52.940
was miserable when they were drinking
or they were sort of wasting their time.

00:46:53.280 --> 00:46:54.490
And we're aware of all of this.

00:46:54.760 --> 00:47:01.219
Well, there was a very clear problem,
which is that that person had no

00:47:01.250 --> 00:47:03.750
interest in what they were doing.

00:47:04.609 --> 00:47:05.979
None whatsoever.

00:47:05.990 --> 00:47:11.630
It felt like the majority of waking
hours were spent in an automaton like

00:47:11.630 --> 00:47:17.600
way, but being awake and aware of the
tedium of it, the frustration of it.

00:47:17.759 --> 00:47:18.750
Andrew Huberman: The professional side.

00:47:18.770 --> 00:47:23.930
So they essentially had very little
intrinsic curiosity or desire to do the

00:47:23.930 --> 00:47:25.950
job that they were successfully doing.

00:47:26.010 --> 00:47:26.260
Paul Conti: Right.

00:47:26.260 --> 00:47:29.190
Which comes out only after exploration,
because it seems like, well,

00:47:29.190 --> 00:47:30.360
what's going on with this person?

00:47:30.860 --> 00:47:33.539
This person has a good job and
their life was going really well,

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:35.280
and they're doing well financially.

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:40.680
And is this person trying to
now overly indulge themselves?

00:47:40.680 --> 00:47:41.790
Is that why they're drinking?

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:42.509
What's going on?

00:47:42.930 --> 00:47:47.030
And what you feel is that this person
had a strong, generative drive, and it

00:47:47.030 --> 00:47:53.109
wasn't met one little bit by what he was
doing, which was creating such frustration

00:47:53.420 --> 00:47:56.740
inside that the person was either
taking himself online or doing something

00:47:56.740 --> 00:47:58.569
that was punitive and self injurious.

00:47:58.859 --> 00:48:00.209
And this is a real story.

00:48:00.209 --> 00:48:06.300
The person exchanged that job
for a job that paid a 10th of

00:48:06.300 --> 00:48:08.560
what the job they had paid.

00:48:08.970 --> 00:48:11.850
And the change in the
person's life was amazing.

00:48:11.910 --> 00:48:13.560
Like, I didn't know this guy could smile.

00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:15.160
He became happy.

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:16.639
He loved what he was doing.

00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:19.629
He sold the larger house,
bought a smaller house, kept it

00:48:19.630 --> 00:48:21.340
beautifully, like he was happy.

00:48:22.080 --> 00:48:23.660
That's what he needed to be happy.

00:48:23.940 --> 00:48:27.730
Because then the generative drive in
him, he loved what he was doing, gets

00:48:27.780 --> 00:48:32.269
enacted, it gets expressed, and then
other things can come then into line.

00:48:32.799 --> 00:48:37.110
He's not being over aggressive towards
himself and drinking too much because

00:48:37.110 --> 00:48:40.470
he's saying, oh, to help with you, to
the world around him and to himself.

00:48:40.980 --> 00:48:44.340
He's not taking something that
serves a purpose in his life.

00:48:44.340 --> 00:48:47.280
Like, again, if the example had been video
games, it would be like, yeah, great.

00:48:47.280 --> 00:48:52.539
You enjoy doing that X amount of time and
go do that and get gratification from it,

00:48:52.559 --> 00:48:54.590
as opposed to then over relying on it.

00:48:54.590 --> 00:48:56.520
And then it's not providing gratification.

00:48:56.530 --> 00:48:57.560
It becomes a distraction.

00:48:57.560 --> 00:49:02.020
So those things came back into
balance in his life, but there

00:49:02.030 --> 00:49:03.200
had to be the understanding.

00:49:03.200 --> 00:49:06.160
And I think there's a lot of that in
people who have a generative drive in

00:49:06.160 --> 00:49:11.379
them that they feel is frustrated by a
world around them that isn't cooperating.

00:49:11.379 --> 00:49:15.500
Now, do I think we can understand that
and change that in the vast majority

00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:16.990
of people who are in that place.

00:49:17.020 --> 00:49:19.680
Yes, but it has to be
looked at first, right.

00:49:19.680 --> 00:49:21.009
Because it's not always that.

00:49:21.059 --> 00:49:22.870
It's just that a lot of the time.

00:49:23.480 --> 00:49:26.120
So it has to be understood
what is it in that person?

00:49:26.120 --> 00:49:30.570
And then how do you go back to those
pillars and look at what's going on,

00:49:30.570 --> 00:49:31.920
that the person is in that place?

00:49:31.950 --> 00:49:35.250
Because the world can bring us
a lot of difficulties, right?

00:49:35.250 --> 00:49:38.879
And that person who now is saddled
with a lot more loans than they expect.

00:49:38.880 --> 00:49:42.480
I have tremendous compassion
for that and sympathy for that.

00:49:42.630 --> 00:49:43.559
That's real.

00:49:43.570 --> 00:49:43.920
Right?

00:49:44.170 --> 00:49:47.509
So people can be up against a lot of
things and that's just one of them, right?

00:49:47.770 --> 00:49:50.230
But it doesn't mean
that life can't be okay.

00:49:51.220 --> 00:49:55.973
It doesn't mean that, but the person has
to feel that there's some way, they have

00:49:56.110 --> 00:50:00.316
to understand enough about themselves
to say, okay, this is what this is.

00:50:00.316 --> 00:50:03.460
And I kind of see what this
is and why and how I'm here.

00:50:03.710 --> 00:50:08.580
And from there I can start to plot
a route to something that is better

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:11.520
because, yes, we have our difficulties
and we can have a lot of them, right?

00:50:11.720 --> 00:50:15.900
But for the vast majority of us, it's
not like they're not surmountable.

00:50:16.270 --> 00:50:18.040
We have to just understand them.

00:50:18.400 --> 00:50:21.760
And let's say if that person goes and
says, I'm going to get some help, and

00:50:21.760 --> 00:50:25.420
they go and someone says, okay, you get
ten sessions of cognitive behavioral

00:50:25.420 --> 00:50:28.109
therapy and you try and like, how
can that person think differently?

00:50:28.110 --> 00:50:29.060
Then they'll feel differently.

00:50:29.060 --> 00:50:32.020
And look, cognitive behavioral
therapy has its place, right?

00:50:32.320 --> 00:50:34.420
But it's not going to solve that.

00:50:34.889 --> 00:50:37.649
That person needs to understand
something about themselves, not redirect

00:50:37.650 --> 00:50:39.100
their thoughts to better places.

00:50:39.670 --> 00:50:44.250
So if the person gets a reflex, because
that reflex works well for the system,

00:50:45.030 --> 00:50:47.940
a reflex works well for the system
that's treating that person, for the

00:50:47.950 --> 00:50:52.200
medical system, the insurance system,
that person isn't helped one bit, right?

00:50:52.530 --> 00:50:54.309
And maybe a medicine can help.

00:50:54.840 --> 00:50:58.639
Maybe a medicine helps to just take down
the anxiety and the tension in the person.

00:50:58.639 --> 00:51:00.470
Then the person can sort
of think more about it.

00:51:00.940 --> 00:51:04.470
And truly medicine did help this person
because the idea of leaving the job.

00:51:04.970 --> 00:51:07.560
I'm leaving the prestige, I'm leaving
the money, is that okay to do like?

00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:10.450
It generated a lot of anxiety and
it helped to kind of bring the

00:51:10.450 --> 00:51:13.499
temperature down a little bit of
that so that he could think about

00:51:13.500 --> 00:51:16.500
it, engage in therapy, ultimately
navigate to where he wanted to be.

00:51:16.779 --> 00:51:18.280
Then we could back away from the medicine.

00:51:18.280 --> 00:51:20.050
So medicine has a role.

00:51:20.350 --> 00:51:25.540
But if he just got medicine, what
are the odds of that helping?

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:26.619
Like, zero, right?

00:51:26.619 --> 00:51:29.530
Because it's not going to make
the answers unless somehow the

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:31.970
person feels a little bit better
and figures it out on their own.

00:51:32.429 --> 00:51:33.650
It's not how it works, right?

00:51:33.650 --> 00:51:40.310
So medicine has its place, but a kind of
therapy that recognizes the limitations

00:51:40.310 --> 00:51:44.319
of medicine in most situations and
is designed to really help the person

00:51:44.349 --> 00:51:46.150
understand, like, that's what we need.

00:51:47.080 --> 00:51:50.180
Andrew Huberman: The example you gave
is a spectacular one because, as you

00:51:50.180 --> 00:51:52.390
mentioned, medication had its place.

00:51:53.440 --> 00:51:57.600
Perhaps even redirection of thought in
some sense had its place, because, as I

00:51:57.600 --> 00:52:04.365
recall, under the pillar of function of
self, one of the key items is salience.

00:52:04.740 --> 00:52:06.980
What we pay attention to
internally or externally, what

00:52:06.980 --> 00:52:08.240
our internal narratives are.

00:52:08.630 --> 00:52:14.700
But in staying with the example of this
individual, again as a phenotypic example

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:23.210
for everybody to learn something from, the
asking of better questions about oneself

00:52:23.210 --> 00:52:26.239
is really what leads to the understanding.

00:52:26.240 --> 00:52:31.940
So, like, better forms of inquiry,
to me, these better forms of

00:52:31.940 --> 00:52:37.549
inquiry, better questions are really
the cardiovascular exercise, the

00:52:37.550 --> 00:52:41.660
strength training, the flexibility
training, the mobility training,

00:52:41.920 --> 00:52:47.209
coordination training of physical
health just translate to mental health.

00:52:47.920 --> 00:52:48.950
Paul Conti: It's so interesting, right?

00:52:48.950 --> 00:52:52.690
Because if you think about it,
in the example I gave, both the

00:52:52.690 --> 00:52:54.759
therapy part through the system.

00:52:55.230 --> 00:52:56.770
The CBT has a place.

00:52:57.690 --> 00:53:00.280
And the medicine part also had a place.

00:53:00.290 --> 00:53:02.169
So both of those things have their role.

00:53:02.460 --> 00:53:06.378
But if we build the whole story of, like,
this is what this is, and this is how

00:53:06.378 --> 00:53:10.640
you're going to be helped around those
things, we don't help that person at all.

00:53:10.670 --> 00:53:13.760
In fact, we ultimately, if you
take on balance, you take all

00:53:13.760 --> 00:53:15.580
comers, we end up doing harm.

00:53:15.670 --> 00:53:19.000
Andrew Huberman: Well, in some ways,
if we stay with the analogy of physical

00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:21.650
health, it would be like the person
who wants to get in shape and then they

00:53:21.650 --> 00:53:26.230
get a, i'm not picking on peloton as
a brand, but just a stationary bike.

00:53:26.520 --> 00:53:29.780
And they pedal every morning and they
lose weight, their blood pressure

00:53:29.780 --> 00:53:30.970
goes down, they're doing better.

00:53:31.280 --> 00:53:35.040
But then at some point, we know
with certainty that if you just do

00:53:35.040 --> 00:53:37.529
the same form of exercise over and
over again, sooner or later you're

00:53:37.530 --> 00:53:38.680
going to get overuse injuries.

00:53:38.690 --> 00:53:40.789
So then there's like the lower
back piece and another piece

00:53:40.789 --> 00:53:42.710
and you become out of balance.

00:53:43.610 --> 00:53:46.890
But I guess this is stealing
from the Lance Armstrong book.

00:53:47.320 --> 00:53:48.990
But it's not about the bike, right?

00:53:49.840 --> 00:53:50.680
It's not about the bike.

00:53:50.680 --> 00:53:52.660
It's about the elevation of heart rate.

00:53:52.679 --> 00:53:56.809
It's about whatever other healthy
activities go along with exercising

00:53:56.809 --> 00:53:58.929
first thing in the morning and all
the things that you're not doing as a

00:53:58.930 --> 00:54:00.940
consequence of exercising in the morning.

00:54:00.950 --> 00:54:07.740
So it seems to me that these better
lines of inquiry as the path to better

00:54:07.740 --> 00:54:13.620
mental health, a better life, that sit
under these pillars of structure of self,

00:54:13.620 --> 00:54:15.930
function of self, are really the key.

00:54:16.180 --> 00:54:19.160
Paul Conti: But in this
example, the parallel that you

00:54:19.170 --> 00:54:20.600
made is even more dramatic.

00:54:20.600 --> 00:54:23.940
It wouldn't be the stationary bike, right?

00:54:23.940 --> 00:54:26.789
Because a stationary bike is
achieving a lot of ends, right.

00:54:26.960 --> 00:54:30.780
It would be more like telling
the person, you should walk more

00:54:30.790 --> 00:54:32.509
briskly when you're going upstairs.

00:54:33.290 --> 00:54:36.599
That's a good idea, but that's
not going to make the change.

00:54:37.129 --> 00:54:41.640
So the idea that some CBT, some medicine
makes sense, it's more like that.

00:54:41.640 --> 00:54:46.690
It's not that walking more briskly up the
stairs isn't a good thing, it's that we

00:54:46.690 --> 00:54:51.700
can't build the story around your whole
health is going to change based upon that.

00:54:51.880 --> 00:54:53.660
And then that's a problem.

00:54:53.660 --> 00:54:57.029
Then if the person thinks, just
walk more briskly up the stairs and

00:54:57.030 --> 00:55:00.490
you'll be healthier, because when
it doesn't work now they've failed.

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:02.979
And this gets used a lot in mental health.

00:55:03.000 --> 00:55:05.580
That person failed this
therapy, failed that medicine.

00:55:06.779 --> 00:55:11.299
I think it's so also ironic because
that's often what the person internalizes.

00:55:11.580 --> 00:55:18.820
Well, they failed because we set
them up 100% for failure, right?

00:55:18.850 --> 00:55:23.510
Because we took things that have their
role, at least potentially have their

00:55:23.510 --> 00:55:30.130
role, and we built the whole story around
them because that story is convenient for

00:55:30.139 --> 00:55:32.720
the systems that are providing the care.

00:55:33.050 --> 00:55:37.010
It's convenient for the healthcare
systems, it's convenient for the insurers.

00:55:37.860 --> 00:55:39.220
CBT packages very nicely.

00:55:39.460 --> 00:55:43.279
And you could see how if you start
changing thoughts and how they

00:55:43.280 --> 00:55:47.309
make you feel like you can get some
movement on the surface, even if

00:55:47.309 --> 00:55:48.630
there's no movement underneath.

00:55:49.130 --> 00:55:53.560
And again, I'm not saying CBT is bad,
but to see it as the whole answer

00:55:54.140 --> 00:55:56.880
guarantees failure in so many situations.

00:55:57.099 --> 00:55:58.310
Same thing with the medicine.

00:55:58.310 --> 00:56:00.650
If you build the whole story
just because it's convenient.

00:56:01.170 --> 00:56:04.470
And by and large, medicines
are cheaper than people, right?

00:56:04.480 --> 00:56:06.750
So you can prescribe
medicines very reflexively.

00:56:07.220 --> 00:56:11.490
Psychiatrists with 15 minutes with
a patient that they can't then

00:56:11.500 --> 00:56:13.200
see back for a couple of months.

00:56:13.200 --> 00:56:15.220
Like, how does that go well?

00:56:15.520 --> 00:56:19.159
The answer is it only goes well
the way a broken clock is right a

00:56:19.160 --> 00:56:21.860
couple of times, twice a day, right?

00:56:21.860 --> 00:56:24.800
I mean, look, sometimes it goes well
where it just somehow it works out.

00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:28.089
And that person can do a little bit of
therapy in 15 minutes and choose the right

00:56:28.090 --> 00:56:32.480
medicines, but by and large, we do those
things because they're convenient for the

00:56:32.480 --> 00:56:38.149
systems, even though that's why people
don't get better like we think they would.

00:56:38.150 --> 00:56:39.450
That's why they stay in systems.

00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:41.499
That's why they come in
and out of emergency rooms.

00:56:41.709 --> 00:56:44.950
That's why they're not able to
stop the drugs that end up only

00:56:44.970 --> 00:56:46.760
being stopped when the person dies.

00:56:47.190 --> 00:56:52.390
This happens all the time, and we
don't stop it because we're coming

00:56:52.390 --> 00:56:55.649
from a perspective that is so limited.

00:56:55.710 --> 00:57:00.469
That's not saying, let's take a step back
and look, can we really help someone?

00:57:00.480 --> 00:57:02.380
Can we really help that person understand?

00:57:02.380 --> 00:57:04.069
Can we help that person make change?

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:08.819
Which ultimately would be, of course,
so much better for the person and

00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:12.940
so much better for society, but
is also better if we just look at,

00:57:12.940 --> 00:57:14.600
bottom line, dollars and cents.

00:57:15.130 --> 00:57:18.660
Because the short term view of
it is cheaper today to have a

00:57:18.660 --> 00:57:21.239
psychiatrist at a 15 minutes
appointment reflexively prescribe

00:57:21.239 --> 00:57:23.359
a medicine that is cheaper today.

00:57:23.710 --> 00:57:27.879
Is that cheaper across time when that
person is utilizing more resources or

00:57:27.880 --> 00:57:29.370
they're in and out of emergency rooms?

00:57:29.630 --> 00:57:35.049
It's so short sighted, which fits with
many ways in how our society works, right.

00:57:35.049 --> 00:57:37.839
That we want gratification and
we want gratification rapidly.

00:57:38.140 --> 00:57:40.650
That's why a person would accept
that their problems could be

00:57:41.000 --> 00:57:42.140
changed by medicine, right?

00:57:42.140 --> 00:57:43.660
We're kind of conditioned that way.

00:57:43.730 --> 00:57:47.240
Andrew Huberman: Well, of course there's
the cost we don't see, which is that

00:57:47.240 --> 00:57:52.640
person doesn't get the opportunity to
express their generative drive, and the

00:57:52.650 --> 00:57:54.440
consequence of that is incalculable.

00:57:54.460 --> 00:57:54.940
Paul Conti: Right?

00:57:55.150 --> 00:57:55.710
Yes.

00:57:55.710 --> 00:58:01.170
And if we take a step back and we look
at that, I think that what we will see is

00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:05.064
that we have, it's not quite like painted
ourselves into a corner, but it's like the

00:58:05.064 --> 00:58:10.049
idea that if there's a beautiful tapestry
that's the size of the wall, that you

00:58:10.049 --> 00:58:12.139
can see that only standing back from it.

00:58:12.459 --> 00:58:15.590
I mean, this goes back, I think,
a couple of thousand years,

00:58:15.700 --> 00:58:16.990
this sort of thought and idea.

00:58:16.990 --> 00:58:21.190
But if you come up too close to it, then
you can't see what it means anymore.

00:58:21.410 --> 00:58:24.799
And we're up so close to it
that we're thinking, well, okay,

00:58:24.809 --> 00:58:26.340
how could one parameter change?

00:58:26.340 --> 00:58:30.340
And can this person get a 15 minutes
visit sooner rather than later?

00:58:30.340 --> 00:58:31.900
Or how about this
medicine instead of that?

00:58:31.900 --> 00:58:35.840
And then it's like our noses are right up
against the tapestry and we don't see that

00:58:36.380 --> 00:58:42.839
we're not doing right by individual people
a lot of the time, and we're not doing

00:58:42.840 --> 00:58:47.029
right by society, which then, if you stop
and think about it, we're not doing right

00:58:47.029 --> 00:58:53.290
by us because any one of us could be in
that position, and many of us have been in

00:58:53.290 --> 00:58:57.270
that position being on the other side of
things and really needing help and needing

00:58:57.270 --> 00:58:59.880
to understand so any of us can be there.

00:58:59.880 --> 00:59:04.770
So if we're failing a lot of individual
people and we're failing the society, it

00:59:04.770 --> 00:59:09.740
doesn't matter who we are listening to
this, ultimately we're failing ourselves.

