01:00:31.219 --> 01:00:37.130
Lets therefore talk about what does
know, and again, placing on the shelf

01:00:37.450 --> 01:00:41.000
the fact that medications can help
and CBT cognitive behavioral therapy

01:00:41.000 --> 01:00:45.359
can help, but they are just but two
components of a much larger picture.

01:00:47.639 --> 01:00:51.430
The map that we described briefly
at the beginning of today's episode,

01:00:51.430 --> 01:00:55.080
and that is, by the way, available as
a downloadable PDF in the show note

01:00:55.110 --> 01:00:58.590
captions if people want to look at it
visually and that was described in a

01:00:58.590 --> 01:01:02.310
lot of detail in episode one, which I
hope people will take the time to listen

01:01:02.310 --> 01:01:06.730
to because it's so rich with depth of
understanding and I'm certain everyone

01:01:06.730 --> 01:01:10.659
will learn a ton about themselves and
others simply by listening to your

01:01:10.660 --> 01:01:12.879
words, I'm absolutely certain of that.

01:01:14.490 --> 01:01:18.990
That map provides essentially
a description of the bins, the

01:01:18.990 --> 01:01:23.710
cupboards to look in to arrive at
better answers and even the sorts

01:01:23.710 --> 01:01:25.470
of questions that one might ask.

01:01:26.950 --> 01:01:30.609
If we could just talk about that in
the context of the example that you

01:01:30.610 --> 01:01:34.930
gave of this person who made this
really incredible choice to move

01:01:34.930 --> 01:01:36.510
away from this higher paying job.

01:01:37.630 --> 01:01:41.570
They were overindulging in
certain maladaptive behaviors.

01:01:41.860 --> 01:01:43.990
And again, we will use this example.

01:01:43.990 --> 01:01:49.010
But this example is but one of an
infinite number of examples that we could

01:01:49.010 --> 01:01:51.390
use of a person who's in a struggle.

01:01:52.090 --> 01:01:54.450
They're doing something that's
not working for them, and they're

01:01:54.500 --> 01:01:57.790
also not doing things that they
know they ought to be doing.

01:01:57.929 --> 01:02:02.729
Okay, this is important for people
to understand, because there are

01:02:02.729 --> 01:02:04.549
going to be people out there that
are thinking, oh, like this poor

01:02:04.549 --> 01:02:05.979
guy, he's making tons of money.

01:02:06.000 --> 01:02:06.580
Poor him.

01:02:06.890 --> 01:02:10.510
But he was experiencing deep
misery, lack of satisfaction.

01:02:10.720 --> 01:02:12.370
So it could have been the reverse example.

01:02:12.380 --> 01:02:15.040
Like, the person isn't in a
job that brings about enough

01:02:15.040 --> 01:02:16.820
wealth for them to thrive.

01:02:16.860 --> 01:02:17.080
Right.

01:02:17.080 --> 01:02:19.150
Because there are financial
realities to life.

01:02:20.530 --> 01:02:21.670
Paul Conti: It's just one example , right.

01:02:21.710 --> 01:02:21.960
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:02:21.960 --> 01:02:24.660
Paul Conti: But it's a good one, I
think, because the person left the money.

01:02:24.940 --> 01:02:25.300
Right.

01:02:25.300 --> 01:02:27.390
So it's like, well, what
would make you leave that?

01:02:27.420 --> 01:02:27.610
Right.

01:02:28.240 --> 01:02:30.779
Well, what would make you leave
that is if you're miserable in the

01:02:30.810 --> 01:02:34.130
situation with that, and you're
happy in the situation without it.

01:02:34.150 --> 01:02:34.370
Andrew Huberman: Right.

01:02:34.370 --> 01:02:37.300
So it's about leaving misery
and finding happiness.

01:02:38.000 --> 01:02:42.839
So if you'd be willing to share with us
a little bit of your mindset during those

01:02:42.840 --> 01:02:47.240
sessions, meaning the sorts of questions
you asked him about the structure of his

01:02:47.240 --> 01:02:50.720
self or to reveal the structure of his
self and the function of his self that

01:02:50.720 --> 01:02:55.430
allowed the both of you to eventually
set him down this far better course.

01:02:56.210 --> 01:02:58.983
What's better than moving away from
frustration and overindulgence and

01:02:59.119 --> 01:03:03.720
maladaptive behavior to deep satisfaction,
peace, contentment and delight, and

01:03:03.720 --> 01:03:05.170
to become a generative human being.

01:03:05.780 --> 01:03:06.060
Paul Conti: Right.

01:03:06.180 --> 01:03:08.420
So we can look in each
of those ten cabinets.

01:03:08.470 --> 01:03:08.759
Right?

01:03:08.929 --> 01:03:11.559
So let's say we look in the
unconscious mind cabinet.

01:03:11.800 --> 01:03:12.819
There's not much there.

01:03:13.380 --> 01:03:17.060
When the person was growing up,
it was very clear that having more

01:03:17.060 --> 01:03:20.500
money and having a job that impressed
people was an important thing.

01:03:20.630 --> 01:03:23.970
He internalizes some of it, so
some of it's unconscious, but

01:03:23.970 --> 01:03:25.149
by and large, he's aware of it.

01:03:25.350 --> 01:03:27.289
Andrew Huberman: And then
that was revealed to you how?

01:03:27.929 --> 01:03:31.740
You would ask him a question about,
tell me about your upbringing.

01:03:32.059 --> 01:03:34.999
And he would say, yeah, money
was important to my family but I

01:03:35.000 --> 01:03:36.740
always felt like we had enough.

01:03:36.949 --> 01:03:38.730
He wasn't super wealthy,
but it had enough.

01:03:39.590 --> 01:03:43.180
When you say there wasn't a lot there, do
you mean that there was no kind of like,

01:03:43.400 --> 01:03:45.900
X marks the spot or blinking red light?

01:03:45.920 --> 01:03:49.500
Like, whoa, there's something really in
his unconscious mind that's in his way.

01:03:49.740 --> 01:03:50.520
Do I have that right?

01:03:50.880 --> 01:03:52.609
Paul Conti: Well, more
because it was conscious.

01:03:53.319 --> 01:03:56.440
So he was aware that it was
very much like beat into him.

01:03:56.930 --> 01:03:57.190
Right?

01:03:57.200 --> 01:04:01.410
Like, this is the only way to be okay
is to have a prestigious job that makes

01:04:01.410 --> 01:04:03.680
a lot of money but he's aware of it.

01:04:04.020 --> 01:04:06.490
If he weren't aware of it, we
have to bring that to light.

01:04:06.520 --> 01:04:06.759
Right?

01:04:06.929 --> 01:04:07.367
But he was aware.

01:04:07.367 --> 01:04:09.529
He's like, look, it
has a big impact on me.

01:04:09.540 --> 01:04:10.700
It makes it hard to step away.

01:04:10.700 --> 01:04:14.210
Like, I know I don't really care that much
about the money, but I also kind of do.

01:04:14.600 --> 01:04:18.140
Andrew Huberman: Sure, I always say money
can't buy happiness, but it certainly

01:04:18.140 --> 01:04:19.880
can buffer certain stressors in life.

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:24.110
Sometimes you hear people who have
a lot of money saying, like, money

01:04:24.110 --> 01:04:26.930
can't buy happiness because there
are a lot of miserable rich people.

01:04:26.930 --> 01:04:32.210
But it's very different to have two night
nurses to take care of a baby than to be

01:04:32.210 --> 01:04:35.110
the person who has to stay up all night
taking care of a kid, especially, or a

01:04:35.110 --> 01:04:39.310
single mother versus a mother that has
a partner who's willing to pitch in.

01:04:40.740 --> 01:04:41.840
You just can't compare

01:04:42.540 --> 01:04:44.050
Paul Conti: That's absolutely true.

01:04:44.270 --> 01:04:48.270
In this case, we're just looking at
money as money as an endpoint, right?

01:04:48.270 --> 01:04:53.950
The idea that no matter what, how
secure and safe is more money better?

01:04:54.470 --> 01:04:57.040
And he had an intrinsic overvalue of that.

01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:01.819
So it made it harder to step away
from it because he was overvaluing it.

01:05:01.820 --> 01:05:04.507
He knew he was overvaluing
it just in and of itself.

01:05:04.680 --> 01:05:06.389
Not even for what it gets you, right?

01:05:06.550 --> 01:05:08.660
But for the psychological meaning of it.

01:05:09.370 --> 01:05:11.510
Then we look at his defensive structures.

01:05:11.510 --> 01:05:15.200
If we look in that cupboard, you see
that they've really shifted, right?

01:05:15.200 --> 01:05:16.779
They shifted from healthy places.

01:05:16.780 --> 01:05:18.819
Now they're sort of twisted and distorted.

01:05:19.059 --> 01:05:24.439
And he's doing a lot of denial, a lot
of avoidance, a lot of rationalization.

01:05:25.140 --> 01:05:28.090
He's enacting a lot of
aggression towards himself.

01:05:28.090 --> 01:05:30.759
And he's doing a lot of projecting.

01:05:31.100 --> 01:05:33.339
He's harming himself with the alcohol.

01:05:33.340 --> 01:05:34.470
He's punishing himself.

01:05:34.830 --> 01:05:37.540
So his defensive structure,
it can be healthy.

01:05:38.000 --> 01:05:40.940
We know that because it
was healthier, right?

01:05:40.940 --> 01:05:43.090
But then we see that it is so twisted.

01:05:43.090 --> 01:05:44.560
So we learn a lot from that, right.

01:05:44.700 --> 01:05:48.040
A lot is conscious in this person,
the defensive structure, can be

01:05:48.040 --> 01:05:49.529
healthy because it was healthy...

01:05:50.110 --> 01:05:51.730
Andrew Huberman:
Eventually it was healthy.

01:05:51.929 --> 01:05:53.200
Paul Conti: Well, it was healthy before.

01:05:53.740 --> 01:05:54.109
Andrew Huberman: I see.

01:05:54.109 --> 01:05:55.319
Paul Conti: It was healthy before.

01:05:55.320 --> 01:05:58.060
So you know that it can be healthy again.

01:05:58.379 --> 01:06:00.320
He has it in him to have healthy defenses.

01:06:00.320 --> 01:06:04.560
They just started getting away from him
as he felt less and less satisfied with

01:06:04.560 --> 01:06:07.770
his job and more and more angry with
himself and more and more miserable.

01:06:08.950 --> 01:06:10.560
Andrew Huberman: This is a
really key point for me and

01:06:10.560 --> 01:06:11.670
everyone else to understand.

01:06:12.590 --> 01:06:16.739
Throughout the years of high school and
college and friends and things of that

01:06:16.740 --> 01:06:23.580
sort, I would hear this like, "I used to
be really good at fitness, or if I had

01:06:23.580 --> 01:06:26.420
a dollar for every time someone said,
you should have seen me in high school".

01:06:27.460 --> 01:06:31.879
Like, the person who lets themselves
go and arguably is very busy with

01:06:32.200 --> 01:06:35.050
professional duties and family
duties, and you can understand why

01:06:35.050 --> 01:06:38.380
their time is more compressed than
it was when they were in high school.

01:06:38.380 --> 01:06:42.260
But nonetheless, you hear these
sorts of things all the time.

01:06:42.700 --> 01:06:46.510
I used to have this sense of I could
do things or that things could work

01:06:46.510 --> 01:06:51.100
out, and then it's as if there was a
previous version of themselves that

01:06:51.100 --> 01:06:55.419
is completely atrophied and the new
version of themselves or the later

01:06:55.420 --> 01:07:00.680
version of themselves rather just simply
doesn't have access to that anymore.

01:07:01.500 --> 01:07:02.260
Paul Conti: That's the impact of trauma.

01:07:03.800 --> 01:07:07.770
Whether it's big trauma or it's
a big event or it's multiple

01:07:07.790 --> 01:07:10.819
things, like, oh, the world just
isn't rewarding me, I'm trying.

01:07:11.399 --> 01:07:11.571
The world's not rewarding me.

01:07:11.571 --> 01:07:11.989
I'm trying.

01:07:11.990 --> 01:07:12.940
The world's not rewarding me.

01:07:13.120 --> 01:07:15.680
Then people become
dispirited, demoralized.

01:07:17.170 --> 01:07:21.790
It's the trauma of that that takes away
the sense of self, the sense of agency.

01:07:21.790 --> 01:07:25.449
Like, I thought I could do things before,
now I don't think I can do things, right.

01:07:25.450 --> 01:07:27.460
But nothing has really changed in me.

01:07:28.180 --> 01:07:30.770
That's a problem and it's a problem.

01:07:30.770 --> 01:07:33.799
The vast majority of
times it's born of trauma.

01:07:33.960 --> 01:07:37.120
Andrew Huberman: Does that necessarily
mean early childhood trauma?

01:07:37.190 --> 01:07:40.070
Or I suppose it could
be later life trauma.

01:07:40.290 --> 01:07:44.430
I mean, one of the things that I like
about what you're saying so much is that

01:07:44.929 --> 01:07:52.339
you, the psychiatrist, hears, I used to
be able to do something well or feel well.

01:07:52.860 --> 01:07:55.840
And that's like, it
sounds like is a signal.

01:07:55.840 --> 01:07:59.359
It's really a beacon of health that
still exists in the person, but

01:07:59.360 --> 01:08:00.410
that they're out of touch with.

01:08:00.700 --> 01:08:05.460
I think for most people, when they think
about themselves or people who talk

01:08:05.469 --> 01:08:09.340
about how they used to be functional
in some domain, and they're no longer

01:08:09.340 --> 01:08:10.960
functional in that domain any longer.

01:08:11.160 --> 01:08:14.080
It sounds as if things
are fundamentally broken.

01:08:14.830 --> 01:08:18.189
It's as if a piece of them
that was functioning drifted

01:08:18.189 --> 01:08:19.519
out of their body and left.

01:08:20.130 --> 01:08:23.200
But I love the optimism, because
I think so much of what we're

01:08:23.200 --> 01:08:27.019
interested in covering today is not
just what's not working and why,

01:08:27.330 --> 01:08:29.200
but also what's working and why.

01:08:29.210 --> 01:08:30.990
And what used to work and why.

01:08:31.310 --> 01:08:36.940
And the idea that within these cupboards
there can be the discovery of problems.

01:08:37.759 --> 01:08:41.450
Clearly, that's why one goes to the
cupboards as we're defining it, but

01:08:41.450 --> 01:08:43.380
that there are a lot of answers.

01:08:44.359 --> 01:08:47.950
The ingredients for success
already exist within us.

01:08:48.960 --> 01:08:52.050
Paul Conti: Especially if we know
we've had that ability before, right?

01:08:52.050 --> 01:08:53.252
Because we know that we had it before.

01:08:53.252 --> 01:08:54.639
So think about in this man.

01:08:55.080 --> 01:08:58.289
He felt that he couldn't make change.

01:08:58.289 --> 01:08:59.210
Like now he's stuck.

01:08:59.219 --> 01:09:01.850
I got a lot of things done.

01:09:01.850 --> 01:09:06.370
I was able to get myself into this school
and achieve this and then get this job.

01:09:06.529 --> 01:09:07.929
He could do all of those things.

01:09:07.930 --> 01:09:12.050
But now he feels like he can't do
anything to make himself happy.

01:09:12.050 --> 01:09:13.429
So we know he could do that.

01:09:13.429 --> 01:09:15.009
He had a strong sense of agency.

01:09:15.339 --> 01:09:16.819
He does it now, right?

01:09:17.430 --> 01:09:20.630
And like people often do,
they feel a sense of loss.

01:09:20.630 --> 01:09:22.790
Like, naturally, I've had
this happen in myself.

01:09:22.930 --> 01:09:25.890
It feels like something's cut out
of you and there's something hollow.

01:09:25.920 --> 01:09:28.210
I had that thing, and now I don't.

01:09:28.840 --> 01:09:30.580
Hence the I'm broken, I'm hopeless.

01:09:30.580 --> 01:09:32.890
The things that we hear over
and over and over again.

01:09:33.189 --> 01:09:36.889
So think about the shift in this
person to what's actually going on,

01:09:37.149 --> 01:09:38.620
which isn't that hard to discern.

01:09:38.630 --> 01:09:39.920
We just pay attention to it.

01:09:40.170 --> 01:09:43.889
So then if we run up the structure of
self, we say, okay, not a lot of it is

01:09:43.890 --> 01:09:46.279
rooted in the unconscious mind, right?

01:09:46.710 --> 01:09:51.189
There are problems of overvaluing certain
things, but they're in the conscious mind.

01:09:51.200 --> 01:09:51.920
Andrew Huberman: He knows.

01:09:51.929 --> 01:09:58.180
Like in his household, over dinner,
it was dad or mom being proud of some

01:09:58.180 --> 01:09:59.920
dollar amount that they had achieved.

01:10:00.170 --> 01:10:01.630
So that narrative exists.

01:10:01.630 --> 01:10:04.670
And he's like, yeah, money was a
big deal in my family kind of thing.

01:10:05.010 --> 01:10:06.429
By the way, I'm not
speaking about my family.

01:10:06.730 --> 01:10:08.330
Rarely were their discussions about money.

01:10:08.330 --> 01:10:09.819
There were discussions about
other things, of course.

01:10:10.100 --> 01:10:11.620
But in this hypothetical.Sorry.

01:10:11.639 --> 01:10:12.069
Paul Conti: He knows.

01:10:13.920 --> 01:10:16.090
He overvalues it, right?

01:10:16.090 --> 01:10:19.016
He knows that independent of what money
buys and what he needs and all that, he

01:10:19.140 --> 01:10:21.059
just puts too much importance in money.

01:10:21.099 --> 01:10:21.939
And he knows that.

01:10:22.090 --> 01:10:22.380
Right?

01:10:22.599 --> 01:10:24.910
So, okay, they're conscious mind issues.

01:10:25.130 --> 01:10:28.400
He's pretty aware of them, and
they're pretty kind of set in him.

01:10:28.400 --> 01:10:29.849
Like those are the
issues, and they're there.

01:10:30.170 --> 01:10:31.150
Okay, we learned that.

01:10:31.440 --> 01:10:33.370
Then we go look at his
defensive structure.

01:10:33.580 --> 01:10:35.680
Boy, that's very, very
helpful to talk about.

01:10:35.680 --> 01:10:37.740
Wow, you had a very healthy
defensive structure.

01:10:37.740 --> 01:10:38.649
What were you doing before?

01:10:38.650 --> 01:10:39.890
A lot of sublimation.

01:10:40.200 --> 01:10:40.480
Right?

01:10:40.660 --> 01:10:41.769
Andrew Huberman: Could
you explain sublimation?

01:10:41.770 --> 01:10:41.950
Paul Conti: Yeah.

01:10:41.950 --> 01:10:45.555
Take anxiety or tension or something
negative in the self, or that

01:10:45.610 --> 01:10:48.330
could be negative, and you channel
it towards something positive.

01:10:48.699 --> 01:10:50.580
He channeled that energy towards learning.

01:10:50.800 --> 01:10:53.500
He channeled some of the
aggressive drive into a sense

01:10:53.500 --> 01:10:55.360
of agency that got achievement.

01:10:55.360 --> 01:11:01.759
So he looked and he said, right, that
network of defense mechanisms that

01:11:01.790 --> 01:11:05.180
comes up out of the unconscious mind
was like looking pretty good, right.

01:11:05.180 --> 01:11:06.080
It was pretty clear.

01:11:06.080 --> 01:11:09.889
Light was coming through it in
a way that wasn't distorted.

01:11:10.100 --> 01:11:14.510
And now we could look at, wow, things
are pretty different now, right?

01:11:14.510 --> 01:11:17.670
As he's saying, no, it's
okay what do you mean?

01:11:17.830 --> 01:11:21.310
I spent 10 hours of my
weekend utterly wasting time.

01:11:21.310 --> 01:11:22.410
And what's wrong with that.

01:11:23.010 --> 01:11:28.250
Or he's rationalizing even that
he likes to drink when he doesn't

01:11:28.260 --> 01:11:30.120
because he's so mad at himself.

01:11:30.120 --> 01:11:33.140
Like the defensive
structure now is twisted.

01:11:33.150 --> 01:11:37.170
So we can say, okay, that's
a big observation, right?

01:11:37.389 --> 01:11:40.210
And then the character structure,
when we look at that, we find a

01:11:40.210 --> 01:11:44.790
person who's pretty good at figuring
out and understanding things and

01:11:44.790 --> 01:11:48.760
coming right up to the precipice of
change, but has a long history of

01:11:48.770 --> 01:11:50.970
then difficulty making the change.

01:11:51.650 --> 01:11:55.420
I know it, and I'm on the verge of
it, but I can't bring myself to do it.

01:11:55.610 --> 01:11:57.039
That's in his character structure.

01:11:57.259 --> 01:12:00.209
Andrew Huberman: By the
way, s uch a common thing.

01:12:00.690 --> 01:12:04.340
I mean, people that know
better know they know better.

01:12:04.870 --> 01:12:07.520
Sometimes you almost have to
wonder whether or not it's like

01:12:08.870 --> 01:12:10.290
a medication in the pocket.

01:12:10.290 --> 01:12:12.109
Like, they could take
it if they wanted to.

01:12:12.400 --> 01:12:13.990
That might even give them some comfort.

01:12:14.340 --> 01:12:15.660
But they just don't do it.

01:12:16.109 --> 01:12:18.939
They just don't engage in the
proper actions to move their

01:12:18.940 --> 01:12:20.240
life from one place to the next.

01:12:20.969 --> 01:12:22.920
Paul Conti: And if we look
then at the level of strivings,

01:12:23.310 --> 01:12:24.490
he does know what he wants.

01:12:24.820 --> 01:12:26.150
He wants a feeling of contentment.

01:12:26.150 --> 01:12:29.330
It's really what he wanted was a
feeling of contentment, a feeling of

01:12:29.340 --> 01:12:31.970
like, I'm taking good care of myself.

01:12:31.970 --> 01:12:33.670
I'm doing something that's of value.

01:12:33.719 --> 01:12:34.889
I'm enjoying doing it.

01:12:35.070 --> 01:12:36.480
He wanted those things.

01:12:36.650 --> 01:12:41.269
And even when we talked more, he had
ideas of what jobs would do that in

01:12:41.270 --> 01:12:43.019
the beginning, he said he had no ideas.

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:47.370
What he really meant that he said to
me, but was also saying to himself,

01:12:47.370 --> 01:12:51.130
is, I have no ideas of jobs that would
meet these requirements for me that

01:12:51.130 --> 01:12:53.460
pay as much as the one I have, right?

01:12:54.370 --> 01:12:58.509
But within him, which we got to
where he knew that there were

01:12:58.510 --> 01:12:59.790
jobs that would make him happy.

01:12:59.790 --> 01:13:01.940
He just had to get over
that they were lower paying.

01:13:02.139 --> 01:13:04.020
So think of what we learn about that.

01:13:04.020 --> 01:13:05.699
There's nothing lost in this man.

01:13:05.700 --> 01:13:07.240
There's nothing cut out of him.

01:13:07.580 --> 01:13:09.360
He's not damaged, he's not hopeless.

01:13:09.570 --> 01:13:12.140
And now he can understand that.

01:13:12.140 --> 01:13:14.120
He understands himself
actually pretty well, right?

01:13:14.140 --> 01:13:17.640
And his conscious mind is
apprehending pretty well what's

01:13:17.640 --> 01:13:19.720
going on and where he wants to go.

01:13:20.020 --> 01:13:23.250
But, boy, as he hasn't taken good
care of himself, the defensive

01:13:23.250 --> 01:13:24.699
structure gets sort of warped.

01:13:24.699 --> 01:13:27.790
And then it makes it a lot
harder to take care of yourself.

01:13:27.790 --> 01:13:29.699
It starts making other problems in life.

01:13:29.949 --> 01:13:32.920
And he starts feeling lousy about himself.

01:13:32.920 --> 01:13:34.780
Like, maybe I can't do
much of anything right.

01:13:34.780 --> 01:13:34.900
Why?

01:13:34.900 --> 01:13:36.339
Because work isn't going as well.

01:13:36.340 --> 01:13:39.589
Because he's drinking too much
and role performance goes down.

01:13:40.000 --> 01:13:41.050
So we can see that.

01:13:41.050 --> 01:13:45.460
And then what's of most interest there
is that there's a character structure

01:13:45.460 --> 01:13:50.230
that can come right up to the precipice
but not pull the trigger, so to speak,

01:13:50.230 --> 01:13:52.240
on what the thing the person wants to do.

01:13:52.550 --> 01:13:55.880
Because now we start getting,
okay, an understanding of

01:13:55.880 --> 01:13:57.800
what's actually going on, right?

01:13:57.800 --> 01:14:01.320
And then if we look at function of self,
let's look in those cabinets too, right?

01:14:01.590 --> 01:14:03.259
To help him be more aware of.

01:14:03.339 --> 01:14:07.820
There's an I here which he was
pretty well aware of, but not enough.

01:14:07.820 --> 01:14:09.930
Like, there's a person here
I'm shepherding through 24

01:14:09.930 --> 01:14:11.049
hours in the day, right?

01:14:11.050 --> 01:14:14.049
Like, I am an I and I'm aware
of what's going on inside of me.

01:14:14.049 --> 01:14:15.870
And it can make me happy or
it can make me miserable.

01:14:15.990 --> 01:14:18.170
Let's be more aware of that.

01:14:18.240 --> 01:14:19.539
Andrew Huberman: How did
he go about doing that?

01:14:19.540 --> 01:14:25.110
Because I find this first step within
addressing the function of self

01:14:25.660 --> 01:14:30.059
awareness and really understanding
that there's an I, there's a me,

01:14:30.059 --> 01:14:31.860
and I'm moving myself through life.

01:14:31.860 --> 01:14:36.779
I find this to be so interesting and
on the one hand, kind of obvious.

01:14:36.799 --> 01:14:38.960
Like, okay, there's a
me, like, tangible thing.

01:14:38.960 --> 01:14:40.360
You look in the mirror, you see yourself.

01:14:40.680 --> 01:14:44.510
But at the same time, it's a
bit abstract, I think to me

01:14:44.510 --> 01:14:45.410
and to many people out there.

01:14:46.330 --> 01:14:50.760
How does one go about building up a
sense of self in a way that provides

01:14:50.760 --> 01:14:52.420
positive agency in the world?

01:14:52.929 --> 01:14:53.550
Is it to tell?

01:14:54.050 --> 01:14:55.259
We hear all the time
about these affirmations.

01:14:56.099 --> 01:14:57.679
And I'm sure there are people
who look at themselves in the

01:14:57.679 --> 01:14:59.129
mirror and say, you are enough.

01:14:59.150 --> 01:15:01.370
And I'm not making fun of these people.

01:15:01.370 --> 01:15:01.540
Right?

01:15:01.650 --> 01:15:06.460
I actually have my own internal
list that I tell myself on waking

01:15:06.460 --> 01:15:09.370
every morning, which has nothing
to do with positive affirmation.

01:15:09.380 --> 01:15:11.949
It's just actually defining the
different roles that I play.

01:15:12.120 --> 01:15:15.269
I don't know why this is useful to me,
but I find it incredibly useful to me.

01:15:16.250 --> 01:15:17.289
It reminds me who I am.

01:15:17.289 --> 01:15:21.430
It also reminds or reassures me
that I don't have any dementia yet.

01:15:23.930 --> 01:15:27.179
So we'll see going
forward, but hopefully not.

01:15:27.460 --> 01:15:28.190
But yeah.

01:15:28.190 --> 01:15:31.750
Let's talk about this line of
inquiry within the category of

01:15:31.750 --> 01:15:36.019
self awareness that people can do
regardless of whatever challenges

01:15:36.670 --> 01:15:38.210
they might be having or not having.

01:15:38.660 --> 01:15:41.380
What does that look like and what
do you think that accomplishes at

01:15:41.380 --> 01:15:44.849
the level of self understanding
and agency in the world?

01:15:45.340 --> 01:15:48.790
Paul Conti: So one way of looking
at that is, and it's not the words

01:15:48.790 --> 01:15:52.880
I would use, but what's pervading a
person and sort of setting the stage?

01:15:53.430 --> 01:15:55.820
Which you can discern by inquiry.

01:15:56.280 --> 01:15:59.360
For example, in this case, the person.

01:15:59.360 --> 01:16:03.700
So there's a person who would
really not think, this is okay.

01:16:04.240 --> 01:16:07.070
This person taking a job at
10% of the previous pay and

01:16:07.070 --> 01:16:08.860
the job has less prestige.

01:16:09.349 --> 01:16:13.510
Who's a person who would be very unhappy
about that and very faulting of that.

01:16:14.250 --> 01:16:17.369
And talk to this person, my
patient, through the lens of that,

01:16:17.440 --> 01:16:18.710
he should feel shame for that.

01:16:19.270 --> 01:16:20.600
That person's not alive.

01:16:22.030 --> 01:16:23.690
The person is not alive.

01:16:23.969 --> 01:16:26.660
So one way of looking at is
what master are you serving.

01:16:27.210 --> 01:16:29.290
And a lot of the givens.

01:16:29.459 --> 01:16:35.450
The automaticity in him was as if that
person was alive inside of him, really

01:16:36.270 --> 01:16:37.880
telling him how this wasn't okay.

01:16:37.880 --> 01:16:41.539
He was fighting that he wasn't aware that,
hey, that's some other person's voice.

01:16:41.540 --> 01:16:45.269
He's like, no, I'm very,
very conflicted about this.

01:16:45.790 --> 01:16:47.489
Actually, he wasn't very
conflicted about this.

01:16:47.489 --> 01:16:50.550
When he starts focusing on the
I, what do I actually think?

01:16:51.200 --> 01:16:51.940
What do I actually think?

01:16:52.820 --> 01:16:55.050
I don't care if I make 90% less.

01:16:55.060 --> 01:16:55.879
Like, I don't care.

01:16:55.880 --> 01:16:56.519
My needs are met.

01:16:56.520 --> 01:16:57.389
I put some money away.

01:16:57.410 --> 01:16:58.219
I want to be happy.

01:16:58.679 --> 01:16:59.490
I'm not conflicted.

01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:04.040
But in order to get there,
we have to look at the I.

01:17:04.049 --> 01:17:07.090
How much is the I at center stage?

01:17:07.110 --> 01:17:10.743
I don't mean in some way of paying
too much attention to the self, but

01:17:10.743 --> 01:17:13.590
we're all acting through the lens of
the I, no matter what we're choosing.

01:17:13.600 --> 01:17:18.309
So to be aware of that and do
I want to be impacted by the

01:17:18.309 --> 01:17:19.570
opinions of this other person?

01:17:19.580 --> 01:17:22.610
Because I can let someone
else's opinions very much.

01:17:22.610 --> 01:17:23.829
I mean, we all do, right?

01:17:24.180 --> 01:17:25.930
Very much impact my thoughts.

01:17:26.240 --> 01:17:30.110
But I want to kind of decide that, do
I really value that person's opinions?

01:17:30.110 --> 01:17:34.659
I don't want them automatically inside my
head telling me how I feel about myself.

01:17:35.080 --> 01:17:39.700
Andrew Huberman: I can't tell you how many
people I know come to me in a place of

01:17:39.710 --> 01:17:41.669
struggle, even though I'm not a clinician.

01:17:42.129 --> 01:17:47.400
And as I listen to what they're struggling
with, it's so clear that they know the

01:17:47.410 --> 01:17:52.909
best answer and route forward, but that
they're dealing with some internal,

01:17:52.940 --> 01:17:56.840
oppressive voice about whether or not
they are a good person or a bad person,

01:17:57.160 --> 01:18:00.720
whether or not the choice they want to
make is really a good choice at all.

01:18:00.720 --> 01:18:07.680
Sometimes those voices are the
voices of parents in these particular

01:18:07.680 --> 01:18:10.389
examples, or the voices of peers.

01:18:11.059 --> 01:18:14.830
And so I think, if I understand correctly,
what you're talking about is getting

01:18:14.830 --> 01:18:21.230
really firmly rooted in who a person
is for themselves and what they really

01:18:21.230 --> 01:18:26.120
value and what they really know to be
true for themselves and really trying to

01:18:26.120 --> 01:18:30.450
not necessarily quiet those voices, but
see those voices truly as other, even

01:18:30.450 --> 01:18:32.260
though they come from within their head.

01:18:32.260 --> 01:18:32.300
Is that right?

01:18:32.429 --> 01:18:32.699
Paul Conti: Yes.

01:18:34.959 --> 01:18:38.340
To stop and think, what
voices do I want inside?

01:18:38.460 --> 01:18:44.800
So maybe I want the voice of a
kind mentor who still held me to

01:18:44.849 --> 01:18:47.280
account for a very high standard.

01:18:48.110 --> 01:18:49.570
It's a good voice to have inside of me.

01:18:49.830 --> 01:18:50.499
Andrew Huberman: A few of those.

01:18:51.169 --> 01:18:53.799
Paul Conti: But what might not be
a good voice is like, say, a not

01:18:53.799 --> 01:18:57.150
so kind mentor for whom you could
never do anything good enough.

01:18:57.800 --> 01:18:58.990
That's not so good.

01:18:58.990 --> 01:19:01.019
Or maybe you take part of that
and you leave part of that.

01:19:01.290 --> 01:19:04.220
But the earlier and more formative
the voices are, the more they're

01:19:04.220 --> 01:19:05.480
in our head automatically.

01:19:05.480 --> 01:19:08.190
Like, think about that man thought
that he was deeply conflicted.

01:19:08.250 --> 01:19:10.379
Absolutely, 100%.

01:19:10.379 --> 01:19:12.179
And he was right.

01:19:12.180 --> 01:19:14.170
Like, his experience was
to be deeply conflicted.

01:19:14.340 --> 01:19:19.160
But when you go in and dig, if
you just dig and you get to like,

01:19:19.160 --> 01:19:23.140
okay, the I is going to assess
this, he's not conflicted at all.

01:19:24.230 --> 01:19:28.120
Which is why then if you're coming up
the function of self ladder and you look

01:19:28.120 --> 01:19:32.110
at defense mechanisms in action, and
what's on top of that salience right

01:19:32.370 --> 01:19:34.750
now is when it creates an immediacy.

01:19:34.760 --> 01:19:38.120
So defense mechanisms in action
sort of inform the process

01:19:38.120 --> 01:19:42.459
and say, hey, the defenses are
shifting to denial, acting out.

01:19:43.009 --> 01:19:45.509
And that's what sort of gives us
a time horizon, like this thing.

01:19:45.509 --> 01:19:46.839
This is not going to be okay.

01:19:47.549 --> 01:19:51.780
That if he kept down this path, what
was very clearly going to happen.

01:19:51.780 --> 01:19:54.429
You don't have to roll the tape
forward that much to see that

01:19:54.440 --> 01:19:55.580
he's going to lose his job.

01:19:56.170 --> 01:19:57.699
He's going to feel very ashamed of that.

01:19:57.700 --> 01:19:59.509
Like a bunch of negative
things are going to happen.

01:19:59.710 --> 01:20:02.799
So it helps the person apprehend
that there's something going on here.

01:20:02.940 --> 01:20:04.419
I'm changing, right.

01:20:04.420 --> 01:20:08.040
Because in some way I'm thinking now
that it's okay that I'm wasting 10

01:20:08.050 --> 01:20:11.540
hours on something that I could really
enjoy if I spent 90 minutes on it.

01:20:12.110 --> 01:20:14.940
Whoa, I'm kind of losing a
little bit perspective there.

01:20:15.179 --> 01:20:17.469
So it adds a sense like
it frames the situation.

01:20:17.469 --> 01:20:19.139
And the salience of it.

01:20:19.190 --> 01:20:21.739
Andrew Huberman: Could you elaborate
a little bit on this defense

01:20:21.740 --> 01:20:23.860
mechanism in action of acting out?

01:20:24.359 --> 01:20:27.460
I think we covered in episode
one, and I'm sure we'll come up

01:20:27.460 --> 01:20:31.599
several times more during today's
discussion about things like denial,

01:20:31.620 --> 01:20:34.360
projection, displacement, et cetera.

01:20:34.740 --> 01:20:39.169
Those defense mechanisms seem to
have their own intrinsic definition.

01:20:39.170 --> 01:20:42.950
But acting out is something that we
hear more and more about these days.

01:20:42.950 --> 01:20:44.070
Like, they're acting out.

01:20:45.870 --> 01:20:47.150
What is acting out?

01:20:48.780 --> 01:20:51.049
Is it acting out of some conflict?

01:20:51.480 --> 01:20:58.370
Is it trying to demolish a struggle
by going and doing something else?

01:20:59.170 --> 01:21:02.980
Paul Conti: We can think of it as, by
and large, an unhealthy manifestation

01:21:03.350 --> 01:21:08.290
of a lot of aggression, which could
be a very high aggressive drive or an

01:21:08.290 --> 01:21:12.420
aggressive drive that's not too high,
but is then furthered, its power is

01:21:12.420 --> 01:21:14.329
furthered by a negative situation.

01:21:14.809 --> 01:21:15.849
Say, like this one.

01:21:16.520 --> 01:21:19.639
Because the acting out, what
was going on here inside of this

01:21:19.640 --> 01:21:22.160
person is he was very, very mad.

01:21:23.130 --> 01:21:28.219
And this isn't a person who expressed
a lot of anger or had outlets for.

01:21:28.559 --> 01:21:32.349
He wasn't going and running 10
miles or this was all inside of him.

01:21:32.349 --> 01:21:34.180
He wasn't getting it out
in one way or another.

01:21:34.510 --> 01:21:37.750
So what he starts doing is he
starts acting out the anger.

01:21:37.750 --> 01:21:41.580
Now, he's angry at the world around
him because he's unhappy in it, and

01:21:41.580 --> 01:21:43.110
it's not giving him more choices.

01:21:43.120 --> 01:21:45.960
Now, of course, this is about
him and not the world around him.

01:21:46.710 --> 01:21:49.630
But he's feeling an anger towards
the world that won't cooperate.

01:21:49.650 --> 01:21:49.920
Right.

01:21:50.230 --> 01:21:51.879
And he's angry towards himself.

01:21:52.459 --> 01:21:54.320
Because he can't make himself happy.

01:21:54.320 --> 01:21:55.129
Look at all of this.

01:21:55.130 --> 01:21:57.450
Look at all that he did, and
look how miserable he is.

01:21:58.100 --> 01:22:01.620
So a way of acting out
then is the drinking.

01:22:02.110 --> 01:22:03.809
Because the drinking is to
hell with the world, right?

01:22:03.980 --> 01:22:06.780
You think I shouldn't be drinking at
night and coming to work hungover?

01:22:07.330 --> 01:22:08.360
I'll do it anyway, right?

01:22:08.360 --> 01:22:09.130
To hell with the world.

01:22:09.130 --> 01:22:10.809
It's a way of snubbing
his nose at the world.

01:22:11.289 --> 01:22:13.820
He's also snubbing his nose at him, right?

01:22:14.050 --> 01:22:15.430
To hell with me, right?

01:22:15.440 --> 01:22:19.310
The guy who now doesn't come across
the way he did before, because

01:22:19.310 --> 01:22:22.591
I'm showing up at work, not in the
responsible way I showed up before, but

01:22:22.591 --> 01:22:23.929
in a way that's a little disheveled.

01:22:23.940 --> 01:22:24.879
Function is lower.

01:22:25.139 --> 01:22:25.940
To hell with me.

01:22:25.940 --> 01:22:27.530
It's a form of self denigration.

01:22:27.880 --> 01:22:29.239
Like, let people think worse of me.

01:22:29.969 --> 01:22:30.159
Why?

01:22:30.160 --> 01:22:32.669
Because I'm so mad at myself that
I think it's justified, right?

01:22:32.910 --> 01:22:36.490
And then there's also the inviting
of, hey, if I really have an

01:22:36.490 --> 01:22:38.190
addiction problem here, I lose my job.

01:22:38.190 --> 01:22:39.760
It's like, fine, I
deserve that too, right?

01:22:40.440 --> 01:22:44.480
There's an acting out against the self
that if the person doesn't stop and look

01:22:44.480 --> 01:22:46.669
at that, that can become true, right?

01:22:46.669 --> 01:22:49.700
Because that person didn't really wasn't
built to say to hell with the world and

01:22:49.700 --> 01:22:52.940
with me, or to not even understand that,
what's the hell with the world mean?

01:22:52.960 --> 01:22:54.190
It also means to hell with me.

01:22:54.190 --> 01:22:55.490
And it's not good for the or me.

01:22:56.139 --> 01:22:58.655
But he was able to understand
that because we would look at,

01:22:58.660 --> 01:23:00.389
like, wow, what shifted in you?

01:23:00.389 --> 01:23:03.170
This is a person who did a lot of
sublimation before, who now all

01:23:03.170 --> 01:23:04.490
of that's going into acting out.

01:23:04.799 --> 01:23:07.589
So they're not taking negative energy
and doing something good with it.

01:23:08.340 --> 01:23:10.700
They're taking negative energy and
doing things that are bad with it.

01:23:10.990 --> 01:23:11.380
Why?

01:23:11.390 --> 01:23:12.770
Because there's too much.

01:23:12.770 --> 01:23:14.389
There's a lot of negative energy.

01:23:14.590 --> 01:23:16.889
It's overwhelming everything else.

01:23:16.900 --> 01:23:20.170
And then it's going down these
pathways where the unhealthy

01:23:20.170 --> 01:23:21.870
defenses are always beckoning us.

01:23:21.880 --> 01:23:23.259
Send the energy down here.

01:23:23.639 --> 01:23:27.559
It's easier to avoid than it is to
face something and figure it out.

01:23:28.180 --> 01:23:31.710
It's easier to just act out than it
is to hold what's inside of us and

01:23:31.730 --> 01:23:33.790
then think about why it's there.

01:23:33.790 --> 01:23:36.389
So the unhealthy defenses
are beckoning us.

01:23:36.590 --> 01:23:41.249
And for him to see you have had
a healthy defensive structure,

01:23:41.250 --> 01:23:42.600
like, you can be healthy again.

01:23:42.609 --> 01:23:44.100
You're not broken, right?

01:23:44.330 --> 01:23:49.760
But to also see the way these defenses
are going is bringing real risk to

01:23:50.620 --> 01:23:52.219
your ability to even be happier.

01:23:52.219 --> 01:23:56.100
You get further down the shame and
loss path, it can be hard, sometimes

01:23:56.100 --> 01:23:57.839
impossible for the person to get back.

01:23:58.249 --> 01:23:59.780
So it sets the stage.

01:23:59.820 --> 01:24:04.400
Like, this is very, very important,
what these defenses are, how they're

01:24:04.410 --> 01:24:07.710
being enacted, and for him to be
able to see that, like, oh, this

01:24:07.710 --> 01:24:10.400
could be healthy, but it's not now.

01:24:10.740 --> 01:24:10.940
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:24:10.940 --> 01:24:20.210
These slow, degrading forms of acting
out and self sabotage and sabotage of

01:24:20.210 --> 01:24:25.330
others, I think, are the particularly
dangerous ones because they're slow.

01:24:25.670 --> 01:24:29.179
And sometimes the change
is imperceptibly slow.

01:24:29.420 --> 01:24:32.719
And then one day somebody arrives
at a place where, as you said, they

01:24:32.839 --> 01:24:37.570
unfortunately can't get back, or it
requires going into residential treatment

01:24:37.570 --> 01:24:43.559
or things that really big departures
in order to get back into life.

01:24:43.560 --> 01:24:46.910
And I would never wish for
somebody to choose to act out

01:24:46.910 --> 01:24:48.410
by driving off a cliff instead.

01:24:48.420 --> 01:24:52.319
But there are other forms of acting
out that immediately wake people up.

01:24:52.959 --> 01:24:55.110
But it seems like people
don't often select those.

01:24:55.110 --> 01:24:58.570
They select these more subtle forms
of acting out where they don't get

01:24:58.570 --> 01:25:04.759
caught or no one's calling them out on
it because plenty of people have five

01:25:04.760 --> 01:25:06.489
or six drinks at happy hour, right?

01:25:06.610 --> 01:25:08.370
As opposed to 50, right?

01:25:08.870 --> 01:25:14.190
So it's slow self sabotage as opposed
to immediate self destruction.

01:25:15.130 --> 01:25:18.300
And again, we're talking about alcohol,
but we talk about food, video games,

01:25:18.300 --> 01:25:21.970
social media, arguing with spouse.

01:25:21.970 --> 01:25:25.410
I mean, all of these kinds of things that
build up over time to eventually deliver

01:25:25.410 --> 01:25:28.809
people to a place of real problems.

01:25:29.850 --> 01:25:33.280
I'm curious, for this particular
individual you worked with, sounds

01:25:33.280 --> 01:25:34.780
like that's not what happened.

01:25:34.780 --> 01:25:38.860
They started this process of self
inquiry around self awareness.

01:25:39.130 --> 01:25:44.910
And did you see that the salience
that is what they paid attention to

01:25:44.910 --> 01:25:48.960
internally and externally immediately
shifted and the defense mechanism

01:25:49.690 --> 01:25:52.009
of acting out immediately dissolved.

01:25:52.010 --> 01:25:54.750
I mean, what was the kind
of contour and time course.

01:25:55.410 --> 01:25:55.960
Paul Conti: There was less.

01:25:56.400 --> 01:26:00.019
If we're looking in the cabinets, there's
a lot in the defence mechanisms in action

01:26:00.020 --> 01:26:04.809
cabinet, there's not as much in the
salience cabinet because this is the major

01:26:04.809 --> 01:26:07.030
thing on his mind right above all else.

01:26:07.370 --> 01:26:10.210
And he was having intrusive thoughts
about it and his self talk was about it.

01:26:10.210 --> 01:26:14.700
But we kind of already knew that, just
like we knew it was in the conscious mind.

01:26:14.700 --> 01:26:16.080
So if you think, where's the money at?

01:26:16.800 --> 01:26:19.529
It's not as much in that realm
because he's aware of it.

01:26:19.549 --> 01:26:22.480
If he thought, oh, this
isn't bothering me very much.

01:26:22.480 --> 01:26:25.800
And then he said, well, all his internal
dialogue is about it, right then,

01:26:26.010 --> 01:26:28.090
okay, there's a lot to achieve there.

01:26:28.380 --> 01:26:31.110
But just as he brought a lot that
was unconscious into the conscious

01:26:31.110 --> 01:26:34.120
mind, was aware of it, it was salient.

01:26:34.160 --> 01:26:40.290
There's less to do there because
the things to understand and change

01:26:40.299 --> 01:26:42.850
are not residing so much there.

01:26:43.670 --> 01:26:46.900
Andrew Huberman: For people that are, no
doubt everyone is thinking about their

01:26:46.900 --> 01:26:52.299
own internal processes and where they
could ask better questions and arrive at

01:26:52.309 --> 01:26:54.210
better answers to help themselves along.

01:26:55.230 --> 01:26:59.130
Perhaps you could elaborate a
little bit more on this salience

01:26:59.559 --> 01:27:01.220
cupboard under function of self.

01:27:02.310 --> 01:27:04.970
To me, salience is what's most apparent.

01:27:05.050 --> 01:27:11.120
As you talked about yesterday and
again today, there's this internal

01:27:11.210 --> 01:27:15.120
narrative, like what's on my mind
often or what kind of jumps to mind.

01:27:15.120 --> 01:27:20.110
I've started doing this recently based
on our discussions here, and I've noticed

01:27:20.110 --> 01:27:22.559
that under different states of arousal.

01:27:22.620 --> 01:27:27.440
And here I'm talking specifically about
sleepiness versus alertness type arousal

01:27:28.490 --> 01:27:33.169
when I wake up in the morning or when
I'm tired in the evening, where my mind

01:27:33.170 --> 01:27:38.780
is at, where it defaults to and what I'm
paying attention to throughout the day

01:27:39.420 --> 01:27:45.070
is that just asking myself to notice,
and I've certainly noticed some patterns.

01:27:46.179 --> 01:27:51.100
For instance, I've noticed that anytime my
overall state is elevated, more alertness,

01:27:51.100 --> 01:27:55.230
or in the middle of exercise, my mind
goes to some not so pleasant thoughts.

01:27:56.560 --> 01:27:59.180
And it's interesting to me,
it's like, wow, this is strongly

01:27:59.180 --> 01:28:01.720
correlated with states of
internal arousal that are healthy.

01:28:01.730 --> 01:28:07.410
Exercise within a limited frame
or exercise in general, if done

01:28:07.410 --> 01:28:08.480
in a healthy way, is healthy.

01:28:09.459 --> 01:28:12.139
And when I'm sleepy, those
thoughts never come about.

01:28:12.740 --> 01:28:16.469
When I wake up in the morning,
certain thoughts tend to leap to mind.

01:28:16.480 --> 01:28:17.040
Other thoughts know.

01:28:17.040 --> 01:28:19.660
So sort of categorization of
different types of thoughts

01:28:19.660 --> 01:28:21.349
depending on my internal state.

01:28:21.360 --> 01:28:26.799
Is that the sort of line of inquiry that
you're suggesting or describing here?

01:28:26.820 --> 01:28:29.889
Paul Conti: Yeah, I think it's quite
half the picture, because half the

01:28:29.889 --> 01:28:33.849
picture would be what's going on in your
mind when your mind is sort of at rest.

01:28:34.380 --> 01:28:38.430
What then starts playing
itself right in your mind.

01:28:38.570 --> 01:28:42.489
The other side of it is what
comes to the fore when there's a

01:28:42.490 --> 01:28:44.079
lot of competition for attention.

01:28:44.549 --> 01:28:49.230
So I'm making this up, but the idea
that if he stubbed his toe really badly,

01:28:49.420 --> 01:28:50.889
he'd still be thinking about this.

01:28:51.879 --> 01:28:52.980
Because it's so much power.

01:28:52.980 --> 01:28:56.280
Now, again, maybe if, God forbid, he has a
badly broken bone, there's a lot of pain,

01:28:56.280 --> 01:28:57.250
like he's going to think of that first.

01:28:57.410 --> 01:29:03.620
But it takes a lot of other stimulus
to be more salient than this.

01:29:04.330 --> 01:29:06.590
So you can look at what's coming
in your mind when your mind is

01:29:06.590 --> 01:29:09.050
sort of free and open, that's
very, very important and relevant.

01:29:09.250 --> 01:29:12.309
And then what's winning out when
there's maybe a high arousal state and

01:29:12.480 --> 01:29:14.630
a lot of competition for attention.

01:29:15.610 --> 01:29:16.670
Andrew Huberman: That's very helpful.

01:29:17.520 --> 01:29:26.400
Again, I think that along with this self
awareness piece, the asking of oneself

01:29:26.830 --> 01:29:32.930
what is happening in my mind when I'm
in different states or throughout the

01:29:32.930 --> 01:29:39.320
day, and as you're describing now,
also including when there are other

01:29:39.679 --> 01:29:41.150
things available to think about.

01:29:42.209 --> 01:29:45.460
Does that include how often I'm
distracted by a particular thought?

01:29:45.810 --> 01:29:49.330
Like how many times throughout the
day my mind goes from the conversation

01:29:49.330 --> 01:29:50.700
I might be in to something else?

01:29:50.920 --> 01:29:51.240
Paul Conti: Yes.

01:29:52.969 --> 01:29:54.349
Does it hijack your attention?

01:29:55.579 --> 01:29:57.229
Is one way of putting that.

01:29:57.599 --> 01:30:02.050
Andrew Huberman: A lot of people mention
to me challenges with intrusive thoughts.

01:30:04.350 --> 01:30:06.500
What can be done about
those intrusive thoughts?

01:30:06.500 --> 01:30:09.710
Or is it simply a matter of paying
attention to the fact that they're

01:30:09.719 --> 01:30:14.379
there and then thinking about
the origins of those thoughts?

01:30:14.500 --> 01:30:14.680
Paul Conti: Right.

01:30:14.809 --> 01:30:15.449
Absolutely.

01:30:15.830 --> 01:30:19.730
One example, you could have intrusive
thoughts because there's trauma in

01:30:19.730 --> 01:30:22.420
your background, maybe very clear
trauma that you're not facing and

01:30:23.030 --> 01:30:25.620
addressing, and then you have intrusive
thoughts that say, I'm not safe.

01:30:25.900 --> 01:30:29.009
Okay, go look for what's
still in the unconscious mind.

01:30:29.010 --> 01:30:32.750
Or when it comes out a little bit, you
push back into the unconscious mind.

01:30:33.140 --> 01:30:36.640
That's a very different scenario
than, like, in this case.

01:30:36.970 --> 01:30:41.020
This man was having intrusive thoughts
about his job situation, his overall

01:30:41.020 --> 01:30:44.430
situation, and it made sense that
he was having those intrusive.

01:30:44.440 --> 01:30:49.480
They were markers of the acuity of it, of,
like, you have to do something about this

01:30:49.809 --> 01:30:51.640
or something very bad is going to happen.

01:30:51.640 --> 01:30:52.919
So the intrusive thoughts there.

01:30:52.919 --> 01:30:54.560
And this made sense, right.

01:30:54.850 --> 01:30:58.090
This is not going well, and your
mind is sort of forcing you to

01:30:58.090 --> 01:31:01.710
pay attention to this because time
really kind of is of the essence.

01:31:01.710 --> 01:31:03.480
You're at real risk now.

01:31:03.639 --> 01:31:07.900
So intrusive thoughts can be anything
from, as they often are, they can be

01:31:07.910 --> 01:31:10.850
markers of something that is traumatic,
something that's underneath the surface,

01:31:10.850 --> 01:31:14.360
something that is really bothering us,
that we've shoved down, that's making

01:31:14.370 --> 01:31:16.330
guilt, shame, distress, vulnerability.

01:31:16.630 --> 01:31:18.630
That's very often the case.

01:31:18.840 --> 01:31:21.910
But sometimes intrusive thoughts
are a marker of like, oh, right.

01:31:21.920 --> 01:31:23.340
That's the thing to pay attention to.

01:31:24.740 --> 01:31:29.869
Andrew Huberman: And once we identify the
intrusive thought, how do we eradicate it?

01:31:29.949 --> 01:31:32.389
I mean, how do we work with it?

01:31:33.780 --> 01:31:39.660
Talking about trauma now, of course, it
might map back to a childhood experience,

01:31:40.160 --> 01:31:46.689
some internal narrative, but is there
some roadmap for moving intrusive thoughts

01:31:46.789 --> 01:31:53.020
from a place of intrusive and disturbing
to simply there and kind of, meh.

01:31:53.090 --> 01:31:55.780
I mean, it'd be wonderful to hit
a delete switch, but obviously,

01:31:55.790 --> 01:31:57.019
we don't work like that.

01:31:57.150 --> 01:31:59.950
Paul Conti: Well, let's take a look,
if we could, at this example, right,

01:32:00.090 --> 01:32:03.570
which is a little bit different if we
run through this example of the person

01:32:03.570 --> 01:32:08.130
in the job, because then we should talk
about trauma driven intrusive thoughts,

01:32:08.130 --> 01:32:11.750
which is, I think, in many ways, the
biggest topic about intrusive thoughts.

01:32:12.040 --> 01:32:14.020
But think of this person here.

01:32:14.020 --> 01:32:17.700
If we go up from salience,
we look at behaviors, right?

01:32:17.700 --> 01:32:20.530
And behavior actually now is
very, very important, right.

01:32:20.530 --> 01:32:21.580
This person is drinking.

01:32:21.580 --> 01:32:23.439
They're still going to
that job they don't want.

01:32:23.449 --> 01:32:26.240
They haven't gone and interviewed
for the jobs they want.

01:32:26.740 --> 01:32:31.640
So we start looking at the behaviors
that are making problems, the changes in

01:32:31.640 --> 01:32:33.369
behaviors that could make things better.

01:32:33.860 --> 01:32:35.696
And then on top of that,
we arrive at strivings.

01:32:35.696 --> 01:32:39.309
And I think when I was talking about
structure of self, I think at least one

01:32:39.309 --> 01:32:45.420
time I misspoke and said striving instead
of self at the top of these pyramids, self

01:32:45.420 --> 01:32:47.550
and striving have a lot of overlap, right.

01:32:47.740 --> 01:32:54.010
Because if you're growing a healthy self
out of the sort of top of the structure

01:32:54.010 --> 01:32:58.110
of self pyramid, then that self is
going to be aware of strivings and it's

01:32:58.110 --> 01:32:59.700
going to be better able to enact them.

01:32:59.700 --> 01:33:04.212
So his sense of self was shaken
here, but he was aware of the

01:33:04.212 --> 01:33:05.480
strivings for a better life.

01:33:05.950 --> 01:33:07.060
So now let's see the roadmap.

01:33:07.820 --> 01:33:08.450
It's interesting, right?

01:33:08.450 --> 01:33:10.780
Because the roadmap is his roadmap.

01:33:11.639 --> 01:33:16.529
If we look in those ten cupboards,
we come up with a roadmap, and the

01:33:16.529 --> 01:33:19.809
roadmap doesn't have us spending very
much time in unconscious land, right.

01:33:20.020 --> 01:33:21.340
Because he doesn't really need that.

01:33:21.780 --> 01:33:25.810
If we look at what makes the
difference for him, what did we do?

01:33:26.580 --> 01:33:32.050
We really cultivated the self awareness,
the I that is making decisions for him.

01:33:32.410 --> 01:33:37.410
We looked at how his defensive structure
had changed and the things he didn't

01:33:37.410 --> 01:33:40.820
want to be there now and the good
things that were there before, and

01:33:40.820 --> 01:33:42.360
how could he get back some of that?

01:33:42.360 --> 01:33:45.719
How could he trend back towards
what was working before?

01:33:45.719 --> 01:33:49.020
So we start to really look at
that and then we go from there

01:33:49.020 --> 01:33:50.480
really to changing behaviors.

01:33:50.960 --> 01:33:54.019
Like it requires a behavioral
change, which is not to walk up to

01:33:54.020 --> 01:33:58.560
the precipice of doing this each
day, but to actually do it, right.

01:33:58.560 --> 01:34:01.130
Because it was very clear all the
vectors, so to speak, inside of

01:34:01.130 --> 01:34:02.559
him were pointing towards doing it.

01:34:02.780 --> 01:34:06.790
And that that was consistent with
the self being healthier, that garden

01:34:06.790 --> 01:34:10.550
growing on top of the structure and
the strivings then being realized.

01:34:10.809 --> 01:34:16.470
So for him, that was the roadmap and
the salience, it wasn't really part

01:34:16.470 --> 01:34:21.269
of it, because the intrusiveness, the
salience bias inside of him made sense.

01:34:21.610 --> 01:34:24.990
And then, of course, it went away
once he made the decisions, right.

01:34:24.990 --> 01:34:28.249
Because the intrusive thoughts of,
like, you have to figure this out.

01:34:28.260 --> 01:34:29.230
You have to figure this out.

01:34:29.230 --> 01:34:32.049
Weren't there anymore, along
with the intrusive thoughts of,

01:34:32.340 --> 01:34:33.480
you'll never figure this out.

01:34:33.870 --> 01:34:37.280
It goes away because he made the
change, but he made the change

01:34:37.280 --> 01:34:40.600
because we looked at self awareness
and we strengthened self awareness.

01:34:40.600 --> 01:34:44.409
We looked at defense mechanisms, how
they could be versus how they are.

01:34:44.650 --> 01:34:47.679
We looked at the behavioral
change, which was really necessary.

01:34:47.679 --> 01:34:51.830
And then also referencing a character
structure that has difficulty.

01:34:52.100 --> 01:34:54.440
Coming across the precipice.

01:34:54.650 --> 01:34:57.470
So we say, okay, that's a
baseline characteristic of him.

01:34:57.470 --> 01:34:58.500
We kind of understand that.

01:34:58.840 --> 01:35:01.159
But how do we help him
change the behaviors anyway?

01:35:01.790 --> 01:35:05.469
When he does that, the self is in
a better, happier, healthier place.

01:35:05.469 --> 01:35:06.769
The strivings are realized.

01:35:07.200 --> 01:35:10.700
This person stops drinking
in the way they were.

01:35:10.930 --> 01:35:14.110
They start doing the enjoyment
aspects of their life.

01:35:14.110 --> 01:35:16.290
They start doing them within
reasonable bounds again, they're

01:35:16.290 --> 01:35:17.440
taking care of themselves.

01:35:17.700 --> 01:35:22.750
Person's smiling and now think the
generative drive is much more fulfilled.

01:35:22.750 --> 01:35:26.360
What comes on top of those pillars, right.

01:35:26.380 --> 01:35:28.570
Is that person has a sense of humility.

01:35:29.030 --> 01:35:32.259
Enough humility to say, I'm
going to walk away from this job.

01:35:32.500 --> 01:35:35.299
It's okay that people in the
job will think I'm crazy.

01:35:35.300 --> 01:35:36.239
How could you leave that?

01:35:36.589 --> 01:35:38.469
And it triggers something
in me in some way.

01:35:38.469 --> 01:35:39.809
But it's okay.

01:35:42.400 --> 01:35:43.400
I'm not out there for that.

01:35:43.400 --> 01:35:46.050
I'm not out there for the big
thing that everybody is guiding.

01:35:46.080 --> 01:35:49.040
I can have the humility to go
to the job that I know makes a

01:35:49.040 --> 01:35:50.430
difference and feels good to me.

01:35:50.610 --> 01:35:52.469
He's empowered to make change.

01:35:52.550 --> 01:35:57.059
He's moving away from the disempowerment
of the alcohol and the avoidance.

01:35:57.589 --> 01:36:00.910
So there's empowerment and humility
and absolutely, if you talk to that

01:36:00.910 --> 01:36:05.380
person on the other side of it, like
shortly as he was enacting it, right.

01:36:05.420 --> 01:36:08.470
Getting just to the other side of
it, there was so much empowerment

01:36:08.470 --> 01:36:12.839
and so much humility, which were
then brought to bear through a sense

01:36:12.840 --> 01:36:14.730
of agency that made the changes.

01:36:14.740 --> 01:36:17.360
That changed the job, that stopped
drinking, that dealt with the people

01:36:17.360 --> 01:36:21.890
who thought negatively of it through a
sense of gratitude of, it's not awful

01:36:21.890 --> 01:36:23.640
that I'm going to go make less money.

01:36:23.640 --> 01:36:24.990
A lot of people said that to him.

01:36:25.000 --> 01:36:25.812
Like, how could you do it?

01:36:25.812 --> 01:36:26.620
It was so terrible.

01:36:26.950 --> 01:36:27.690
It's not terrible.

01:36:28.530 --> 01:36:29.229
I'm grateful.

01:36:29.230 --> 01:36:29.860
You know what I'm going to do?

01:36:29.860 --> 01:36:30.929
I'm going to go make an amount of money.

01:36:30.929 --> 01:36:32.050
That's all that I need.

01:36:32.849 --> 01:36:35.409
So it was like, that's what
helps a person do that thing.

01:36:35.430 --> 01:36:37.490
And that's actually true.

01:36:38.170 --> 01:36:39.960
That's what mattered to him.

01:36:39.960 --> 01:36:45.690
So an activated, an active, a verb,
sense of agency and gratitude, then

01:36:45.690 --> 01:36:49.559
leads him to the place where there
was at peace, contentment, delight.

01:36:49.570 --> 01:36:53.769
He was delighting in the job
that he chose, and his generative

01:36:54.000 --> 01:36:55.810
drive was in accord with it.

01:36:56.240 --> 01:36:59.150
Then eventually, we stopped at
some point, working together.

01:36:59.150 --> 01:37:00.509
He didn't need me anymore.

01:37:00.510 --> 01:37:02.599
He could always come back,
but we didn't need me anymore.

01:37:02.929 --> 01:37:03.420
Then you look at.

01:37:03.429 --> 01:37:04.780
How are those last sessions?

01:37:05.010 --> 01:37:09.290
A lot of the last sessions were
him in an excited way, telling

01:37:09.290 --> 01:37:11.040
me what he was doing, right.

01:37:11.070 --> 01:37:12.830
Like, oh, and then we
did this, and I did this.

01:37:12.840 --> 01:37:13.610
I figured this out.

01:37:14.050 --> 01:37:15.424
He was so happy about it.

01:37:15.424 --> 01:37:20.280
And you can see that man's generative
drive, which naturally is quite high

01:37:20.320 --> 01:37:21.800
in him, but was being squelched.

01:37:21.800 --> 01:37:23.799
That brings him out of balance.

01:37:24.050 --> 01:37:27.820
Now, the generative drive was in quite a
good place, and he had enough aggression

01:37:27.820 --> 01:37:32.090
or assertion to go and do that job and
to do that well, and even enough to

01:37:32.120 --> 01:37:35.600
counter anybody who would still kind of
rise up and say, that wasn't a good idea.

01:37:35.840 --> 01:37:37.040
He could counter all that.

01:37:37.040 --> 01:37:38.400
He was getting pleasure from it.

01:37:38.570 --> 01:37:40.750
He didn't need to seek pleasure.

01:37:40.750 --> 01:37:41.160
By what?

01:37:41.160 --> 01:37:41.980
Not even pleasure?

01:37:42.020 --> 01:37:43.640
Because alcohol was pleasurable?

01:37:43.940 --> 01:37:44.700
No, pleasure.

01:37:44.730 --> 01:37:47.700
Because harming himself and
saying, to hell with you, to the

01:37:47.700 --> 01:37:49.250
world, and to him was pleasurable.

01:37:49.250 --> 01:37:50.830
He's not getting pleasure that way.

01:37:50.830 --> 01:37:54.099
He's getting pleasure in healthier ways,
taking care of himself, doing the job

01:37:54.520 --> 01:37:56.280
he loves, doing his leisure activities.

01:37:56.280 --> 01:37:59.940
Like, the man comes into
balance, and then life is good.

01:37:59.990 --> 01:38:03.540
And when you say, yeah, okay, come back
in a couple of months, comes back in a

01:38:03.540 --> 01:38:04.950
couple of months, maybe in six months.

01:38:04.950 --> 01:38:06.450
Comes back one more time,
I don't see him again.

01:38:06.850 --> 01:38:07.460
That's great.

01:38:07.870 --> 01:38:09.059
He totally doesn't need me again.

01:38:09.060 --> 01:38:11.900
And I atrophied from his life.

01:38:12.110 --> 01:38:12.599
Great.

01:38:13.010 --> 01:38:15.173
That's the success state of it.

01:38:15.380 --> 01:38:19.359
Andrew Huberman: He eventually
arrived at being truly wealthy with

01:38:19.360 --> 01:38:23.900
all the components of mental health
and peace, contentment, delight.

01:38:25.000 --> 01:38:29.920
As you describe his story, which is
a remarkable one, it occurs to me

01:38:29.940 --> 01:38:36.189
that the narratives that we hear as
children end up being so powerful.

01:38:36.429 --> 01:38:36.769
Paul Conti: Yes.

01:38:37.080 --> 01:38:41.440
Andrew Huberman: And I'm sure there are
people out there that receive such direct

01:38:41.440 --> 01:38:43.940
messages from their mother and or father.

01:38:44.140 --> 01:38:45.460
Like, you have to do this.

01:38:45.460 --> 01:38:46.770
You cannot do that.

01:38:47.530 --> 01:38:52.450
But often we get messages through
observing and overhearing.

01:38:52.730 --> 01:38:53.089
Right.

01:38:53.910 --> 01:38:58.519
The way that our mother talks about
our father when he's out of the room.

01:38:59.100 --> 01:39:02.000
The way that our father talks about
our mother when she's out of the room.

01:39:02.000 --> 01:39:06.230
And some of this could be nonverbal,
like a rolling of the eyes or somebody

01:39:06.250 --> 01:39:09.822
saying, yes, yes, agreeing, and
then they walk out and they just.

01:39:10.229 --> 01:39:11.650
And kind of blowing them off.

01:39:12.139 --> 01:39:18.369
I mean, kids are, we are all so aware
and integrating all of that all the time.

01:39:18.369 --> 01:39:22.670
And I do think those messages get
woven into us at a very deep level.

01:39:23.250 --> 01:39:23.650
Paul Conti: Absolutely.

01:39:24.120 --> 01:39:27.040
Andrew Huberman: And then, of course,
there are the conscious narratives

01:39:27.040 --> 01:39:30.620
that we build up as we go through
in particular, I think, elementary

01:39:30.620 --> 01:39:31.790
and middle school and high school.

01:39:31.820 --> 01:39:35.729
I mean, I can still remember a
negative comment somebody made about

01:39:35.730 --> 01:39:38.300
a jacket that I was wearing in,
like, the third or the fourth grade.

01:39:38.620 --> 01:39:40.340
I forget everything else
that happened that year.

01:39:41.060 --> 01:39:41.820
Remember that?

01:39:42.240 --> 01:39:42.610
Yeah.

01:39:42.610 --> 01:39:45.929
And I'm not insecure about
the clothing that I pick.

01:39:45.940 --> 01:39:48.769
I mean, obviously it's a
black button down shirt.

01:39:49.120 --> 01:39:50.759
I've had similar shirts
since the first grade.

01:39:50.759 --> 01:39:51.360
Just kidding.

01:39:52.119 --> 01:39:57.460
But the fact that that's embedded
in my memory systems is, like,

01:39:57.460 --> 01:40:03.330
just speaks to the salience of
negative, of insults basically.

01:40:03.340 --> 01:40:03.969
It was an insult.

01:40:04.510 --> 01:40:08.780
And I'm sure I've insulted plenty
of kids coming up as a teenager

01:40:08.780 --> 01:40:14.170
and back and forth, but these
narratives get so deeply embedded.

01:40:14.570 --> 01:40:18.860
And the idea that one could pick a
different path of vocation or, like, you

01:40:18.980 --> 01:40:24.330
miss the opportunity to be truly happy at
a deep level based on these narratives.

01:40:24.520 --> 01:40:26.180
I mean, on the one hand, it's obvious.

01:40:26.180 --> 01:40:31.019
On the other hand, you just go
like, whoa, this is not good.

01:40:31.030 --> 01:40:33.830
This is a flaw in the design.

01:40:35.830 --> 01:40:40.660
And yet you're giving us a roadmap to
understanding and to overcoming it.

01:40:40.730 --> 01:40:40.940
Paul Conti: Right?

01:40:41.280 --> 01:40:43.330
Let's say we take your
examples and we really look.

01:40:43.330 --> 01:40:45.279
They're great examples and
we look at them, right?

01:40:45.910 --> 01:40:48.550
The person making fun of
the coat in third grade.

01:40:49.040 --> 01:40:51.690
We're assuming it hasn't harmed you, it
hasn't changed the course of your life.

01:40:52.080 --> 01:40:52.839
What does it tell us?

01:40:52.850 --> 01:40:56.360
It shows that negative
stimuli are very salient.

01:40:56.750 --> 01:40:59.640
I'm sure you got a lot of compliments
in third grade too, right?

01:40:59.870 --> 01:41:02.360
But it's the negative that stands
out, which just shows that there's a

01:41:02.630 --> 01:41:05.270
salience bias in us towards the negative.

01:41:05.270 --> 01:41:07.720
And that's probably about
survival and threat sensing.

01:41:07.730 --> 01:41:12.790
Like, in some ways it makes sense
around human survival, but it doesn't

01:41:12.790 --> 01:41:14.580
make sense around human trauma.

01:41:15.930 --> 01:41:20.960
So you had given the example of what
gets communicated to the child when,

01:41:20.990 --> 01:41:24.449
say, mother says something negative about
father when father's out of the room.

01:41:24.450 --> 01:41:26.849
Father says something negative about
mother when mother's out of the room.

01:41:26.850 --> 01:41:27.760
Just to give an example.

01:41:28.340 --> 01:41:32.570
So, children, because the
complex cognitive mechanisms

01:41:32.580 --> 01:41:34.230
haven't been formed yet, right?

01:41:34.740 --> 01:41:39.169
Then the natural way that the brain
functions is in a self referential way.

01:41:39.919 --> 01:41:44.230
So the child generally doesn't
have the capacity to say, like, oh,

01:41:44.830 --> 01:41:49.190
mom and Dad aren't really getting
along well in this certain way.

01:41:49.370 --> 01:41:53.636
So when Dad's not here, mom vents a little
bit about something about him by saying.

01:41:53.636 --> 01:41:55.450
The child isn't thinking
about that, right?

01:41:55.690 --> 01:41:59.420
Then what the child will often
internalize is, okay, there's

01:41:59.420 --> 01:42:00.880
me and there's mom and dad.

01:42:00.890 --> 01:42:04.240
And mom says dad is bad,
and dad says mom is bad.

01:42:05.110 --> 01:42:07.440
And I must be bad too, right?

01:42:07.480 --> 01:42:11.049
Because in general, if your
parents are bad, then the

01:42:11.290 --> 01:42:12.580
child takes that on themselves.

01:42:12.580 --> 01:42:16.342
Now, again, I'm giving a simple example,
but I'm very much extrapolating it.

01:42:16.342 --> 01:42:18.730
I mean, imagine if that were very,
very aggressive where the mother,

01:42:18.750 --> 01:42:21.600
when this happens, just tells
the child how awful the father

01:42:21.600 --> 01:42:23.490
is and the father does the same.

01:42:23.490 --> 01:42:26.969
Someone's not going to come out the other
side of that being like, you know what?

01:42:27.120 --> 01:42:28.849
Maybe they're both awful, but I'm not.

01:42:28.929 --> 01:42:30.160
That's not how that goes.

01:42:30.570 --> 01:42:35.539
So the lessons, the traumatic lessons
of childhood get internalized.

01:42:36.139 --> 01:42:38.640
And they don't even always
have a solution state.

01:42:38.650 --> 01:42:42.430
So you think about the man who knew,
like, okay, you have to go get this job.

01:42:42.430 --> 01:42:43.890
And all those things he internalized.

01:42:43.890 --> 01:42:47.420
You might say, well, I mean,
he got to a good place for him.

01:42:48.580 --> 01:42:52.720
So for better or for worse, at
least there was a place to go.

01:42:52.730 --> 01:42:53.989
To go work hard, go succeed.

01:42:53.990 --> 01:42:57.030
Go check this box you've been
told you're supposed to check, but

01:42:57.070 --> 01:42:59.160
oftentimes there is no solution state.

01:42:59.170 --> 01:43:02.360
So how many children, I mean, it's
terrible that this is such a high

01:43:02.360 --> 01:43:07.410
percentage of the work adult practitioners
do is helping people who, as children

01:43:07.420 --> 01:43:13.050
were told one way or another that
they were worthless, incapable, bad.

01:43:13.840 --> 01:43:15.650
That gets put into the child.

01:43:16.100 --> 01:43:17.490
Unfortunately, far, far...

01:43:17.630 --> 01:43:20.480
I mean, one time on the
planet is too frequent, let

01:43:20.480 --> 01:43:22.559
alone how often this happens.

01:43:23.840 --> 01:43:26.079
Andrew Huberman: That example
makes really good sense.

01:43:26.609 --> 01:43:28.220
And this is a question.

01:43:28.250 --> 01:43:34.400
Could we add to that the example
whereby the child overhears examples

01:43:34.400 --> 01:43:39.549
of what say, men should be like or
women should be like these things.

01:43:39.559 --> 01:43:45.539
It's not so much like, you did wrong
Andrew or you did wrong Paul, or

01:43:45.540 --> 01:43:48.239
telling the daughter you screwed up.

01:43:48.250 --> 01:43:54.090
But it's more, again, narratives that
we overhear, or even a parent showing

01:43:54.650 --> 01:43:58.940
delight or excitement about a certain
phenotype in the world, like, oh, wow,

01:43:58.940 --> 01:44:00.550
look at that person, or look at them.

01:44:01.230 --> 01:44:02.750
Isn't she beautiful, right?

01:44:02.759 --> 01:44:05.380
That the young child thinks like,
okay, well, then that's the epitome of

01:44:05.380 --> 01:44:07.270
beauty through the lens of the parent.

01:44:07.580 --> 01:44:08.900
Or, gosh, like this person.

01:44:10.460 --> 01:44:14.269
Then that child internalizes
that this is the epitome of

01:44:14.280 --> 01:44:16.269
disgust with another human being.

01:44:16.580 --> 01:44:21.159
And I think children are so savvy without
realizing it, it's like, okay, well,

01:44:21.160 --> 01:44:25.820
then I guess you move toward that and you
aspire to that and you deflect from that.

01:44:26.080 --> 01:44:29.500
And you can see how these
trajectories can be set very early on.

01:44:29.500 --> 01:44:32.929
I mean, these are the four lane
highways that we were talking about

01:44:33.590 --> 01:44:37.457
in episode one, where just routes of
neural processing that can bring us

01:44:37.580 --> 01:44:41.650
to choices in life and places in life
that oftentimes you go like, I don't

01:44:41.650 --> 01:44:43.320
want to go down this path anymore.

01:44:45.070 --> 01:44:49.730
The exploration of early narratives,
both direct and indirect, first person

01:44:49.730 --> 01:44:52.170
and third person, seem so critical.

01:44:53.160 --> 01:44:54.389
How does one go about that?

01:44:54.389 --> 01:44:57.629
I mean, clearly, with a trained
clinician like you, you would

01:44:57.630 --> 01:44:59.030
guide somebody through the process.

01:44:59.030 --> 01:45:02.490
But if somebody were to try and
do this in some sort of structured

01:45:02.490 --> 01:45:06.060
way for themselves, what do
those lines of inquiry look like?

01:45:06.060 --> 01:45:10.739
Because we have vast number of experiences
from childhood, but some messages are

01:45:10.740 --> 01:45:11.870
going to be more salient than others.

01:45:11.920 --> 01:45:12.400
Paul Conti: Sure.

01:45:12.690 --> 01:45:12.849
Yeah.

01:45:12.849 --> 01:45:17.860
The idea that reflective self scrutiny can
help us, I think, is just a great idea.

01:45:17.860 --> 01:45:18.860
It's a great concept.

01:45:18.860 --> 01:45:22.680
And we do a lot of different things
sort of inside, and we're guided

01:45:22.680 --> 01:45:23.950
to do a lot of things inside.

01:45:24.300 --> 01:45:28.960
But this, I think, should overshadow many,
if not most of all those other things

01:45:28.960 --> 01:45:31.019
of what's really going on inside of me.

01:45:31.020 --> 01:45:34.159
Because if you think about
it, a lot of people will come

01:45:34.160 --> 01:45:36.269
through that and they'll learn.

01:45:36.269 --> 01:45:39.839
So the person is told like, this is what
beautiful is, this is what successful

01:45:39.860 --> 01:45:42.050
is, this is what good enough looks like.

01:45:42.670 --> 01:45:47.320
And that person may, through all sorts
of experiences, maybe other people in

01:45:47.320 --> 01:45:50.780
their lives who are more balanced, be
able to arrive on the other side of

01:45:50.780 --> 01:45:54.840
that even still sometimes going through
the midst of it, depending upon age

01:45:54.840 --> 01:45:59.970
and situation, and know, like, okay, my
father and mother thinks like, this is

01:45:59.970 --> 01:46:01.500
what beauty is, this is what success is.

01:46:01.500 --> 01:46:04.370
But it's one set of opinions,
and there's not a set of opinions

01:46:04.370 --> 01:46:05.819
that are going to define me.

01:46:05.819 --> 01:46:09.610
Sometimes people get to that place,
but a lot of times they don't.

01:46:09.639 --> 01:46:13.375
And they carry that lesson forward
and they're not aware of it, right?

01:46:13.680 --> 01:46:17.970
So they think that they're very
unattractive, even though other people

01:46:17.970 --> 01:46:19.280
are giving them different signals.

01:46:19.500 --> 01:46:22.400
They think that they're very
dumb, even though other people

01:46:22.400 --> 01:46:23.469
are giving them different signals.

01:46:23.469 --> 01:46:27.180
And their own grades and their own success
may be giving them different signals.

01:46:27.190 --> 01:46:30.130
But they're not putting
the two things together.

01:46:30.150 --> 01:46:32.130
And that's going to generate tension.

01:46:32.390 --> 01:46:36.110
That might be why that person doesn't
follow up on potential relationships.

01:46:36.110 --> 01:46:37.880
They just don't think they're
good enough and the person's

01:46:37.880 --> 01:46:39.820
eventually going to reject them
because of what they look like.

01:46:40.740 --> 01:46:45.070
They're taking that with them
in this example from childhood.

01:46:45.240 --> 01:46:50.410
Or they're not satisfied with the job
that in other ways is really great.

01:46:50.720 --> 01:46:51.590
They enjoy the work.

01:46:51.590 --> 01:46:52.960
They enjoy everything,
but doesn't pay enough.

01:46:53.450 --> 01:46:53.720
Why?

01:46:53.730 --> 01:46:56.699
Because they have some false idea
inside of what it's supposed to pay.

01:46:57.150 --> 01:47:00.360
Because it's what the parent
said by self scrutiny.

01:47:00.540 --> 01:47:01.380
What are the givens?

01:47:01.429 --> 01:47:03.959
I always think it goes
back to the math minor.

01:47:04.049 --> 01:47:08.160
If you can't solve the problem,
go back and look at the givens.

01:47:08.730 --> 01:47:10.330
What are you taking for granted?

01:47:10.330 --> 01:47:13.309
Like, oh, I know that every time
I see an X, that X equals four.

01:47:13.530 --> 01:47:13.860
Really?

01:47:13.870 --> 01:47:17.746
Maybe you wrote down four somehow
because you were thinking of four at

01:47:17.830 --> 01:47:20.379
the time and X actually is a three.

01:47:20.409 --> 01:47:20.610
Right.

01:47:21.089 --> 01:47:24.820
Just go back and look at what
you're taking for granted.

01:47:25.070 --> 01:47:28.410
And a lot of times this is what we're
doing in the therapy process, and then

01:47:28.410 --> 01:47:29.600
that's when the person can realize.

01:47:29.610 --> 01:47:33.500
So I'm simplifying, but for the
person to realize, like, oh, there's

01:47:33.510 --> 01:47:35.170
a voice in my head, so to speak.

01:47:35.170 --> 01:47:36.050
It's a natural voice.

01:47:36.290 --> 01:47:40.719
That is the voice of this person who
may not even be around anymore, whose

01:47:40.730 --> 01:47:44.660
opinion doesn't mean to me what it
did before, but that voice is saying,

01:47:44.670 --> 01:47:47.460
you're unattractive, you're not making
enough money, you're not good enough.

01:47:47.690 --> 01:47:48.180
And you know what?

01:47:48.240 --> 01:47:51.579
I don't believe that
they can identify that.

01:47:51.580 --> 01:47:54.180
And then it doesn't happen all at once.

01:47:54.180 --> 01:47:56.330
But you can get it out of you.

01:47:56.330 --> 01:47:58.930
Generally, you don't get it out of you
unless you realize that it's there.

01:47:59.160 --> 01:48:01.360
Andrew Huberman: What is the
process of getting it out?

01:48:02.700 --> 01:48:10.299
Because I think that we all have the
capacity to remember certain things and to

01:48:10.300 --> 01:48:13.710
arrive at a place where we can understand.

01:48:13.710 --> 01:48:16.529
Okay, I'm taking for granted
the fact that there's a voice

01:48:16.529 --> 01:48:18.260
in my head that says blank.

01:48:18.350 --> 01:48:20.820
Actually, I have a brief anecdote
to say about this, and this

01:48:20.830 --> 01:48:22.670
isn't I have a friend thing.

01:48:22.760 --> 01:48:26.690
I literally have a female friend
who the other day called me

01:48:27.020 --> 01:48:31.639
laughing and crying because she was
being evicted from her apartment.

01:48:32.070 --> 01:48:35.009
And she told her mother
about this over the phone.

01:48:35.010 --> 01:48:38.009
And her mother's response was,
well, at least you're thin.

01:48:39.100 --> 01:48:39.700
Paul Conti: Wow.

01:48:40.740 --> 01:48:43.820
Andrew Huberman: And she was laughing
and crying about it because it reflected

01:48:43.820 --> 01:48:48.580
so much of her childhood that no other
accomplishment of having a job, having

01:48:48.580 --> 01:48:52.330
an apartment, et cetera, mattered.

01:48:52.350 --> 01:48:54.090
It was about one thing.

01:48:54.139 --> 01:48:57.380
It was about a certain form of
aesthetic beauty that I'm not even

01:48:57.380 --> 01:49:00.660
sure she subscribes to, even though
she happens to be thin, right?

01:49:00.660 --> 01:49:05.009
So the fact that her mother would
lift that from the conversation,

01:49:05.010 --> 01:49:09.830
there's such a deprivation of so
many things in that interaction.

01:49:10.420 --> 01:49:12.320
But it really wasn't
about that interaction.

01:49:13.360 --> 01:49:16.700
She was calling me because it was really
about her entire childhood, right.

01:49:16.720 --> 01:49:19.179
And obviously, I'm not
equipped to solve the problem.

01:49:19.180 --> 01:49:21.490
And it wasn't a request for
money or anything of that sort.

01:49:21.490 --> 01:49:29.309
It was almost like the hilarity and the
sadness of the whole picture, right?

01:49:29.430 --> 01:49:33.570
But again, it speaks to these
narratives that we internalize

01:49:33.570 --> 01:49:36.420
and that sometimes show up in very
glaring ways in the real world.

01:49:36.420 --> 01:49:38.929
It's like to hear that, I
think, was shocking to her.

01:49:38.940 --> 01:49:41.460
I think she needed to
tell me to, is this real?

01:49:41.460 --> 01:49:44.329
But then it was clear that
that message had existed in her

01:49:44.330 --> 01:49:45.730
head for a long time anyway.

01:49:46.650 --> 01:49:49.299
Paul Conti: That could be very
pivotal if she realizes that, right?

01:49:49.330 --> 01:49:52.870
And even the power of the humor of
it is like, this is absurd, right.

01:49:52.880 --> 01:49:57.190
That can be very powerful in creating
change, because if there's some vestige

01:49:57.240 --> 01:50:01.199
of that inside of her where she still
believes, like, oh, I'm not good enough

01:50:01.200 --> 01:50:04.840
because I achieved A, B and C, but I
don't look like X or whatever, it can

01:50:05.010 --> 01:50:09.160
very much help because there's a lot of
power behind realizing that absurdity

01:50:09.160 --> 01:50:10.870
of, like, oh, my, that's bizarre.

01:50:11.100 --> 01:50:13.230
But wait, is any of that inside of me?

01:50:13.310 --> 01:50:14.900
Am I carrying some of that with me?

01:50:14.900 --> 01:50:18.700
I mean, there's an incentive for
self scrutiny through what you're

01:50:18.700 --> 01:50:22.489
describing, because what's the ideal
amount of that to still be in her?

01:50:23.080 --> 01:50:23.460
Zero.

01:50:24.859 --> 01:50:28.090
Andrew Huberman: So as one comes to
realize, the messages they've heard,

01:50:28.090 --> 01:50:32.130
or perhaps, like, in this case,
that they're still hearing, is the

01:50:32.130 --> 01:50:35.710
process of overcoming those messages
and really arriving at the self.

01:50:36.250 --> 01:50:40.799
It sounds to me like it's a two part
process, at least two part process.

01:50:40.809 --> 01:50:43.110
It's to look in the bin
of what are the givens?

01:50:43.110 --> 01:50:46.610
What am I taking for granted
about the internal narratives and

01:50:46.740 --> 01:50:51.460
thinking about their origins in
childhood or elsewhere, but then also

01:50:51.809 --> 01:50:54.740
cultivating the self awareness piece
that's under the function of self.

01:50:54.740 --> 01:50:57.580
Like, wait, what's really true
for me at the level of me?

01:51:00.059 --> 01:51:03.180
And this is really, I think, about
separating out the voices in one's

01:51:03.190 --> 01:51:07.450
head, these internalized narratives from
the person that we really truly are.

01:51:07.700 --> 01:51:10.390
Paul Conti: Because the idea is
that those two pillars encompass

01:51:10.410 --> 01:51:11.549
everything we need to look at.

01:51:12.410 --> 01:51:14.580
Those ten cupboards encompass everything.

01:51:14.580 --> 01:51:15.599
So it's all that.

01:51:15.980 --> 01:51:18.680
The person who's going and looking
at the givens, they're trying to

01:51:18.680 --> 01:51:22.420
understand what might be in my
unconscious mind that I'm not aware of.

01:51:23.180 --> 01:51:24.220
And, wow.

01:51:25.020 --> 01:51:28.870
The last time I got this big award
at work, I had this reflexive thought

01:51:28.870 --> 01:51:30.179
of like, but you're not thin enough.

01:51:30.840 --> 01:51:31.460
Wow.

01:51:31.849 --> 01:51:32.309
Whoa.

01:51:32.320 --> 01:51:32.690
Right?

01:51:33.070 --> 01:51:35.559
There could be a process, like,
that's going on inside of me.

01:51:35.580 --> 01:51:37.210
I don't want that going on inside of me.

01:51:37.630 --> 01:51:41.880
So the process of trying what is
unconscious in us that may be causing

01:51:41.880 --> 01:51:44.980
us harm, which is often where,
that's where the trauma goes, right.

01:51:44.980 --> 01:51:48.440
It's where the childhood trauma
seats itself, which brings us back

01:51:48.440 --> 01:51:49.949
around to the intrusive thoughts.

01:51:50.490 --> 01:51:51.889
Intrusive, negative thoughts.

01:51:51.889 --> 01:51:56.430
A negative self dialogue usually
does not mean what it meant to the

01:51:56.430 --> 01:51:58.470
man who need to change jobs, right.

01:51:58.500 --> 01:52:00.630
Because they were there for a good reason.

01:52:01.050 --> 01:52:03.680
Then he needed to make change more often.

01:52:05.219 --> 01:52:10.910
The vestiges, the hangover, the
lingering badness of some prior trauma.

01:52:10.940 --> 01:52:14.130
So oftentimes when you think, we
talked a little bit yesterday about

01:52:14.130 --> 01:52:17.110
the person who was driving in the
car and just telling themselves over

01:52:17.110 --> 01:52:19.130
and over that they're a loser, right?

01:52:19.130 --> 01:52:21.640
And then they can't achieve
the things that they achieved

01:52:21.650 --> 01:52:22.940
when they stopped doing that.

01:52:23.120 --> 01:52:25.929
I'm simplifying a little,
but that's the basics of it.

01:52:26.380 --> 01:52:31.290
Because the intrusive thoughts, the
self narrative, all the negativity in

01:52:31.290 --> 01:52:35.780
us is often coming from places that
are in the unconscious mind, right?

01:52:35.780 --> 01:52:36.620
Not always.

01:52:36.790 --> 01:52:39.710
But this idea that I don't think
I'm good enough, I'm saying it to

01:52:39.710 --> 01:52:43.539
myself over and over again, like,
wow, let's go back and look at why.

01:52:43.549 --> 01:52:46.000
Because the answer to that,
again, lies in a different place.

01:52:46.000 --> 01:52:47.750
There's just a different roadmap, right?

01:52:47.760 --> 01:52:51.250
The man who needed to change jobs
had a roadmap that spent a little

01:52:51.250 --> 01:52:54.110
bit of time in the I, self awareness.

01:52:54.110 --> 01:52:58.433
And then it went through self defense
mechanisms in action land, and it

01:52:58.433 --> 01:53:02.180
spent a lot of time with behavior,
and then it got up to the strivings.

01:53:02.240 --> 01:53:03.150
That's his roadmap.

01:53:03.450 --> 01:53:07.960
Whereas for someone who's laboring under
the intrusive thoughts, the negative self

01:53:07.960 --> 01:53:13.050
talk, the automaticity, the givens of
childhood trauma then needs to go to a

01:53:13.050 --> 01:53:17.089
different place where now we're spending
time in the unconscious mind thinking

01:53:17.090 --> 01:53:20.969
about what's there, figuring out what's
there, bringing things to consciousness.

01:53:20.980 --> 01:53:25.130
That person, say, realizing
maybe your friend, you had this

01:53:25.140 --> 01:53:27.190
realization of, like, oh, my goodness.

01:53:27.400 --> 01:53:30.360
We say, wow, did that bring something
to the conscious mind in her.

01:53:30.640 --> 01:53:34.469
If so, great, let's look at that and
let's look a lot at it and let's look.

01:53:34.469 --> 01:53:36.030
Are there other things there too?

01:53:36.559 --> 01:53:37.670
Are there other givens?

01:53:38.160 --> 01:53:41.369
Let's bring them to conscious, to
consciousness so that we can talk about

01:53:41.369 --> 01:53:44.769
them, we can identify them, and then
look at how does that relate to defense

01:53:44.769 --> 01:53:46.360
mechanisms and character structure?

01:53:46.360 --> 01:53:47.930
And now what are we doing?

01:53:48.250 --> 01:53:53.199
A process of interested inquiry
like this is really interesting.

01:53:53.200 --> 01:53:55.559
I mean, it should be interesting
to the person doing it.

01:53:55.650 --> 01:53:56.900
It's them, right?

01:53:56.900 --> 01:53:59.656
And it should be interesting to the
person doing it with them, right?

01:53:59.656 --> 01:54:02.259
Because if you're a therapist
and it's not interesting to you,

01:54:02.259 --> 01:54:03.730
you need another job, right?

01:54:04.320 --> 01:54:05.549
Or you're talking to a friend.

01:54:05.560 --> 01:54:07.209
Like if it's a friend is
going to be interested.

01:54:07.210 --> 01:54:12.160
So there's an interested, honest,
open inquiry with the idea of holiday

01:54:12.360 --> 01:54:16.059
.
 Let's learn things so that we
can make change for the better.

01:54:16.299 --> 01:54:19.910
And even though, as we talked about
yesterday, the intrusive thoughts and

01:54:19.910 --> 01:54:23.710
the self dialogue that's gone on over
and over and over again, it doesn't go

01:54:23.710 --> 01:54:28.320
away easily, but that doesn't mean it
doesn't atrophy over time and go away.

01:54:28.610 --> 01:54:31.669
Or that the person can have that
reflexive thought like, oh, there's

01:54:31.670 --> 01:54:34.439
the thought again that I'm a loser
or that I should cut myself or I

01:54:34.440 --> 01:54:35.796
should drink or whatever it is.

01:54:35.980 --> 01:54:39.720
I know that thought appears in me
automatically at times because it

01:54:39.720 --> 01:54:42.950
was in my head for so long, but
it does not telling me anything,

01:54:43.530 --> 01:54:44.710
it's just an automatic thought.

01:54:44.710 --> 01:54:46.860
It's not telling me I should drink,
it's not telling me anything, right?

01:54:46.900 --> 01:54:49.650
Other than the fact that, oh,
that's what happens in human beings.

01:54:50.179 --> 01:54:54.039
That's how the self understanding
brings change in us and gets us

01:54:54.059 --> 01:54:57.460
over the barriers of why i've been
trying this for what modern mental

01:54:57.460 --> 01:54:58.310
health would often have us think.

01:54:58.310 --> 01:55:02.330
I took the Silexan, I did
the ten sessions of CBT.

01:55:02.330 --> 01:55:02.979
I'm a failure.

01:55:02.980 --> 01:55:04.099
Nothing will ever get better.

01:55:04.800 --> 01:55:07.839
A different framing that says,
hey, this can get better over time.

01:55:07.839 --> 01:55:10.599
And my understanding and my efforts
and my thought redirection, my

01:55:10.600 --> 01:55:12.760
behavioral changes all makes it better.

01:55:12.760 --> 01:55:15.400
And then those things I don't want
in my head, they're going away.

01:55:16.010 --> 01:55:18.040
It's taken time, but they're going away.

01:55:18.650 --> 01:55:21.139
Andrew Huberman: I'm relieved to hear
you say that one can have intrusive

01:55:21.139 --> 01:55:27.229
thoughts and that one approach to dealing
with those is to acknowledge them and

01:55:27.230 --> 01:55:33.280
look at them and not try and push them
back deeper, not try and eradicate them.

01:55:35.420 --> 01:55:38.949
I'm familiar with having intrusive
thoughts, not all the time, but at

01:55:39.099 --> 01:55:40.440
various periods throughout my life.

01:55:40.440 --> 01:55:46.290
And the idea that one can just
extinguish them is a great idea,

01:55:46.290 --> 01:55:48.460
but that's simply not the way
it's worked, at least not for me.

01:55:48.770 --> 01:55:53.469
But I have found that if I just say, okay,
this is spontaneously coming up through

01:55:53.470 --> 01:55:58.050
the neural circuits of my subconscious,
and they're intrusive, and I don't like

01:55:58.050 --> 01:56:03.639
them, but I eventually arrive at exactly
the place that you describe, which is

01:56:03.639 --> 01:56:06.310
that there's nothing actionable here.

01:56:07.780 --> 01:56:12.910
They go from being intrusive and
troubling to intrusive and just kind

01:56:12.910 --> 01:56:15.610
of mildly irritating to intrusive.

01:56:15.610 --> 01:56:17.289
And like, okay.

01:56:20.429 --> 01:56:24.070
And yes, I go through some redirect,
like, try and redirect my attention from

01:56:24.070 --> 01:56:27.270
time to time when they're happening,
but I eventually just get to a place

01:56:27.270 --> 01:56:31.389
where it's like, okay, it's just
a boring story or boring imagery.

01:56:31.530 --> 01:56:32.459
There's nothing there.

01:56:32.809 --> 01:56:33.569
There's nothing there.

01:56:33.570 --> 01:56:36.240
And then they eventually
break up like clouds.

01:56:36.620 --> 01:56:38.939
That process could take a while because...

01:56:39.240 --> 01:56:41.580
Paul Conti: You took the energy
out of them, you made them go

01:56:41.580 --> 01:56:43.389
away, which happened over time.

01:56:43.389 --> 01:56:45.995
And then the energy that was so
powerful becomes less and less and less.

01:56:45.995 --> 01:56:46.580
And what happens?

01:56:46.580 --> 01:56:47.990
They dissipate, they atrophy.

01:56:48.540 --> 01:56:50.570
That's how they go away,
because there's no more power.

01:56:51.230 --> 01:56:53.130
There's no more power in them.

01:56:53.179 --> 01:56:55.600
And that really is the
way that we make change.

01:56:55.600 --> 01:57:01.760
And I think your emphasis upon the
fact that it takes time, the fact

01:57:01.760 --> 01:57:05.050
that it takes effort, the fact
that it only goes away slowly.

01:57:05.500 --> 01:57:11.110
Over 20 years of, at times, being a
therapist, what I've seen the most

01:57:11.110 --> 01:57:15.009
daunting, the thing that makes people
just give up and go away and go back

01:57:15.009 --> 01:57:18.589
to the things that hurt bad, give up
on themselves, are that it takes time.

01:57:18.709 --> 01:57:22.920
And if you think it's supposed to take
two weeks, and the world around you

01:57:22.920 --> 01:57:26.104
is kind of leading you to think that,
and then you go for help, and the

01:57:26.104 --> 01:57:29.460
help kind of leads you to think that
whether it's two weeks or it's ten

01:57:29.460 --> 01:57:35.820
weeks, if it's going to take two years,
you're going to go away disheartened.

01:57:35.830 --> 01:57:38.740
Or maybe more angry at
yourself or maybe demoralized.

01:57:38.750 --> 01:57:43.650
So we have to look at the truth of
all of this, parallel to your story.

01:57:43.650 --> 01:57:48.050
In my own life, for years and years
and years, I carried a negative

01:57:48.059 --> 01:57:51.309
voice inside that was always waiting
for me to do something wrong.

01:57:51.339 --> 01:57:55.090
So if I say something that's a little
bit off or not exactly what I want to say

01:57:55.300 --> 01:57:57.490
now, it would say, like, that wasn't good.

01:57:57.490 --> 01:58:00.740
It says something negative inside to me,
or it's waiting for me to drop something

01:58:00.740 --> 01:58:02.549
and say that I'm stupid and clumsy, right.

01:58:02.820 --> 01:58:04.409
With me all the time.

01:58:04.660 --> 01:58:07.610
But over time, through self reflection,
through therapy, like, through a

01:58:07.610 --> 01:58:11.290
lot of hard work, but a desire for
things to be better and a desire to

01:58:11.290 --> 01:58:14.060
understand it, it's not there anymore.

01:58:14.090 --> 01:58:17.000
I mean, every now and then,
it'll raise its head, right?

01:58:17.270 --> 01:58:17.860
I'll do something.

01:58:17.860 --> 01:58:18.110
Really?

01:58:18.110 --> 01:58:19.200
I dropped a cup of coffee.

01:58:19.200 --> 01:58:21.910
I haven't done it in ten years,
and it made a mess, and now people

01:58:21.910 --> 01:58:24.830
are coming to clean it up, and,
man, the voice came back, right?

01:58:24.830 --> 01:58:26.049
But I could recognize it.

01:58:26.480 --> 01:58:27.740
I really feel bad about this.

01:58:27.740 --> 01:58:30.190
And now it gives that voice
a chance to come out, but it

01:58:30.210 --> 01:58:32.530
doesn't come out much anymore.

01:58:32.530 --> 01:58:36.700
Whereas I lived with it for years,
it doesn't come out much anymore.

01:58:37.000 --> 01:58:39.739
And when it came out not that
long ago, I could recognize it.

01:58:39.920 --> 01:58:42.870
I'm not happy I did this and
let me help clean it up, but it

01:58:42.870 --> 01:58:44.200
doesn't mean I'm an idiot, right?

01:58:44.210 --> 01:58:48.920
So the voice in my head can just
go away, as I've been helping it

01:58:48.930 --> 01:58:50.650
to do for a bunch of years now.

01:58:51.070 --> 01:58:51.210
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

01:58:51.210 --> 01:58:54.210
I think also important for people to
understand is that it takes time, but

01:58:54.210 --> 01:59:00.599
that we can all potentially engage in,
right, actions, moving towards strivings

01:59:01.079 --> 01:59:06.760
and hopefulness as we cope with those
and try and diminish those internal

01:59:06.760 --> 01:59:08.610
narratives, those intrusive thoughts.

01:59:08.610 --> 01:59:12.490
It's not as if during the entire
process, you can't function.

01:59:12.490 --> 01:59:17.790
I mean, I think that it's cognitively and
sometimes even physically demanding to do,

01:59:17.790 --> 01:59:24.389
but we can still engage in healthy ways in
the world, and we can still try and avoid

01:59:24.410 --> 01:59:27.580
acting out and avoid forms of denial.

01:59:28.430 --> 01:59:33.740
As I say this, I'm realizing that the
wish for or the impulse to really just

01:59:33.740 --> 01:59:39.400
suppress intrusive thought born of
trauma or whatever else is really futile.

01:59:39.620 --> 01:59:40.600
That's not going to work.

01:59:40.600 --> 01:59:41.820
It's not going to work.

01:59:41.859 --> 01:59:46.160
We have to embrace these narratives and
not expect them to disappear in a finger

01:59:46.160 --> 01:59:52.269
snap, but embrace them and see them and
look at them and be unafraid to look

01:59:52.270 --> 01:59:55.220
at them and discount where they are.

01:59:55.410 --> 01:59:56.570
Absolutely not true.

01:59:57.770 --> 02:00:01.060
Paul Conti: I would say
unafraid to understand, right?

02:00:01.289 --> 02:00:07.000
Because we must understand means we must
look at what's going on inside of us.

02:00:07.050 --> 02:00:09.760
When I didn't like that voice
but was afraid of it, like,

02:00:09.760 --> 02:00:11.003
what is going on inside of me?

02:00:11.003 --> 02:00:12.059
What does this say about me?

02:00:12.910 --> 02:00:14.910
And I'm directing away from it.

02:00:14.910 --> 02:00:18.069
Well, that's why it was with
me for, like, several decades.

02:00:18.400 --> 02:00:22.140
But when I start to go look at
it, I can find an answer to it.

02:00:22.390 --> 02:00:27.059
And again, you have to look at what's
going on in that person because one

02:00:27.059 --> 02:00:30.240
might presume, and maybe people listening
are presuming this or maybe not, but

02:00:31.500 --> 02:00:34.699
a reasonable presumption that might
just reflexively happen in a person

02:00:34.959 --> 02:00:39.070
would be to think that, oh, when I was
younger, the messaging I was getting

02:00:39.070 --> 02:00:40.280
was that you're not good enough.

02:00:40.690 --> 02:00:40.960
Right?

02:00:40.960 --> 02:00:41.540
You're not good enough.

02:00:41.680 --> 02:00:44.180
That's why I carry with
me, you're not good enough.

02:00:45.170 --> 02:00:50.490
But it's not that sometimes it's the
opposite, that I was rewarded a lot

02:00:50.490 --> 02:00:53.689
when I was younger for doing things
in a way everyone thought was great.

02:00:54.299 --> 02:00:57.629
Like getting great grades and being well
behaved, doing all sorts of things that

02:00:57.690 --> 02:01:03.080
brought a lot of positive reinforcement
to me, but I never handled well things

02:01:03.090 --> 02:01:05.399
that fell even a little bit short of that.

02:01:05.399 --> 02:01:06.849
And then it would evoke a lot of shame.

02:01:06.849 --> 02:01:11.199
So the oppression inside is not
coming from denigration, it's

02:01:11.199 --> 02:01:12.430
coming from something different.

02:01:13.360 --> 02:01:17.540
Which is also why this is not
a search to blame someone.

02:01:18.560 --> 02:01:21.690
Because sometimes the people
who are giving the message,

02:01:21.870 --> 02:01:23.240
they're doing the best they can.

02:01:23.240 --> 02:01:27.859
I mean, someone who's saying to a child,
you're a loser, like, that's not okay.

02:01:28.489 --> 02:01:30.209
No matter what, that's not okay.

02:01:30.559 --> 02:01:32.790
But that's often not how it happens.

02:01:33.120 --> 02:01:37.350
The parent, like you says, communicating,
they don't realize that every time

02:01:37.350 --> 02:01:40.030
they're admiring a certain level of
wealth or a certain kind of beauty,

02:01:40.030 --> 02:01:44.039
they're giving that message that the
child that doesn't meet that or ends

02:01:44.040 --> 02:01:45.420
up not meeting that isn't good enough.

02:01:45.790 --> 02:01:46.760
But they don't know that.

02:01:46.760 --> 02:01:51.090
Or my parents tried to nurture me
and they did a good job of it in many

02:01:51.090 --> 02:01:52.660
ways, and teachers did a good job.

02:01:52.720 --> 02:01:55.790
So they're not realizing, hey,
this person's going to end up

02:01:56.400 --> 02:01:59.680
a bunch of years from now not
thinking anything's good enough.

02:02:00.030 --> 02:02:00.740
They don't know that.

02:02:00.740 --> 02:02:03.139
So it's not a search for blame.

02:02:03.139 --> 02:02:04.679
And I think that's very, very important.

02:02:04.789 --> 02:02:05.580
Very important.

02:02:05.670 --> 02:02:10.070
Because often people don't want to look
inside because they think either I'm

02:02:10.070 --> 02:02:13.710
going to find something dramatically
wrong with me, and the answer I would

02:02:13.720 --> 02:02:17.069
give is there's almost surely not
something dramatically wrong with

02:02:17.080 --> 02:02:18.440
you if you're having that thought.

02:02:18.759 --> 02:02:22.290
And if somehow there is, you're better
off looking at it now than later.

02:02:23.459 --> 02:02:24.359
So that's part of it.

02:02:24.359 --> 02:02:27.970
The other part is that people
become worried that they're

02:02:28.000 --> 02:02:29.519
going to ruin something.

02:02:30.890 --> 02:02:35.460
I like my parents and if I go look
at this, I'm going to hate them.

02:02:35.980 --> 02:02:38.629
People say things or
think things like that.

02:02:38.630 --> 02:02:42.799
And the idea that we may get down
to something that really involves

02:02:42.810 --> 02:02:46.780
someone being responsible for something
bad, now, if that's the truth, the

02:02:46.780 --> 02:02:48.429
person already knows that inside.

02:02:48.510 --> 02:02:52.380
The vast majority of times, they know
that they're just not facing that.

02:02:52.639 --> 02:02:54.999
But most of the time also, it's not that.

02:02:54.999 --> 02:02:56.690
It's just like, okay,
that's how life evolved.

02:02:56.690 --> 02:02:57.950
And what's the predisposition?

02:02:57.950 --> 02:03:01.400
Like, I was smart enough to get good
grades, and I have a low threshold

02:03:01.420 --> 02:03:05.710
for shame, and people reinforced me
and like, oh, I can kind of understand

02:03:05.710 --> 02:03:08.630
that, so then I can get control over it.

02:03:08.630 --> 02:03:12.880
It's not a search for anger,
frustration, blame of self or others.

02:03:13.179 --> 02:03:13.419
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

02:03:13.909 --> 02:03:19.099
Oftentimes I hear that people are afraid
of dealing with these deeper issues or

02:03:19.100 --> 02:03:23.750
addressing these deeper issues for fear
that they'll lose, say, their drive.

02:03:23.790 --> 02:03:24.080
Right.

02:03:24.220 --> 02:03:26.400
The thing that makes them
successful in the first place.

02:03:26.400 --> 02:03:30.500
And that allows them perhaps even to
afford therapy or afford the time to

02:03:30.500 --> 02:03:31.950
think about these sorts of questions.

02:03:31.950 --> 02:03:37.760
So it seems to me that the drives that you
referred to earlier, the generative drive,

02:03:37.780 --> 02:03:44.250
the aggressive drive, and the pleasure
drive, are such critical nodes or areas

02:03:44.250 --> 02:03:49.960
to look for all of us in terms of figuring
out whether or not we're doing well or

02:03:49.960 --> 02:03:54.800
less well according to some features
that are pretty much universal in people.

02:03:55.080 --> 02:03:59.790
Essentially what I'm saying is, at
least by my understanding, we all

02:03:59.790 --> 02:04:02.200
have drives to some extent or another.

02:04:02.900 --> 02:04:06.389
And to the extent that our aggressive
drive is very high and pleasure

02:04:06.389 --> 02:04:09.700
drive is very high, and whether
or not it's pointed in the right

02:04:09.700 --> 02:04:11.469
direction, it can be generative.

02:04:11.590 --> 02:04:14.330
If it's not, perhaps it can
undermine our generative drive.

02:04:14.950 --> 02:04:19.770
I'm very curious to know how you've
observed the different ranges of

02:04:19.770 --> 02:04:22.870
these drives in people and how that
predicts whether or not people will

02:04:22.870 --> 02:04:26.229
do more or less well in different
areas of life, essentially how

02:04:26.230 --> 02:04:27.670
the different drives play out.

02:04:29.520 --> 02:04:32.390
Paul Conti: I think that the first
thing to say is where the drives are

02:04:32.460 --> 02:04:36.850
at, so to speak, in any of us is a
combination of nature and nurture.

02:04:37.090 --> 02:04:42.960
So the nature part tells us the range,
sort of that the drive is going to be.

02:04:43.210 --> 02:04:47.649
But because nurture means so much
to humans, as we understand from

02:04:47.650 --> 02:04:51.620
epigenetics, from the advancement
of science, we see more and more

02:04:51.620 --> 02:04:53.710
and more how much nurture matters.

02:04:53.970 --> 02:04:58.059
So the range that's denoted by
nature is probably pretty broad.

02:04:58.120 --> 02:05:04.020
We see the manifestation of that, and then
the nurture lets us then move that drive.

02:05:04.029 --> 02:05:07.140
Now, sometimes nurture, that's not
gone in the right place can move

02:05:07.140 --> 02:05:08.460
the drive in the wrong direction.

02:05:08.890 --> 02:05:12.990
But as adults, as people who can
take care of ourselves, who can

02:05:13.280 --> 02:05:17.830
learn about ourselves, we can
change where the drives are seated.

02:05:17.860 --> 02:05:21.720
It's not an easy thing to do because
it requires a lot of changes of

02:05:21.720 --> 02:05:24.190
self knowledge and hard work.

02:05:24.299 --> 02:05:25.280
But we can do it.

02:05:25.530 --> 02:05:29.330
We can change the sort of
array of how those drives are

02:05:29.330 --> 02:05:31.450
manifesting themselves within us.

02:05:31.450 --> 02:05:33.969
And we see that, I mean, that's
part of the hopefulness of

02:05:34.370 --> 02:05:35.500
mental health treatment, right?

02:05:35.500 --> 02:05:39.760
That we see not just surface changes,
but we can see changes on a deeper level.

02:05:39.760 --> 02:05:42.830
So I think it's important that
these things are not fixed.

02:05:43.119 --> 02:05:45.059
Although there are some natural elements.

02:05:45.270 --> 02:05:49.349
Someone who may have a natural sort of
low aggression or low self assertion,

02:05:49.350 --> 02:05:52.490
okay, it's going to be in the lower
range, but it doesn't mean that it's

02:05:52.500 --> 02:05:54.820
locked in at any one particular point.

02:05:55.210 --> 02:05:59.079
And that the place that we want to be,
what is the place that's consistent

02:05:59.090 --> 02:06:04.600
with the things that we want, the
agency and gratitude as verbs, and the

02:06:04.600 --> 02:06:06.160
sense of well being and all of that.

02:06:06.520 --> 02:06:12.519
So the idea is the state of health
has the generative drive as prominent.

02:06:12.519 --> 02:06:16.429
It's the dominant drive, and then
aggression and pleasure, which are

02:06:16.430 --> 02:06:20.670
still active in us, but they're
subserving the generative drive, and

02:06:20.690 --> 02:06:22.440
that's the state that we wish to be in.

02:06:22.440 --> 02:06:26.360
So when we're assessing, okay, why is
there something that doesn't feel okay

02:06:26.360 --> 02:06:28.769
or something that's not going okay, then?

02:06:28.790 --> 02:06:32.720
One way to start is to look at,
okay, what's going on in the person,

02:06:32.720 --> 02:06:34.279
what may be off in the drives.

02:06:34.279 --> 02:06:39.080
That gives us a very strong
idea of, okay, what's going on.

02:06:39.080 --> 02:06:44.769
A way of understanding what's going on as
we then go and look in the ten cupboards

02:06:44.830 --> 02:06:48.670
to figure out the specifics, okay, what
is actually going on here that we can then

02:06:48.670 --> 02:06:53.610
change, but the framing of what's going
on can come through the lens of looking at

02:06:53.610 --> 02:06:56.070
the drives and how they're manifest in us.

02:06:56.370 --> 02:07:00.700
Andrew Huberman: What does it look like
when the aggressive drive is very high

02:07:01.450 --> 02:07:04.120
and the pleasure drive is also very high?

02:07:04.360 --> 02:07:08.639
Paul Conti: So if these drives are
running too high, where we end up

02:07:08.770 --> 02:07:12.169
at is in a place of envy and envy.

02:07:12.170 --> 02:07:15.719
I'm always sort of on the soapbox
about envy, because I think envy

02:07:15.720 --> 02:07:17.830
is just so wildly destructive.

02:07:18.140 --> 02:07:22.910
And if the aggressive drive is
very high, so the person say, in

02:07:25.009 --> 02:07:28.049
one way this can manifest itself,
just wants more and more and more.

02:07:28.049 --> 02:07:31.800
They're not getting satisfaction
from anything, but they want more.

02:07:31.960 --> 02:07:35.580
That may be because of a strong
vulnerability inside of them.

02:07:35.580 --> 02:07:38.420
So something that might map
to narcissism, for example.

02:07:39.170 --> 02:07:43.950
There could be a strong aggressive
drive to get more, and that leads

02:07:43.950 --> 02:07:45.690
to something that's very unhealthy.

02:07:45.690 --> 02:07:50.200
So the idea that I want more, I need more,
I don't have enough, I can't get enough,

02:07:50.470 --> 02:07:58.269
then fosters envy, which is not the desire
to be better or to have more, but it's

02:07:58.270 --> 02:08:02.550
just the desire to feel better about the
self, whether that involves raising the

02:08:02.550 --> 02:08:04.729
self up or bringing someone else down.

02:08:04.729 --> 02:08:06.910
That's why envy is destructive.

02:08:07.160 --> 02:08:11.600
So very high levels of aggression that
are not tempered, for example, by a

02:08:11.600 --> 02:08:16.840
generative drive that would also be
high, then create a circumstance of

02:08:16.849 --> 02:08:19.400
envy, and the envy is destructive.

02:08:19.400 --> 02:08:23.530
And the same happens if the
pleasure drive is very, very strong.

02:08:23.830 --> 02:08:25.980
So if one continues to want more pleasure.

02:08:25.980 --> 02:08:27.530
So I can't find any satisfaction.

02:08:27.540 --> 02:08:28.750
I don't feel good about myself.

02:08:28.980 --> 02:08:30.650
I feel bereft inside.

02:08:31.140 --> 02:08:35.469
And I see that pleasure can make me
feel better, but just for a little bit.

02:08:35.719 --> 02:08:38.049
Then it fades away and I want
more of it, and I want more of it.

02:08:38.360 --> 02:08:40.950
That also can lead to the place of envy.

02:08:40.950 --> 02:08:42.240
Like that's the outcome.

02:08:42.559 --> 02:08:47.299
So if the aggressive drive is running
very high, or the pleasure drive is

02:08:47.300 --> 02:08:51.430
running very high, or if both are running
very high, but it only takes one in

02:08:51.430 --> 02:08:53.379
order to end up in a place of envy.

02:08:53.620 --> 02:08:59.030
So if the generative drive is not high
enough to overcome how high the aggressive

02:08:59.030 --> 02:09:01.720
drive is, which would mean then the
aggressive drive would be sublimated

02:09:02.050 --> 02:09:03.980
towards good, productive things.

02:09:03.990 --> 02:09:07.219
So take the energy and put it
towards something that is goodness.

02:09:07.490 --> 02:09:11.910
But if the aggressive drive was way
out there ahead of the generative

02:09:11.920 --> 02:09:16.389
drive, that ends up in a place of
envy, as does the pleasure drive.

02:09:16.400 --> 02:09:21.190
If I want more and more and more,
but I never get satisfaction from

02:09:21.440 --> 02:09:25.660
anything, it never brings me any sense
of goodness that where it ends is in a

02:09:25.660 --> 02:09:29.070
place of vulnerability and resentment.

02:09:29.290 --> 02:09:31.809
Because envy involves wanting more.

02:09:33.009 --> 02:09:37.210
If we look at what's really
going on, envy under the surface

02:09:37.219 --> 02:09:38.610
involves wanting everything, right?

02:09:38.610 --> 02:09:44.620
If a person is at the outer limits of
envy, which is why envy is so destructive.

02:09:44.639 --> 02:09:49.739
Because if I can't get enough pleasure
and there's so much aggression in me, then

02:09:49.870 --> 02:09:51.669
I'm not going to make myself feel better.

02:09:51.719 --> 02:09:54.970
But what I can do is make
other people feel worse.

02:09:55.490 --> 02:09:56.860
Andrew Huberman: I want to
ask you more about envy.

02:09:56.860 --> 02:10:02.500
But first I want to ask is one way to
characterize the generative drive and

02:10:02.660 --> 02:10:08.500
to distinguish it from the other drives
is to say that generative drives are pro

02:10:08.500 --> 02:10:15.080
social, meaning they tend to bring about
benevolent interactions between people in.

02:10:15.080 --> 02:10:19.530
Paul Conti: The sense that pro
social as constructive, right?

02:10:20.080 --> 02:10:24.869
In a sense, building goodness, then yes,
because it's the drive in us that makes

02:10:24.870 --> 02:10:26.970
us want to love and nurture things.

02:10:26.970 --> 02:10:30.309
That makes us want to learn and
sometimes learn to make better in the

02:10:30.310 --> 02:10:32.150
world or learn for learning's sake.

02:10:32.430 --> 02:10:35.210
The drive is a drive of goodness.

02:10:35.470 --> 02:10:39.330
So if the drive is then going to
enact itself in the world around

02:10:39.330 --> 02:10:44.019
us, it's going to be pro social,
because we exist as social units.

02:10:44.270 --> 02:10:44.300
I

02:10:44.300 --> 02:10:46.779
mean, if we decide, oh, I want
to be an island off somewhere,

02:10:46.869 --> 02:10:47.909
that's not healthy, right?

02:10:47.910 --> 02:10:53.130
We exist in social units from small, like
a nuclear family to a neighborhood, all

02:10:53.130 --> 02:10:56.000
the way up to nations and to the planet.

02:10:56.260 --> 02:11:00.170
So if we perceive the truth of that,
that, hey, there's an interdependence

02:11:00.389 --> 02:11:06.760
between me and others, and I see that
then the drive will lead to choices and

02:11:06.820 --> 02:11:08.820
behaviors that are socially constructive.

02:11:10.170 --> 02:11:12.660
Andrew Huberman: Earlier, you talked
about aggression, and you were clear

02:11:12.660 --> 02:11:16.730
to make sure that we all understood
that aggression does not necessarily

02:11:16.730 --> 02:11:20.919
mean violent aggression, that there
are different forms of aggression.

02:11:21.230 --> 02:11:24.130
I'm curious if you could give us
some examples of how you've observed

02:11:25.250 --> 02:11:30.240
people with high levels of aggression
and high levels of pleasure drive as

02:11:30.240 --> 02:11:36.409
well, both male and female, and here,
without defaulting to stereotypes.

02:11:36.410 --> 02:11:40.060
I think a lot of people just despite
the fact that you've clarified

02:11:40.060 --> 02:11:43.570
what aggression is and isn't in
the context of this conversation,

02:11:43.770 --> 02:11:47.680
we hear the word aggression and we
think verbal attack, physical attack.

02:11:48.210 --> 02:11:51.250
However, the way you're describing
aggression and the aggressive drive, I

02:11:51.250 --> 02:11:55.020
have a feeling that you're referring to
other expressions of aggression as well.

02:11:55.179 --> 02:11:59.509
Paul Conti: So if the aggressive drive
is running too high, and that could have

02:11:59.699 --> 02:12:04.089
factors of nature, factors of nurture,
factors of the situation the person is

02:12:04.090 --> 02:12:08.590
in, factors of their whole life, but it
ends up at the moment in a place that

02:12:08.590 --> 02:12:14.170
is too high, then what that person is
doing in one way or another is to try

02:12:14.170 --> 02:12:16.939
and exert an unhealthy level of control.

02:12:17.389 --> 02:12:19.880
And that can be done in
so many different ways.

02:12:19.880 --> 02:12:23.440
It can be done in that overt
way of just intimidating people.

02:12:24.020 --> 02:12:26.179
Of using harsh language towards people.

02:12:26.179 --> 02:12:27.840
It can be done by manipulating people.

02:12:27.980 --> 02:12:29.610
It can be done through passive aggression.

02:12:29.610 --> 02:12:33.549
There are all sorts of ways that the
person can try and exert unhealthy

02:12:33.549 --> 02:12:35.900
control, but that's where we end up.

02:12:36.160 --> 02:12:39.960
If there's too much expression
of the aggressive drive in us,

02:12:40.470 --> 02:12:41.210
Andrew Huberman: That makes sense.

02:12:41.210 --> 02:12:45.210
And it reminds me of an example from
my own life, where, first off, I

02:12:45.210 --> 02:12:50.590
should say I've had almost exclusively
positive collaborations among my

02:12:50.590 --> 02:12:52.309
colleagues at Stanford and elsewhere.

02:12:53.059 --> 02:12:56.019
Every one of those collaborations
has ended in a paper that

02:12:56.020 --> 02:12:57.040
we were all happy with.

02:12:57.050 --> 02:13:01.510
But more importantly, the relationships
grew and were not diminished.

02:13:03.020 --> 02:13:07.240
But I had one collaboration with
someone, not to be named, where it

02:13:07.240 --> 02:13:11.620
was going very well, but I had the
need to reschedule an appointment.

02:13:12.139 --> 02:13:15.820
So I sent ahead a note about the
fact that my car dealing with.

02:13:15.820 --> 02:13:16.700
I had some other things.

02:13:16.700 --> 02:13:21.429
I explained why I need to reschedule the
appointment and didn't receive a reply,

02:13:21.429 --> 02:13:25.030
which was a little unusual, but then
eventually received a reply that said,

02:13:26.790 --> 02:13:30.649
well, it's clear that you don't want to
pursue this collaboration, which is like

02:13:30.650 --> 02:13:32.730
the furthest thing from the truth, right?

02:13:35.010 --> 02:13:38.000
And so I expressed that, and then
the collaboration was reinstated.

02:13:38.540 --> 02:13:43.260
But it brought to mind some concern for
me because it was sort of an extreme

02:13:43.260 --> 02:13:48.559
reaction to something that happens among
academics or anyone we get busy with.

02:13:48.590 --> 02:13:49.219
Things come up.

02:13:49.270 --> 02:13:52.230
It was important to tend
to the car, that is.

02:13:52.800 --> 02:13:58.110
And then at some point later, they
were late to a number of meetings.

02:13:58.550 --> 02:13:59.730
Okay, no big deal.

02:13:59.790 --> 02:14:01.429
We're academics, we tend to run late.

02:14:01.490 --> 02:14:03.140
That's typical of many academics.

02:14:03.480 --> 02:14:07.920
But then I was late once to a meeting,
and they essentially left and wrote

02:14:07.920 --> 02:14:11.630
an email that said something of the
sort, like, I've got my own great

02:14:11.630 --> 02:14:14.250
ideas, so I'm no longer interested
in pursuing the collaboration.

02:14:15.059 --> 02:14:21.449
And I was pretty shocked, because there
was nothing really outside the ordinary

02:14:21.450 --> 02:14:26.170
in terms of busyness and prefaceorial
schedules, and there were other people

02:14:26.170 --> 02:14:28.080
involved, postdocs and things like that.

02:14:28.410 --> 02:14:31.530
And there was a great
project to be worked out.

02:14:32.010 --> 02:14:35.379
So I remember being disappointed,
but also really kind of surprised.

02:14:35.410 --> 02:14:39.150
But then when I mapped it back to the
earlier example of the car incident,

02:14:39.150 --> 02:14:44.470
I thought, well, there's a real sort
of lack of ability of this person to

02:14:44.470 --> 02:14:48.700
handle disappointment, and yet they're
exerting or demonstrating rather some

02:14:48.700 --> 02:14:52.790
of the same behavior, of occasionally
running tardy and these kinds of things.

02:14:53.890 --> 02:14:56.629
And I remember feeling like
it was pretty aggressive.

02:14:56.629 --> 02:15:01.179
It's a pretty aggressive reaction
to something that could have

02:15:01.180 --> 02:15:02.410
been handled with a conversation.

02:15:02.410 --> 02:15:05.440
Now, I must say I'm very grateful that
the collaboration didn't proceed and it

02:15:05.440 --> 02:15:08.179
went elsewhere and it worked out great,
and they're doing great and we're doing

02:15:08.179 --> 02:15:14.349
great, and so, no hard feelings, but
it stands out to me as a pretty salient

02:15:14.349 --> 02:15:19.070
example of aggression, but not played
out at the level of yelling or anything.

02:15:19.070 --> 02:15:21.470
There's a passivity in there,
but then there's also kind

02:15:21.470 --> 02:15:23.790
of a kind of entitlement.

02:15:24.110 --> 02:15:26.380
And here, of course, I'm only looking
at the other person's behavior.

02:15:26.380 --> 02:15:29.680
And I should acknowledge, I
realize canceling, not good.

02:15:29.690 --> 02:15:30.650
Being late, not good.

02:15:30.660 --> 02:15:32.669
But listen, I'm a human being, and I...

02:15:32.849 --> 02:15:33.810
Paul Conti: You canceled once.

02:15:33.810 --> 02:15:34.469
You were late once.

02:15:35.440 --> 02:15:36.379
This isn't habitual.

02:15:36.380 --> 02:15:38.059
This is human stuff.

02:15:38.070 --> 02:15:38.370
Right?

02:15:38.420 --> 02:15:38.700
Andrew Huberman: Right.

02:15:38.910 --> 02:15:43.269
And a lot of good work had gone
into the project, and there was a

02:15:43.280 --> 02:15:48.769
cost where, most importantly, the
postdoc suffered because they weren't

02:15:48.770 --> 02:15:50.300
involved in these interactions at all.

02:15:50.730 --> 02:15:52.870
And yet the project halted at that point.

02:15:54.180 --> 02:15:58.800
So to me, that seems like an
example of somebody who has a,

02:15:59.080 --> 02:16:01.230
well, strong, aggressive drive.

02:16:01.290 --> 02:16:04.220
And that's clear from that
they are incredibly successful

02:16:04.309 --> 02:16:06.019
in the academic domain.

02:16:07.559 --> 02:16:13.350
And when disappointed, lashes back
or is passive, one or the other.

02:16:13.350 --> 02:16:15.639
Is that what we're getting at here?

02:16:16.190 --> 02:16:19.440
Not surprisingly, perhaps, the person
rarely publishes with other people.

02:16:19.830 --> 02:16:20.080
Paul Conti: Right.

02:16:20.290 --> 02:16:22.660
Andrew Huberman: Probably that doesn't
make a very good collaborative partner.

02:16:22.710 --> 02:16:23.050
Paul Conti: Right.

02:16:23.520 --> 02:16:24.820
And it totally makes sense.

02:16:25.270 --> 02:16:30.680
Think about what you're describing here,
which is some vulnerability in the person.

02:16:30.690 --> 02:16:33.889
There's some way in which the person
doesn't feel good enough, no matter

02:16:33.889 --> 02:16:35.670
what this person has achieved.

02:16:36.150 --> 02:16:41.469
So then there's a sense of the
need and the right to over control.

02:16:41.670 --> 02:16:45.959
So when you agree to work together,
you didn't agree that I'll

02:16:45.959 --> 02:16:47.420
never have to cancel anything.

02:16:47.660 --> 02:16:48.059
Right?

02:16:48.109 --> 02:16:48.309
Sure.

02:16:48.540 --> 02:16:53.840
But the thought was different, the
framing is different on the other end,

02:16:53.840 --> 02:16:55.810
that now we're going to work together.

02:16:56.160 --> 02:16:58.630
So I'm exerting significant
control over you.

02:16:58.630 --> 02:17:00.049
And again, you're not aware of it.

02:17:00.789 --> 02:17:02.380
And maybe that he's not aware of it.

02:17:02.380 --> 02:17:03.530
Andrew Huberman: In
this case, it was a she.

02:17:03.900 --> 02:17:07.880
Paul Conti: Okay, so I was thinking of
someone different, but she has to have

02:17:07.880 --> 02:17:12.869
some deficit of self that results then
in the reflexive need to over control.

02:17:13.129 --> 02:17:16.539
And think about the first
response is a non response.

02:17:17.150 --> 02:17:18.649
Which is, that's aggression.

02:17:18.649 --> 02:17:20.280
But it's just passive aggression.

02:17:20.849 --> 02:17:23.589
The thought would be, well, you're
worried something doesn't feel good in you

02:17:23.590 --> 02:17:25.389
because I didn't respond, which was true..

02:17:25.600 --> 02:17:27.509
You're expecting a response,
maybe you don't know.

02:17:27.510 --> 02:17:28.590
Did you get the email?

02:17:29.110 --> 02:17:29.730
What's happening?

02:17:29.730 --> 02:17:30.400
Is she mad?

02:17:30.629 --> 02:17:31.820
So it's sort of effective.

02:17:31.820 --> 02:17:34.900
It creates some consternation
and some dissonance in you.

02:17:35.540 --> 02:17:39.659
Then on top of that, the person
is willing to potentially, at that

02:17:39.660 --> 02:17:41.259
point, sacrifice the relationship.

02:17:42.009 --> 02:17:44.449
So you think about
aggression now is not good.

02:17:45.000 --> 02:17:48.420
This excess aggression
is not good for you.

02:17:48.799 --> 02:17:52.209
It also is clearly eclipsing
the generative drive.

02:17:52.830 --> 02:17:55.180
Because it's not good for this
person and their research.

02:17:55.440 --> 02:17:59.059
It's not good for this person
and the postdocs in their lab.

02:17:59.619 --> 02:18:03.880
But the person is willing to
accept that in the service of

02:18:03.910 --> 02:18:06.140
gratifying the excess in aggression.

02:18:06.150 --> 02:18:09.510
Now, so then you said something
that then sort of made it okay.

02:18:09.780 --> 02:18:10.630
For the short term.

02:18:10.630 --> 02:18:10.920
Okay.

02:18:10.920 --> 02:18:13.709
Then the person feels gratified, like,
whether you apologize or not, they

02:18:13.709 --> 02:18:17.580
took it as you've, to some degree,
bowed down before me now, like, it'll

02:18:17.580 --> 02:18:19.719
be okay, at least for the short term.

02:18:20.490 --> 02:18:25.699
But then the next thing that happens
actually does end the collaboration.

02:18:26.270 --> 02:18:28.130
So that's not good.

02:18:28.590 --> 02:18:31.719
And you say, even from a self
serving perspective, that person was

02:18:31.719 --> 02:18:33.379
collaborating with you for a reason.

02:18:34.010 --> 02:18:38.390
She saw a benefit to the science
that she's very, very interested in

02:18:38.400 --> 02:18:40.580
through the collaboration with you.

02:18:40.780 --> 02:18:44.059
But then let that all go
in the service of what?

02:18:44.389 --> 02:18:46.910
In the service of the ego.

02:18:46.920 --> 02:18:49.550
Of, I don't feel good enough about myself.

02:18:50.030 --> 02:18:53.209
The response to that then,
is a response of envy.

02:18:53.420 --> 02:18:55.539
That I don't like that you
have the freedom to behave

02:18:55.540 --> 02:18:57.900
differently than I want you to.

02:18:58.129 --> 02:18:58.698
I don't like any of it.

02:18:58.698 --> 02:19:01.950
I don't like that I don't control
you as much as I would like to.

02:19:01.969 --> 02:19:05.820
And ultimately, it's that
envy that becomes destructive.

02:19:05.830 --> 02:19:08.240
So it's a setback for that person.

02:19:08.240 --> 02:19:10.630
It's destructive of the
science that person was doing.

02:19:10.630 --> 02:19:12.689
It's destructive of the
science that you were doing.

02:19:12.940 --> 02:19:15.330
So envy is destructive.

02:19:15.360 --> 02:19:20.209
And here, the high level of
aggression, the aggressive

02:19:20.209 --> 02:19:21.860
drive, is at a very high place.

02:19:21.870 --> 02:19:23.809
It's exceeding the generative drive.

02:19:24.009 --> 02:19:26.160
The pleasure drive isn't high enough
either, because there's not enough

02:19:26.160 --> 02:19:29.329
pleasure coming from the great
science that's being done, right?

02:19:29.580 --> 02:19:35.369
So then the person is approaching the
world through the lens of envy, right?

02:19:35.370 --> 02:19:36.380
They don't feel good enough.

02:19:36.380 --> 02:19:40.150
They want to exert that
aggression through over control.

02:19:40.150 --> 02:19:43.420
And what they end up doing
is destructive, right?

02:19:43.420 --> 02:19:44.620
And it's very clearly destructive.

02:19:44.620 --> 02:19:47.259
It's a great example because
it's destructive of the science,

02:19:47.510 --> 02:19:50.590
which is ostensibly the reason
that you're there, right?

02:19:50.800 --> 02:19:52.030
It's the reason you were there.

02:19:52.040 --> 02:19:55.510
But someone who needs to
exert over control is there,

02:19:55.520 --> 02:19:56.699
not just for that reason.

02:19:56.920 --> 02:20:01.630
And then the other reasons can trump the
generative reason that they're there.

02:20:01.630 --> 02:20:09.440
And that's how envy, when it is the
product of aggression or pleasure

02:20:09.440 --> 02:20:16.330
seeking, being too high, always
unfailingly creates destruction.

02:20:16.360 --> 02:20:23.890
And how different is that from
agency and gratitude as active verbs.

02:20:24.780 --> 02:20:28.750
There's a sense of agency, but the agency
isn't being exactly enacted, because if

02:20:28.750 --> 02:20:32.450
the agency is being enacted in the service
of science or career or whatever it may

02:20:32.450 --> 02:20:34.940
be, that's not going so well, right.

02:20:35.349 --> 02:20:37.850
And the gratitude part isn't active.

02:20:37.850 --> 02:20:40.180
Like, my goodness, I'm here,
I have this great career.

02:20:40.180 --> 02:20:41.199
I'm discovering things.

02:20:41.199 --> 02:20:42.739
I get to spend my life in science.

02:20:42.740 --> 02:20:43.920
I get to collaborate with you.

02:20:44.090 --> 02:20:45.640
There's so many things to feel good about.

02:20:45.830 --> 02:20:47.350
I have postdocs in my lab, right?

02:20:47.600 --> 02:20:50.180
I get to nurture them because
I know more and I can guide.

02:20:50.830 --> 02:20:51.909
That's not leading.

02:20:52.120 --> 02:20:58.100
Envy is not those things, which is why
people who are doing that, at least in

02:20:58.100 --> 02:21:03.320
this realm of life, although this often
this bleeds into other realms of life,

02:21:03.780 --> 02:21:10.040
the vast majority of times you see, is
someone who does not have happiness in

02:21:10.040 --> 02:21:11.769
the way that happiness with the quotes.

02:21:11.770 --> 02:21:18.690
That happiness is the sense of peace,
the sense of well being, right?

02:21:19.279 --> 02:21:21.250
Being able to delight
in things, contentment.

02:21:21.650 --> 02:21:23.070
The person doesn't have that.

02:21:23.680 --> 02:21:24.900
And here it's interesting, right?

02:21:24.900 --> 02:21:28.430
This person gets to the highest
levels of academia, and they're very

02:21:28.430 --> 02:21:31.980
successful, and they have a lab of
their own, and they're collaborating.

02:21:31.980 --> 02:21:33.840
You think that's all great, right?

02:21:34.350 --> 02:21:35.459
But not inside of them.

02:21:35.459 --> 02:21:40.099
It's not bringing them those things, as
evidenced by how this person is behaving.

02:21:40.100 --> 02:21:44.680
And I would bet almost 100% if you say,
what's that person like in other aspects

02:21:44.680 --> 02:21:47.460
of life, at least in the professional
realm, probably in others, too.

02:21:47.719 --> 02:21:49.470
No one's going to describe a happy person.

02:21:50.719 --> 02:21:52.710
Andrew Huberman: So much of
what you just said captures

02:21:52.710 --> 02:21:55.660
this individual extremely well.

02:21:56.160 --> 02:21:59.820
And it also reminds me that so much
of the way that you're describing this

02:21:59.820 --> 02:22:05.089
aggressive drive can also be observed,
perhaps in the way that people show up

02:22:05.090 --> 02:22:09.280
to social interactions, not necessarily
big interactions, maybe even just

02:22:09.280 --> 02:22:10.610
interactions between two people.

02:22:10.610 --> 02:22:15.080
What I'm thinking of here is the
person, male or female, who shows up

02:22:15.490 --> 02:22:20.430
and just kind of takes over, talks
the whole time and tells stories.

02:22:20.430 --> 02:22:24.399
I went to a meal when I graduated
university, and someone showed up

02:22:24.400 --> 02:22:28.419
for the first time at this meal,
meaning we had never met them before,

02:22:28.419 --> 02:22:31.570
and just sat down and just started
telling stories for like an hour.

02:22:32.220 --> 02:22:33.970
And it was interesting.

02:22:34.000 --> 02:22:35.440
Portions of it were captivating.

02:22:35.440 --> 02:22:38.789
And then at some point I realized
this is either total pathology.

02:22:38.790 --> 02:22:45.760
Like, this person is crazy, but they
weren't crazy, or they have no recognition

02:22:45.760 --> 02:22:49.990
that they're absorbing all the oxygen in
the room, as it's sometimes described.

02:22:49.990 --> 02:22:55.349
But it seemed like they had this need to
just control the whole environment by way

02:22:55.350 --> 02:22:59.400
of speech, just like fire hose stories.

02:23:00.380 --> 02:23:03.469
And I've seen this definitely
in the academic realm.

02:23:03.809 --> 02:23:07.080
I've seen this in the non academic
realm, in social settings.

02:23:07.930 --> 02:23:12.660
What's interesting is perhaps why this
person does this or these people do this.

02:23:12.660 --> 02:23:15.479
But what's also interesting
is how people react to it.

02:23:16.580 --> 02:23:21.599
On the one hand, I think most people find
that kind of obnoxious, but there also

02:23:21.599 --> 02:23:25.860
seem to be people who see this as like,
oh, that person has a lot of agency.

02:23:25.860 --> 02:23:27.019
Like they're a leader.

02:23:27.920 --> 02:23:30.920
They actually grab a lot of the
attention that they're seeking.

02:23:30.920 --> 02:23:33.840
And we tend to view those
people as kind of empowered.

02:23:34.460 --> 02:23:38.329
I don't actually think that they're
necessarily empowered, but perhaps it

02:23:38.330 --> 02:23:42.199
stems from the feeling that the rest
of us, I like to think, have, which

02:23:42.199 --> 02:23:45.780
is some sense of social etiquette,
where there's some give and take.

02:23:45.830 --> 02:23:49.939
You walk into a room, you kind of
assess what's the context here.

02:23:49.939 --> 02:23:53.400
There's some listening as well
as some speaking and so on.

02:23:54.420 --> 02:23:58.389
When someone shows up and kind of violates
all those rules, on the one hand, it can

02:23:58.389 --> 02:24:00.219
be obnoxious and overtake everything.

02:24:00.219 --> 02:24:06.070
But as it said before, there's also the
sense of like, oh, that must be nice

02:24:06.070 --> 02:24:09.169
to just be able to be as one feels.

02:24:09.819 --> 02:24:14.710
And so I'm describing this not because
I think people should mimic this type

02:24:14.710 --> 02:24:18.849
of behavior either way, be really
meek and not say anything that's

02:24:18.850 --> 02:24:22.790
on their mind or just overtake, but
because I feel like it might be an

02:24:22.790 --> 02:24:25.180
exploration of this aggressive drive.

02:24:25.870 --> 02:24:29.739
And if someone's doing that, are
they trying to mask something else?

02:24:30.440 --> 02:24:35.049
And why do people react to these
seemingly powerful people in this way?

02:24:35.230 --> 02:24:37.080
Paul Conti: These things happen
in the world around us, right?

02:24:37.080 --> 02:24:39.700
They're independent of the
spectrum of gender, the spectrum

02:24:39.700 --> 02:24:41.350
of intelligence, achievement.

02:24:41.780 --> 02:24:44.210
They're human problems.

02:24:44.380 --> 02:24:49.170
So a person you're describing,
whether that person has character

02:24:49.170 --> 02:24:53.669
structure, problems that are present
with them across time, or whether

02:24:53.670 --> 02:24:57.810
they're in a certain place, whether
it's in life or today, we don't know

02:24:57.810 --> 02:24:59.740
for sure what the underpinnings.

02:24:59.750 --> 02:25:04.350
But what you're describing is
it's a presentation of narcissism.

02:25:05.450 --> 02:25:10.569
And narcissism is rooted not in
confidence, not in arrogance.

02:25:11.080 --> 02:25:13.050
It's rooted in vulnerability.

02:25:13.060 --> 02:25:17.099
It's rooted in I don't feel good enough.

02:25:17.390 --> 02:25:22.420
And narcissism then engages with
the world through the lens of envy.

02:25:22.559 --> 02:25:24.949
So no one else gets to have any time.

02:25:25.309 --> 02:25:27.200
No one else gets to say anything funny.

02:25:27.540 --> 02:25:29.919
No one else maybe gets
to say anything at all.

02:25:31.010 --> 02:25:34.039
There's a dominance of the room, right?

02:25:34.039 --> 02:25:38.580
There's a dominance of the room
that comes through an inability to

02:25:38.580 --> 02:25:44.209
tolerate the back and forth of human
interactions, human engagement.

02:25:44.620 --> 02:25:47.499
So then that person becomes very dominant.

02:25:47.500 --> 02:25:48.390
And why is that?

02:25:48.390 --> 02:25:51.730
Because when they tell a story and they
get a laugh or even if it's not that funny

02:25:51.730 --> 02:25:55.189
and it's a 15 minute story, but somebody
smiles a little bit or nobody smiles.

02:25:55.580 --> 02:25:57.240
They can perceive inside that.

02:25:57.240 --> 02:25:59.230
Like, I just did that, I said that.

02:25:59.230 --> 02:26:00.770
And maybe somebody responded positively.

02:26:00.770 --> 02:26:02.949
I feel good about that for a split second.

02:26:02.950 --> 02:26:03.600
Now that's gone.

02:26:03.920 --> 02:26:08.470
And then the next thing comes, and
the next thing comes, because people

02:26:08.470 --> 02:26:12.120
who are coming at the world through
the lens of narcissism, whether it's

02:26:12.120 --> 02:26:19.209
just in that particular event or it's
across life, are never satisfied.

02:26:19.210 --> 02:26:22.710
And nothing ever brings enough
goodness, nothing ever brings

02:26:22.760 --> 02:26:25.660
enough feeling of pleasure.

02:26:25.820 --> 02:26:29.470
So the person then wants more, and
that's how the person dominates the room.

02:26:29.780 --> 02:26:31.750
Now that can be very seductive.

02:26:32.290 --> 02:26:36.979
Narcissistic people, not always, but
are often very seductive because of

02:26:36.980 --> 02:26:39.909
that appearance of mastery, of control.

02:26:40.139 --> 02:26:44.020
So that person did have, we could
look at it in the short term and say

02:26:44.360 --> 02:26:46.380
that person had mastery over the room.

02:26:47.029 --> 02:26:48.730
No one said anything for an hour but them.

02:26:49.390 --> 02:26:50.539
So they had mastery over the room.

02:26:50.539 --> 02:26:52.089
They had control over the room.

02:26:52.089 --> 02:26:54.709
But what they're doing
is exerting over control.

02:26:55.809 --> 02:26:58.090
It's like Pennywise and
Pound Foolish, right?

02:26:58.600 --> 02:27:01.029
That borrow a dollar today
to pay back 100 tomorrow.

02:27:01.510 --> 02:27:03.320
Because they got to control that room.

02:27:03.600 --> 02:27:07.730
But a lot of people, not everyone,
some people are seduced by it.

02:27:08.190 --> 02:27:10.950
But a lot of people will take away
from that something that's not a good

02:27:10.950 --> 02:27:14.630
feeling, something that wasn't mutual,
that doesn't make a person want to

02:27:14.639 --> 02:27:18.070
collaborate with that person, even
be in the same space as that person.

02:27:18.450 --> 02:27:22.360
So it's counterproductive
because the people who might come

02:27:22.360 --> 02:27:23.880
under the spell, so to speak.

02:27:24.420 --> 02:27:26.590
They're the people who were
brought under the spell, right.

02:27:26.599 --> 02:27:32.160
They're less observant, dynamic,
intuitive, introspective.

02:27:32.160 --> 02:27:35.570
They're not the people that you
want, in a sense, on your side.

02:27:36.029 --> 02:27:39.480
The people that would be most valuable
to collaborate with, even as thought

02:27:39.480 --> 02:27:43.010
partners have conversations with
those people are going to be put off

02:27:43.040 --> 02:27:46.839
because even if they don't know exactly
what's wrong, they know, like, that

02:27:46.840 --> 02:27:49.940
didn't feel good and they map, do I
want that feeling more in my life?

02:27:49.940 --> 02:27:50.420
No.

02:27:51.339 --> 02:27:53.850
So that's the counterproductive aspects.

02:27:53.949 --> 02:27:56.447
That's why narcissism is destructive,
because you might say, well,

02:27:56.520 --> 02:28:01.760
there's nothing destructive in that
interaction, but again, you have to

02:28:01.760 --> 02:28:05.300
be standing so up close to it that
you don't see the bigger picture.

02:28:05.300 --> 02:28:08.880
Because when you stand back from that,
that's not a person who's, by and

02:28:08.880 --> 02:28:11.929
large, you see that's not a person
who's interconnected in the world around

02:28:11.930 --> 02:28:15.830
them, has a group of good, supportive
friends, has a bunch of colleagues where

02:28:15.830 --> 02:28:19.390
they can sort of exchange information,
because all that social dynamic

02:28:19.390 --> 02:28:20.950
has to happen in the rest of life.

02:28:21.230 --> 02:28:27.039
So you're seeing a situation that is
counterproductive, that is destructive.

02:28:27.039 --> 02:28:31.420
And you always see that when people are
enacting narcissism, whether it's okay,

02:28:31.420 --> 02:28:34.140
a bunch of bad things have happened,
and for whatever reason, like, I'm in an

02:28:34.240 --> 02:28:37.660
unhealthy place and I'm enacting it right
now, or if I'm enacting it every day of

02:28:37.660 --> 02:28:41.150
my life, because it's in my character
structure and I haven't recognized and

02:28:41.150 --> 02:28:43.140
changed it, it's always destructive.

02:28:43.950 --> 02:28:47.359
Andrew Huberman: The narcissists that
I've known and observed almost always

02:28:47.359 --> 02:28:52.860
seem to have a partner who clearly
supports their narcissism, or at least

02:28:52.960 --> 02:28:57.450
doesn't speak up very much against
it, at least not publicly, and not

02:28:57.450 --> 02:28:59.800
much else except a professional role.

02:28:59.910 --> 02:29:04.070
In fact, there's one scientist who I did
not work with who comes to mind, and the

02:29:04.070 --> 02:29:09.700
joke about him was always that this person
would talk about themselves endlessly

02:29:09.970 --> 02:29:13.539
for the first half hour that you run into
them and say, okay, well, enough about me.

02:29:13.540 --> 02:29:14.730
Why don't you tell me about me?

02:29:16.410 --> 02:29:21.460
This person, moved to a different country
with their partner, comes back every

02:29:21.460 --> 02:29:26.670
once in a while, has essentially done
nothing over the last decade or so,

02:29:26.680 --> 02:29:31.600
kind of left the field, and it's kind of
secretly the laughingstock of the field.

02:29:31.950 --> 02:29:34.300
There was one other
anecdote about this person.

02:29:35.160 --> 02:29:36.070
I'm not picking on them.

02:29:36.070 --> 02:29:39.570
I'm just trying to explore these
dimensions of aggression and

02:29:39.580 --> 02:29:42.890
low pleasure drive and envy.

02:29:45.200 --> 02:29:48.930
At lab meetings, it was well known
that they would host a basketball game,

02:29:50.059 --> 02:29:58.150
but it was well known that you did not
want to score on this person because

02:29:58.199 --> 02:30:00.340
you would be asked to leave the lab.

02:30:00.340 --> 02:30:03.080
And Indeed, several people were
asked to leave the laboratory

02:30:03.620 --> 02:30:07.750
for having embarrassed the lab
head at one of these lab events.

02:30:07.940 --> 02:30:11.330
Paul Conti: By participating in
exactly the event that was described

02:30:11.330 --> 02:30:14.609
in the way it was described and
doing something competent, right?

02:30:14.610 --> 02:30:17.549
Andrew Huberman: So the game was
essentially a way for the person to

02:30:17.549 --> 02:30:21.930
build themselves up, and they were a
mediocre, at best, basketball player.

02:30:21.930 --> 02:30:26.190
So here's this game where everyone's
expected to pretend, right?

02:30:26.190 --> 02:30:29.109
And I have to pretend that the
person is actually better at

02:30:29.110 --> 02:30:30.190
what they do than they are.

02:30:31.049 --> 02:30:36.620
And in some ways, it feels like
a replica of how narcissism shows

02:30:36.630 --> 02:30:38.070
up in so many other areas of life.

02:30:38.070 --> 02:30:43.640
Like you said, these people are rarely
surrounded by people who are actually

02:30:43.680 --> 02:30:48.320
very bright, self effacing, et cetera.

02:30:50.059 --> 02:30:52.830
They tend to gather people that
just support them or no one at all.

02:30:53.010 --> 02:30:56.359
Because no reasonably healthy person
would choose to be around that.

02:30:57.420 --> 02:31:02.699
Paul Conti: Because that game is a
metaphor for all of life for that person.

02:31:02.720 --> 02:31:06.070
It's sending that message, like,
see this message and extrapolate

02:31:06.070 --> 02:31:07.180
it out to everything else.

02:31:09.150 --> 02:31:09.600
What's the metaphor?

02:31:09.700 --> 02:31:10.639
What's it communicating?

02:31:10.639 --> 02:31:16.490
It's communicating that you don't
do anything better than I do.

02:31:17.640 --> 02:31:20.340
You don't rise above me
interestingly, right?

02:31:20.340 --> 02:31:22.529
You don't arise above me in any way.

02:31:22.530 --> 02:31:24.129
You don't get to know things I don't know.

02:31:24.330 --> 02:31:31.150
You don't get to do anything better than
I do or I will be destructive towards you.

02:31:32.670 --> 02:31:34.780
It's fascinating because
it's not about the game.

02:31:34.780 --> 02:31:40.779
The game is a way of
communicating that message.

02:31:41.169 --> 02:31:41.289
Interesting.

02:31:41.289 --> 02:31:42.650
The person's not even
that good at the game.

02:31:42.660 --> 02:31:44.330
Like why not choose something
you're really good at.

02:31:44.870 --> 02:31:47.310
Because then the message is
not communicated as clearly.

02:31:47.310 --> 02:31:49.050
And a lot of this is unconscious.

02:31:49.300 --> 02:31:51.950
Let's choose something I'm kind
of fair to middling at, right?

02:31:51.950 --> 02:31:55.840
And then make it very clear that
no one gets to be better or I do

02:31:55.840 --> 02:31:57.110
something destructive to them.

02:31:57.559 --> 02:31:59.009
That's exactly what that is.

02:31:59.330 --> 02:32:01.820
And imagine someone is
thrown out of the lab.

02:32:01.820 --> 02:32:05.949
I mean, this is in many ways
the biggest thing in their life

02:32:05.949 --> 02:32:06.820
or one of the biggest things.

02:32:06.929 --> 02:32:08.210
Andrew Huberman: Antigenerative.

02:32:08.269 --> 02:32:13.190
I mean, the cost of that in the
larger world is one less potentially

02:32:13.200 --> 02:32:14.610
fantastic scientist, right?

02:32:14.610 --> 02:32:18.810
Paul Conti: And that's always the broader
picture because the narcissist is standing

02:32:18.870 --> 02:32:21.589
very, very close to the tapestry, right?

02:32:22.320 --> 02:32:27.950
So the interaction there is you have
squared a basket when I have not.

02:32:27.950 --> 02:32:32.150
So you don't understand the message
that you're not supposed to exceed me.

02:32:32.360 --> 02:32:35.159
And now I will get rid of you because
you're dangerous to have around.

02:32:35.559 --> 02:32:39.120
Because you don't get the message
and you may exceed me in other ways.

02:32:39.150 --> 02:32:42.669
And also I'm going to feel better
because I have the power to be punitive,

02:32:43.610 --> 02:32:46.080
even though it's wantonly punitive.

02:32:46.120 --> 02:32:47.560
And completely unjustified.

02:32:47.560 --> 02:32:51.820
But I have the power to do that and it'll
make me feel good than to push you away.

02:32:51.820 --> 02:32:54.369
And I know that's not going to be good
for you and I'll feel good about that.

02:32:55.070 --> 02:32:57.589
But that doesn't last, of course.

02:32:57.589 --> 02:32:59.239
That's why the person continues to do it.

02:32:59.500 --> 02:33:07.340
And it also doesn't understand at all
that that's not good for science or most

02:33:07.340 --> 02:33:09.560
importantly, that's not good for me.

02:33:10.510 --> 02:33:13.670
There's a graduate student in that
lab because you didn't say fire the

02:33:13.670 --> 02:33:17.259
graduate student, make the graduate
student leave if the person wasn't good.

02:33:17.520 --> 02:33:19.660
No, it's make the graduate
student leave no matter what.

02:33:19.660 --> 02:33:23.740
So the person is doing things that are
injurious to the society around us,

02:33:24.020 --> 02:33:28.170
obviously to the specific person they're
targeting, and also to themselves.

02:33:28.380 --> 02:33:34.290
And that's where, if you follow
envy and you see high levels of it

02:33:34.299 --> 02:33:36.129
in situations that are unbounded.

02:33:36.409 --> 02:33:39.780
It's like this situation is unbounded in
the sense that the person can do that.

02:33:39.780 --> 02:33:40.940
There's no higher authority.

02:33:42.580 --> 02:33:43.550
Andrew Huberman: This is changing.

02:33:43.900 --> 02:33:47.850
And by the way, I should back up a second
and say that I do believe, and it's

02:33:47.850 --> 02:33:50.410
been my experience, that most scientists
and lab heads are not narcissists,

02:33:51.690 --> 02:33:53.669
are quite kind, are benevolent.

02:33:53.680 --> 02:33:54.699
I mean, they'd be a little quirky.

02:33:54.700 --> 02:33:59.470
We're scientists, after all, but
not narcissists at the same time.

02:33:59.470 --> 02:34:04.199
It is true that for a long time,
less so now, laboratories were

02:34:04.199 --> 02:34:05.020
sort of like little fiefdoms.

02:34:05.920 --> 02:34:08.060
There was very little oversight
from the universities.

02:34:08.400 --> 02:34:12.200
And so the lab you joined became
your entire world and landscape.

02:34:12.780 --> 02:34:16.820
And there was some exploitation by
narcissistic lab heads for sure.

02:34:17.290 --> 02:34:20.490
As you said, it was unbounded, right?

02:34:20.500 --> 02:34:21.580
Like there was no oversight.

02:34:21.580 --> 02:34:26.840
Whereas this would be much harder to
recreate today if someone wanted to.

02:34:26.840 --> 02:34:30.280
Paul Conti: And I think that's why
by almost everyone listening to

02:34:30.280 --> 02:34:32.190
this, it will resonate with them.

02:34:32.190 --> 02:34:35.439
They'll find some familiarity,
because you see this, or you

02:34:35.450 --> 02:34:39.619
can see this in situations where
there's a bounded group of people.

02:34:39.619 --> 02:34:42.489
There's just a certain group of
people in a certain situation,

02:34:42.489 --> 02:34:43.529
and that's who they are.

02:34:43.849 --> 02:34:47.869
But the authority of the person
leading the group is unbounded.

02:34:48.100 --> 02:34:52.310
So there's a situation where if that
person has narcissistic tendencies,

02:34:52.310 --> 02:34:56.000
aggressive drive is too high, pleasure
drive is high, but not being met.

02:34:56.160 --> 02:35:00.449
If all of those things are happening,
that's when you see this come to light.

02:35:00.469 --> 02:35:06.220
Which is why the destruction varies
based upon the destruction that's

02:35:06.250 --> 02:35:08.800
permissible within the framework.

02:35:09.350 --> 02:35:13.150
So here that this person wasn't
going to fire everyone in their lab.

02:35:13.680 --> 02:35:16.650
So in a sense, they could
only damage their lab so much.

02:35:16.660 --> 02:35:19.589
Although maybe if you damage your
lab so much, you don't get funding,

02:35:19.590 --> 02:35:21.079
you inadvertently sink yourself.

02:35:22.090 --> 02:35:25.419
So even there, that person could
bring about their own destruction.

02:35:25.680 --> 02:35:30.140
But when you see the other end,
where it's truly unbounded in

02:35:30.210 --> 02:35:36.410
the sense of war, someone who can
control a machine of war, who then

02:35:36.690 --> 02:35:38.940
has everything, what do they need?

02:35:39.520 --> 02:35:42.249
Well, they need something they
don't have and never will get.

02:35:42.260 --> 02:35:46.400
So now they start enacting war,
and war is destructive, right?

02:35:46.400 --> 02:35:48.260
And you think, oh, that
person wants something.

02:35:48.420 --> 02:35:50.700
How many times does someone start a war?

02:35:52.090 --> 02:35:53.759
There's clearly an unjustified war, right?

02:35:53.759 --> 02:35:56.640
It's a war because they want
something, then they get

02:35:56.650 --> 02:35:57.929
something and they're satisfied.

02:35:58.779 --> 02:36:00.319
That's not how it goes, right?

02:36:00.530 --> 02:36:03.380
Then they get something and they're
not satisfied, and they want more.

02:36:03.380 --> 02:36:08.010
So in discussions at times about
narcissism and envy and how that

02:36:08.010 --> 02:36:10.419
can play out on the world stage.

02:36:10.650 --> 02:36:15.599
So sometimes huge events in human
history will come up and people, for

02:36:15.599 --> 02:36:20.110
example, will bring up Adolf Hitler
and the idea that Hitler wanted things.

02:36:20.120 --> 02:36:20.880
Wanted things.

02:36:21.570 --> 02:36:28.279
No, the unbound narcissism, the
unbound envy wanted destruction.

02:36:29.070 --> 02:36:33.449
This is a person who, if things
had continued to go as this person

02:36:33.450 --> 02:36:38.270
intended, there would have been no
one left on earth but him, because the

02:36:38.270 --> 02:36:44.110
process is nothing but destructive,
which is why, after the fact, there's

02:36:45.400 --> 02:36:47.349
incalculable human carnage, right?

02:36:47.349 --> 02:36:51.630
And he himself was among the
incalculable human carnage, because

02:36:51.830 --> 02:36:56.000
that's the endpoint of narcissism.

02:36:56.000 --> 02:36:58.440
Of narcissism on a broad stage.

02:36:58.550 --> 02:37:02.029
That's the endpoint of envy
at its highest magnitude.

02:37:02.030 --> 02:37:03.619
And we see that as examples.

02:37:03.620 --> 02:37:08.580
Whether we see on the smaller stage of
the lab head that you're describing or

02:37:08.580 --> 02:37:14.580
on the larger stage of unbounded war, we
end up with destruction, like 100% time.

02:37:14.590 --> 02:37:17.510
That's the final common
pathway for all of that.

02:37:18.080 --> 02:37:20.949
Andrew Huberman: Are there some
consistent themes of childhood that

02:37:20.970 --> 02:37:22.679
lead somebody to become a narcissist?

02:37:23.840 --> 02:37:28.740
And in addition to that, I'm curious
whether or not narcissists ever have

02:37:29.369 --> 02:37:34.210
insight, whether or not, if offered the
opportunity to explore the ten cupboards

02:37:34.260 --> 02:37:39.120
under the structure of self and function
of self, whether or not they eventually

02:37:39.559 --> 02:37:44.040
see inside those cupboards and go,
oh, my goodness, I've got this self

02:37:44.070 --> 02:37:47.489
that's clearly overinflated, and I've
got these defence mechanisms, and I'm

02:37:47.490 --> 02:37:52.220
so envious and modify their behavior,
or whether or not the narcissists are

02:37:52.220 --> 02:37:55.780
immune from constructive self reflection.

02:37:55.790 --> 02:38:01.819
Paul Conti: To answer the first part
is the vast majority of narcissism.

02:38:01.820 --> 02:38:06.330
It may be all of it, we don't know for
sure, is rooted in the childhood trauma

02:38:06.360 --> 02:38:13.070
of not feeling good enough, which is not
an excuse for people doing awful things.

02:38:13.070 --> 02:38:14.119
That's not what we're saying.

02:38:14.120 --> 02:38:16.140
We're trying to have an
explanatory mechanism, right?

02:38:16.480 --> 02:38:21.240
Which goes back to formative life
experiences and not feeling good enough,

02:38:21.500 --> 02:38:25.640
whether it was because that person was
directly denigrated, or that person

02:38:25.640 --> 02:38:29.430
wasn't denigrated, but could never
work hard enough, never could be enough

02:38:29.770 --> 02:38:35.739
to get approval right again, it's not
100%, and human beings are complicated,

02:38:35.960 --> 02:38:39.609
but if you go and look, you see that,
that there was never a state of like,

02:38:39.610 --> 02:38:41.070
oh, I feel good enough about myself.

02:38:41.070 --> 02:38:44.880
And if there's never a state of, I feel
good enough about myself because someone

02:38:44.880 --> 02:38:48.140
has told me that and given me the pat
on the head or given me the positive

02:38:48.140 --> 02:38:52.369
comment, you can see how in a certain sort
of natural lay of the land, genetically

02:38:52.370 --> 02:38:56.710
and in concert with other experiences,
that person can get to adulthood

02:38:56.990 --> 02:38:59.470
with a lot of aggression in them.

02:38:59.690 --> 02:39:02.350
And never having experienced
I'm good enough, it's still

02:39:02.350 --> 02:39:03.800
running along inside of them.

02:39:04.050 --> 02:39:07.170
And then they're enacting that
aggression in the world around them.

02:39:07.170 --> 02:39:09.839
That's most commonly what we see.

02:39:10.080 --> 02:39:16.509
And because there's such deep
vulnerability and such deep insecurity,

02:39:16.789 --> 02:39:20.810
then people say, people who suffer from
full blown narcissism, narcissistic

02:39:20.810 --> 02:39:26.610
personality disorder, so inaction of
envy on the highest levels, that is,

02:39:27.420 --> 02:39:33.539
they're so defended, they're so strongly
defended in an unhealthy manner from

02:39:33.559 --> 02:39:39.120
seeing their own vulnerability that it
is extremely difficult to get that person

02:39:39.120 --> 02:39:44.550
to come around and say, okay, let's look
in those ten cupboards within the field.

02:39:44.550 --> 02:39:48.410
People often talk about
treating narcissistic people.

02:39:48.570 --> 02:39:51.429
They talk about it in a nihilistic way.

02:39:51.969 --> 02:39:55.740
And some, you'll see, very experienced
people say, oh, that's impossible, right?

02:39:55.740 --> 02:39:56.590
That never gets better.

02:39:56.590 --> 02:39:59.170
Now, I'm not a believer
in therapeutic nihilism.

02:40:00.070 --> 02:40:06.939
I think that, yes, it is the norm
that that person just can't get it

02:40:07.080 --> 02:40:08.980
together to go look at that thing.

02:40:08.990 --> 02:40:11.580
They're so defended against
it, they're so afraid of it.

02:40:12.129 --> 02:40:13.450
They won't look anywhere near it.

02:40:13.450 --> 02:40:16.570
So they're looking in the other direction
and they're furthering all that unhealth.

02:40:16.910 --> 02:40:19.470
It's not the case that
it's always that way.

02:40:19.840 --> 02:40:24.100
And on a couple of occasions I
have worked with, seen, witnessed

02:40:24.100 --> 02:40:27.200
narcissistic people who can make changes.

02:40:27.480 --> 02:40:30.370
Now, it's usually in the context
of something very extreme

02:40:30.370 --> 02:40:32.109
that causes them to do that.

02:40:32.110 --> 02:40:36.730
So someone who will no longer have
access to family members they want to

02:40:36.730 --> 02:40:42.759
see or to financial resources that they
need to keep themselves afloat, it's

02:40:42.770 --> 02:40:44.279
things that often are that dramatic.

02:40:44.280 --> 02:40:45.300
It's not always that.

02:40:45.690 --> 02:40:50.070
But we can see, though, in those kind
of extenuating situations where the

02:40:50.350 --> 02:40:53.320
problem is so big, the envy is so high.

02:40:54.190 --> 02:40:57.500
But the motivation for change is
very, very high, because since on

02:40:57.500 --> 02:41:01.050
the carrot and stick model, the stick
here is very, very strong, that if

02:41:01.050 --> 02:41:05.539
a person then goes and does that,
you can see change inside of them.

02:41:05.539 --> 02:41:12.130
So we're never in a place of therapeutic
nihilism, but the barriers to that

02:41:12.140 --> 02:41:17.460
are very, very high because the self
is so wounded that the person is

02:41:17.490 --> 02:41:19.530
protecting that self so strongly.

02:41:19.530 --> 02:41:22.509
That's why the narcissism
and envy are so full blown.

02:41:22.809 --> 02:41:25.020
And it's hard to get that
person to go back and look.

02:41:25.300 --> 02:41:26.480
But not impossible.

02:41:26.650 --> 02:41:30.100
Andrew Huberman: Based on what you're
telling me, it seems that it's a

02:41:30.100 --> 02:41:37.730
very low probability that a non
clinician could change a narcissist.

02:41:37.740 --> 02:41:43.270
In other words, if one is engaging in
the world with a narcissist because

02:41:43.270 --> 02:41:46.220
they have to, presumably, or they
just find themselves in that place,

02:41:47.530 --> 02:41:50.880
would you say to that person, there's
very little, if anything, that you

02:41:50.880 --> 02:41:56.079
can do to change the narcissist's
behavior or psychological framework?

02:41:57.009 --> 02:42:01.350
Because, of course, if the narcissist
can't often do it for themselves with the

02:42:01.350 --> 02:42:06.439
help of a skilled clinician, why would
anyone else be able to achieve that?

02:42:06.700 --> 02:42:09.910
Paul Conti: We're coming at what
we're doing here from a perspective

02:42:09.940 --> 02:42:12.380
of truth about human beings, right?

02:42:12.389 --> 02:42:15.179
And that truth brings with it hopefulness.

02:42:15.750 --> 02:42:19.330
It brings with it hopefulness that people
can change and how people can change.

02:42:19.480 --> 02:42:23.500
And I am 100% all for that.

02:42:23.510 --> 02:42:25.480
It's the way to look at ourselves.

02:42:25.940 --> 02:42:32.620
But it is also true that there are
aspects of pathology that require

02:42:32.650 --> 02:42:35.180
clinical treatment in order to improve.

02:42:35.190 --> 02:42:35.210
S

02:42:35.210 --> 02:42:38.100
o now we're looking from the other
side and saying, hey, there's a

02:42:38.110 --> 02:42:42.120
problem here and there's a deep
problem here, and that we have to

02:42:42.120 --> 02:42:45.520
come at from a different perspective
of how can you help that problem?

02:42:45.730 --> 02:42:48.580
And there's a science behind this,
too, of what level of clinical

02:42:48.580 --> 02:42:52.810
care, for example, is most likely
to be helpful to someone like this?

02:42:52.990 --> 02:42:55.460
And it's not an individual clinician even.

02:42:55.710 --> 02:42:59.086
It's a team of people who work
through different modalities who

02:42:59.330 --> 02:43:01.410
can sort of wrap around that person.

02:43:01.410 --> 02:43:06.009
So it's not just a level of clinical
care is needed, but it's a relatively

02:43:06.009 --> 02:43:07.550
high level of clinical care.

02:43:07.770 --> 02:43:11.410
And that, in general, is the only
way that we get at narcissism.

02:43:11.410 --> 02:43:14.840
That's not 100%, but that's
the vast majority of time.

02:43:15.090 --> 02:43:16.860
So what can then, the person do?

02:43:16.870 --> 02:43:17.100
Andrew Huberman: Right.

02:43:17.100 --> 02:43:19.590
Paul Conti: A person cannot be
a team of clinicians, right.

02:43:19.880 --> 02:43:22.289
What that person can do,
one choice is to disengage.

02:43:23.340 --> 02:43:27.519
But disengagement can come with
the promise of reengagement.

02:43:28.469 --> 02:43:32.460
Many, many times I've worked with
people and practiced and rehearsed with

02:43:32.460 --> 02:43:37.150
them, like, okay, what might you say
to someone along the lines of, I've

02:43:37.150 --> 02:43:40.510
known you for a long time, or, I care
about you, or, I love you, whatever

02:43:40.510 --> 02:43:45.070
they may say to lead in, but I can't
be with you, or I can't be around you.

02:43:46.029 --> 02:43:48.950
There's something going on
that makes it not okay for me.

02:43:48.960 --> 02:43:50.543
It doesn't feel okay in person.

02:43:50.750 --> 02:43:54.010
Maybe says things like, you're aggressive
or demeaning or whatever it is.

02:43:54.010 --> 02:43:56.479
Or maybe they just say, it just
doesn't feel okay, I can't have it.

02:43:56.780 --> 02:44:00.579
And then the need to step
away from the person.

02:44:00.580 --> 02:44:04.900
But, look, if you got some help, right,
if you took better care of yourself

02:44:04.900 --> 02:44:09.500
in ways that would be better for you
and for the people around you, then

02:44:09.500 --> 02:44:10.910
of course I'd want to be in your life.

02:44:11.310 --> 02:44:12.100
Something like that.

02:44:12.100 --> 02:44:19.069
So disengagement can come with that
encouragement, right, to the person.

02:44:19.509 --> 02:44:22.819
But one way or another, you have to
set boundaries, which is okay, I have

02:44:22.820 --> 02:44:26.410
to deal with this person, so I'll deal
with them a little bit, or I don't have

02:44:26.460 --> 02:44:28.140
to deal with this person, so I won't.

02:44:28.630 --> 02:44:31.449
Or I can't get away from this person,
so I have to take with a grain of

02:44:31.450 --> 02:44:33.479
salt what they're saying to me.

02:44:33.610 --> 02:44:38.290
But ultimately, some form of
strong boundaries or disengagement

02:44:38.570 --> 02:44:40.460
is like, that's the response.

02:44:42.160 --> 02:44:43.430
That's the self care response.

02:44:43.640 --> 02:44:46.240
For the person who's with the narcissist.

02:44:46.800 --> 02:44:48.920
Andrew Huberman: What are some
other ways that the aggressive drive

02:44:48.950 --> 02:44:53.080
and pleasure drive and generative
drive, for that matter, play out?

02:44:53.240 --> 02:44:57.419
For instance, we talked about the former
patient of yours who eventually switched

02:44:57.429 --> 02:45:02.250
jobs, clearly had a generative drive
within him, but it was being blocked by

02:45:02.250 --> 02:45:06.930
a number of choices rooted in narratives
that originated in childhood, et cetera.

02:45:07.470 --> 02:45:10.789
We talked about individuals with
high aggressive drive, high degree of

02:45:10.790 --> 02:45:14.740
pleasure drive, but a very diminished
capacity to experience pleasure

02:45:14.790 --> 02:45:18.070
and therefore a lot of envy and the
destruction that comes with envy.

02:45:18.250 --> 02:45:18.600
Paul Conti: Yes.

02:45:19.200 --> 02:45:22.730
Andrew Huberman: What are some of the
other variations on these drives as you

02:45:22.870 --> 02:45:24.480
observe them in your clinical practice?

02:45:25.360 --> 02:45:29.840
Paul Conti: Well, our overall framing
is we want the generative drive to be

02:45:29.840 --> 02:45:34.289
the one that's deterministic . It's
the one with the strongest influence.

02:45:34.299 --> 02:45:38.690
So we want to nurture the generative
drive in us and in others, and it

02:45:38.690 --> 02:45:40.730
makes sense for us to talk about that.

02:45:41.250 --> 02:45:44.120
But we've looked at how do
things get out of balance.

02:45:44.660 --> 02:45:48.060
And from the perspective of,
well, what if the aggressive

02:45:48.060 --> 02:45:50.119
drive or the pleasure drive.

02:45:50.210 --> 02:45:51.490
What if they're too high?

02:45:51.799 --> 02:45:52.069
Right?

02:45:52.289 --> 02:45:57.480
And then it makes sense that often, not
always, what can be driving them to be so

02:45:57.480 --> 02:45:59.420
high are things that aren't healthy in us.

02:45:59.810 --> 02:46:03.499
Then the higher they get, the
harder it is to gratify them.

02:46:03.800 --> 02:46:06.560
So we end up with that problem of envy.

02:46:07.309 --> 02:46:13.020
But we can be out of balance in the
other direction too, where the person

02:46:13.020 --> 02:46:19.550
does not experience an ability to
engage with the world around them.

02:46:20.290 --> 02:46:23.619
They don't think they can do anything
to change anything for the better,

02:46:23.639 --> 02:46:25.200
inside or outside of themselves.

02:46:25.200 --> 02:46:26.669
And they're not doing much.

02:46:26.669 --> 02:46:32.480
They don't feel that they can do much and
also not receiving pleasure from things.

02:46:32.480 --> 02:46:34.870
There's no gratification from
the things a person is doing.

02:46:34.870 --> 02:46:38.910
Like, we see situations like this
too, with the aggressive drive,

02:46:38.910 --> 02:46:40.840
the pleasure drive, or both.

02:46:41.090 --> 02:46:46.609
And then we end up not at envy, because
envy is the side of excess, but we end

02:46:46.619 --> 02:46:49.630
up at demoralization on the lower side.

02:46:49.660 --> 02:46:54.170
Now, demoralization is not a
specific psychiatric diagnosis.

02:46:54.170 --> 02:46:58.310
It can predispose to psychiatric
problems like the biochemical

02:46:58.310 --> 02:46:59.810
abnormality of depression.

02:47:00.590 --> 02:47:05.349
But what we're talking about here is
not a psychiatric diagnosis, right?

02:47:05.359 --> 02:47:07.280
Like envy is not a psychiatric diagnosis.

02:47:07.280 --> 02:47:11.410
It's a thing that can be experienced,
that can lead to diagnoses.

02:47:11.590 --> 02:47:13.539
The same thing with demoralization.

02:47:13.680 --> 02:47:18.979
If you don't feel that you can make
a difference to anything and you're

02:47:18.980 --> 02:47:23.079
not enjoying anything or feeling
gratification from anything, then

02:47:23.090 --> 02:47:24.750
that pool is going to win out.

02:47:24.760 --> 02:47:27.080
That's going to be a demoralized person.

02:47:27.090 --> 02:47:32.339
The same way, of course, we know in
experiments, when you have a rat going

02:47:32.340 --> 02:47:35.560
for food, if you do it enough, when
the rat goes for the food and you take

02:47:35.560 --> 02:47:37.959
the food away, the rat stops trying.

02:47:37.980 --> 02:47:40.360
Andrew Huberman: And they
learned helplessness, right?

02:47:40.379 --> 02:47:42.510
Paul Conti: That exists in us too.

02:47:42.510 --> 02:47:44.630
And it comes along with
all sorts of other things.

02:47:44.640 --> 02:47:48.830
Because being not rats, we have
a whole bunch of thoughts about

02:47:48.840 --> 02:47:51.439
that, of, oh my God, I'm not good
enough and nothing will ever be.

02:47:51.440 --> 02:47:52.070
Okay.

02:47:52.800 --> 02:48:01.030
So demoralization then can be very strong
in taking a person away from the other

02:48:01.030 --> 02:48:05.689
things we're trying to seek, either
because that person has essentially the

02:48:05.690 --> 02:48:09.715
learned helplessness and all the things,
the complicated things inside of us that

02:48:09.770 --> 02:48:14.430
can come along with that, or the person
isn't gaining pleasure from anything.

02:48:14.730 --> 02:48:18.940
So when we're considering the ways
in which we can be out of balance, we

02:48:18.940 --> 02:48:24.539
think, okay, aggression and pleasure
drive, if one or the other or both

02:48:24.840 --> 02:48:28.470
is too high, we end up at envy.

02:48:28.940 --> 02:48:35.040
And if one or the other or both are
too low, we end up with demoralization.

02:48:35.320 --> 02:48:37.939
And you can take almost any scenario.

02:48:38.089 --> 02:48:40.220
It could be a scenario of
something that's just not really

02:48:40.220 --> 02:48:41.560
not going well for a person.

02:48:41.560 --> 02:48:44.810
It's not a clinical scenario,
it's a thing in a person's life.

02:48:45.160 --> 02:48:50.579
Or we can take clinical scenarios
and the vast majority outside of

02:48:50.649 --> 02:48:54.890
outliers, like a head injury, for
example, we can take those scenarios

02:48:54.920 --> 02:48:59.529
and we can look at it in that way,
and we can understand what's going on.

02:48:59.540 --> 02:49:03.820
At least we can understand enough
that when we go back and look in the

02:49:04.370 --> 02:49:08.539
ten cupboards of the two pillars,
we can then have some understanding

02:49:08.540 --> 02:49:10.170
of, okay, what is going on.

02:49:10.170 --> 02:49:15.430
We know the basic picture and how things
are not in the balance we want them in

02:49:15.670 --> 02:49:19.970
now, we can understand that enough to go
back and then look in those ten cupboards.

02:49:19.970 --> 02:49:24.970
And I believe that just about everything
except those biological outliers,

02:49:25.559 --> 02:49:30.099
like a head injury, fits into that
heuristic, which is why we can use it

02:49:30.109 --> 02:49:32.510
to understand, we can use it to help.

02:49:32.530 --> 02:49:33.830
We can use it to make change.

02:49:34.810 --> 02:49:38.760
Andrew Huberman: What a powerful lens
to think about and explore the self and

02:49:39.110 --> 02:49:44.009
where things are working for us and where
things are possibly not working for us.

02:49:45.110 --> 02:49:50.240
If I or anyone else out there wanted
to get some read on, assess their

02:49:50.240 --> 02:49:53.930
level of aggressive drive and their
level of pleasure drive and their

02:49:53.930 --> 02:49:57.930
ability to experience pleasure, what
sorts of questions would one ask?

02:49:59.450 --> 02:50:04.830
For instance, is it a
question of how driven am I?

02:50:05.029 --> 02:50:06.869
How much get up and go do I have?

02:50:07.540 --> 02:50:11.410
How much pleasure do I experience
from an interaction with a

02:50:11.410 --> 02:50:13.840
puppy, an interaction with food?

02:50:14.190 --> 02:50:15.120
Is it too much?

02:50:15.550 --> 02:50:16.789
Does it draw me off course?

02:50:16.800 --> 02:50:20.350
Are those the sorts of very
simple, but perhaps also very

02:50:20.350 --> 02:50:24.300
informative questions that we could
start to use to probe our psyche?

02:50:24.920 --> 02:50:28.130
Paul Conti: Yeah, I think yes,
but I would come top down.

02:50:28.770 --> 02:50:33.730
So if the goal of health is that
aggression and pleasure, those

02:50:33.730 --> 02:50:38.010
drives are subserving the generative
drive, then start to look there.

02:50:38.250 --> 02:50:43.670
If a person can take an honest inventory
of self, like, what kind of force

02:50:43.700 --> 02:50:46.470
am I being in the world around me?

02:50:46.900 --> 02:50:51.800
And that could mean, for example, what
kind of force am I being in my family?

02:50:52.830 --> 02:50:55.079
Am I denigrating to the people around me?

02:50:55.420 --> 02:50:57.880
Are the other people in
the home afraid of me?

02:50:58.039 --> 02:50:59.980
What kind of force am I
being a force for good?

02:50:59.980 --> 02:51:00.910
Am I bolstering?

02:51:01.770 --> 02:51:06.570
People can't always see that in
themselves and take stock of themselves.

02:51:06.570 --> 02:51:08.740
But what we're talking about is
situation where we think a person

02:51:08.740 --> 02:51:10.269
can like they can bring to bear.

02:51:10.520 --> 02:51:12.820
Who am I being in the world in other ways?

02:51:13.490 --> 02:51:16.560
Think of the example of the person
who needed to leave the job.

02:51:16.570 --> 02:51:19.864
Who could look at that and say,
no, I'm not being generative in

02:51:19.864 --> 02:51:20.411
the world in the way I want to.

02:51:20.480 --> 02:51:26.760
I'm certainly not doing my job
as well as I would want to.

02:51:26.760 --> 02:51:28.320
I'm making my own life worse.

02:51:29.179 --> 02:51:32.700
So that person could then see that's out
of balance or in another way, a person

02:51:32.700 --> 02:51:39.119
might see a lot of what I'm doing is sort
of self serving or maybe destructive.

02:51:39.430 --> 02:51:41.190
People can realize that, right?

02:51:41.270 --> 02:51:46.109
So you can realize by taking an
inventory of self, is the generative

02:51:46.120 --> 02:51:48.219
drive what's deterministic in me?

02:51:48.219 --> 02:51:51.360
And again, not always, but we're
talking about a process of exploration.

02:51:51.670 --> 02:51:55.100
If the answer to that is yes, if you
say, I'm trying to be the best person

02:51:55.100 --> 02:51:58.930
that I can, and I think about the
people over whom I have any authority.

02:51:59.440 --> 02:52:03.140
And I try to be reasonable, and I try to
be fair, and I try and be circumspect, and

02:52:03.770 --> 02:52:05.630
I try and think in someone else's shoes.

02:52:06.400 --> 02:52:10.289
Sometimes I have to set boundaries or
expectations or even punishment, right?

02:52:10.289 --> 02:52:12.600
But I'm careful about how I'm doing that.

02:52:12.609 --> 02:52:16.730
And I'm certainly not perfect, and I
get things wrong at times, but I do

02:52:16.760 --> 02:52:18.230
think I'm contributing to the world.

02:52:18.230 --> 02:52:21.779
I'm doing whatever I take
on as well as I can do it.

02:52:21.780 --> 02:52:23.080
I'm productive at work.

02:52:24.090 --> 02:52:26.990
My kids are doing okay or my
friends are doing all right.

02:52:27.110 --> 02:52:32.020
Whatever it is that if we can come
up with that, then we can say,

02:52:32.020 --> 02:52:35.220
okay, exhale a little bit, like
you're in a good place, right.

02:52:35.220 --> 02:52:36.960
It doesn't mean everything
is optimal, of course.

02:52:36.970 --> 02:52:40.070
So then go look at the level
of the aggressive drive, which

02:52:40.070 --> 02:52:42.059
might mean how assertive am I?

02:52:42.080 --> 02:52:42.350
Right?

02:52:42.509 --> 02:52:45.100
Am I the kind of person who
comes up to the precipice but

02:52:45.100 --> 02:52:46.570
doesn't make the decision?

02:52:46.820 --> 02:52:49.180
Am I the kind of person
who's a little too assertive?

02:52:49.180 --> 02:52:51.380
And sometimes I'm sort of
walking on people a little bit.

02:52:51.620 --> 02:52:54.180
Like a person can go look at
the aggressive drive within

02:52:54.180 --> 02:52:55.620
them or pleasure seeking.

02:52:55.620 --> 02:52:58.260
Am I doing things that
bring me gratification?

02:52:58.690 --> 02:53:03.380
Am I engaging with the people around me
in a way that brings the gratification

02:53:03.630 --> 02:53:05.909
that one might wish for, right?

02:53:05.910 --> 02:53:08.279
So if it's in a romantic
relationship, is there romance?

02:53:08.280 --> 02:53:09.640
Like, are we being nice to one another?

02:53:10.340 --> 02:53:13.240
So you can go and look at that and
say, am I getting gratification

02:53:13.240 --> 02:53:14.340
from the things I'm doing?

02:53:14.870 --> 02:53:21.150
Am I taking wherever this drive
is within me and trying to satisfy

02:53:21.150 --> 02:53:23.780
it in reasonable, healthy ways
that are also good for others?

02:53:23.850 --> 02:53:25.320
And we're back to the generative drive.

02:53:25.620 --> 02:53:28.970
So that's one way of coming at it, and
it's the way that we would like to,

02:53:28.970 --> 02:53:32.190
because now what are we trying to do next?

02:53:32.190 --> 02:53:33.409
And what can we make things better?

02:53:33.409 --> 02:53:34.820
Can we optimize things?

02:53:34.820 --> 02:53:37.289
Okay, things are okay, but
can we make them better?

02:53:37.710 --> 02:53:43.410
But let's say we see that the generative
drive is not winning the day, right?

02:53:43.590 --> 02:53:44.810
And people can see that.

02:53:45.090 --> 02:53:47.320
Look, I'm seeking pleasure, right?

02:53:47.320 --> 02:53:49.980
It's why I got, for example, I
hear over and over, that's why I

02:53:49.980 --> 02:53:51.840
got addicted to this substance.

02:53:51.840 --> 02:53:55.340
It now is not providing any pleasure
to me and is now making me miserable.

02:53:55.719 --> 02:53:58.120
But I wanted what it was giving me.

02:53:58.359 --> 02:54:02.780
Again, this doesn't mean that the person
just wants to have the world's best time.

02:54:02.780 --> 02:54:03.030
Right?

02:54:03.190 --> 02:54:05.350
It may mean that they're
really suffering a lot.

02:54:05.950 --> 02:54:10.279
And the pleasure that that drug
gave them was some relief from pain.

02:54:10.660 --> 02:54:15.659
This is how many, many people
tragically ended up becoming addicted

02:54:15.660 --> 02:54:18.820
to and dying from opiates, right?

02:54:18.820 --> 02:54:21.890
Because, say, the opiate after the
surgery or the opiate after the

02:54:21.890 --> 02:54:24.609
injury, then is soothing something.

02:54:25.060 --> 02:54:27.719
And it's soothing something
because that person feels less bad

02:54:27.719 --> 02:54:29.099
about something inside of them.

02:54:29.340 --> 02:54:31.229
You hear this all the time.

02:54:31.240 --> 02:54:31.520
That.

02:54:31.530 --> 02:54:33.099
That then fosters addiction.

02:54:33.099 --> 02:54:37.650
So that person looking for pleasure,
this isn't something where we would

02:54:37.650 --> 02:54:40.699
say in some light hearted manner, that
person took chances with their life.

02:54:40.700 --> 02:54:42.119
I mean, sometimes we'll see that.

02:54:42.650 --> 02:54:45.969
But more and more, what people are looking
for then is relief from suffering, right?

02:54:46.179 --> 02:54:50.560
But we can get to that point where we
can ascertain, for whatever reason,

02:54:50.560 --> 02:54:53.060
that the pleasure seeking is too much.

02:54:53.090 --> 02:54:56.670
And if pleasure seeking and
aggression are too much, we

02:54:56.680 --> 02:54:58.490
become aware of dissatisfaction.

02:54:58.940 --> 02:55:01.929
If you're relying too much on
aggression, I always want my way.

02:55:01.929 --> 02:55:03.510
It's not always going to happen.

02:55:03.990 --> 02:55:05.720
Or I always want that pleasurable thing.

02:55:05.720 --> 02:55:07.160
I always want to feel better.

02:55:07.520 --> 02:55:08.979
That also doesn't happen.

02:55:08.980 --> 02:55:09.180
Right?

02:55:09.710 --> 02:55:13.470
So then that can guide us towards
being aware of where are those drives?

02:55:13.470 --> 02:55:19.340
And if the drives are high, how
much dissonance is created by

02:55:20.370 --> 02:55:24.789
what's actually coming of the drive
versus the level the drive is at.

02:55:25.700 --> 02:55:29.479
I guess it's a long way of saying yes to
your question, but I would sort of come

02:55:29.480 --> 02:55:35.180
top down because the generative drive is
so important and it does gate forward.

02:55:35.180 --> 02:55:40.480
Like, kind of, where are we at in
the spectrum of how healthy am I?

02:55:40.500 --> 02:55:45.489
Or are there elements of unhealth I want
to kind of go after, or am I seeing things

02:55:45.490 --> 02:55:50.010
in myself that really say things that
are unhealthy are really dominating my

02:55:50.400 --> 02:55:52.490
life are deterministic, like addiction.

02:55:53.440 --> 02:55:54.410
Just one example.

02:55:54.410 --> 02:55:54.730
Addiction.

02:55:55.050 --> 02:55:56.310
Things that are self destructive.

02:55:56.760 --> 02:56:00.840
Because then that's a place to then look
at it more through the clinical lens.

02:56:00.840 --> 02:56:05.034
And maybe I won't just talk to a
trusted other and go get a book, but

02:56:05.100 --> 02:56:08.870
maybe I should have clinical care.

02:56:09.450 --> 02:56:13.710
Andrew Huberman: Yeah the example of
addiction is very potent, and it also

02:56:13.710 --> 02:56:20.680
brings to mind the perhaps less apparently
dangerous situation, but one that I

02:56:20.690 --> 02:56:24.140
think is really common, where people
have a certain amount of aggressive

02:56:24.140 --> 02:56:29.250
drive, they have a certain amount of
pleasure drive, but there's a kind

02:56:29.250 --> 02:56:34.380
of passivity and draining out of the
generative drive or competing out of the

02:56:34.390 --> 02:56:37.259
generative drive because of social media.

02:56:38.170 --> 02:56:42.850
And the reason I bring this up is, again,
not because I dislike social media.

02:56:42.850 --> 02:56:46.760
I rely on and use social media for
teaching and learning extensively.

02:56:46.760 --> 02:56:47.050
Really.

02:56:47.700 --> 02:56:51.910
But in going back to the pillars that
underlie whether or not we achieve

02:56:51.910 --> 02:56:55.039
and experience agency, gratitude,
peace, contentment, and delight

02:56:56.179 --> 02:56:58.039
within the pillar of function of self.

02:56:58.529 --> 02:57:02.520
There's this thing, salience,
and what we're paying attention

02:57:02.520 --> 02:57:03.920
to, internal and external.

02:57:04.270 --> 02:57:10.810
And social media does seem to me a
unique circumstance, never before

02:57:11.040 --> 02:57:15.939
observed in human evolution, where
you have a near infinite number

02:57:15.940 --> 02:57:18.199
of environments available to you.

02:57:18.199 --> 02:57:21.039
And we know that a picture is
worth 1000 words and a movie

02:57:21.040 --> 02:57:22.610
is worth a billion pictures.

02:57:22.780 --> 02:57:24.410
When it comes to drawing our attention.

02:57:24.410 --> 02:57:28.590
I mean, you give a young child,
even an infant, an iPad, I

02:57:28.590 --> 02:57:30.020
mean, that kid is in the tunnel.

02:57:30.509 --> 02:57:32.219
I don't necessarily
think that's a bad thing.

02:57:32.280 --> 02:57:34.970
And computers and computer screens
are going to be a part of their

02:57:34.970 --> 02:57:36.520
lives now and forever, presumably.

02:57:36.520 --> 02:57:45.430
But it is the case that there are a lot
of people who perhaps have the propensity

02:57:45.430 --> 02:57:50.450
for a strong generative drive, but
because they also have a propensity for

02:57:50.450 --> 02:57:55.980
a pleasure drive, they wake up, they pick
up the phone, they look at the phone,

02:57:56.250 --> 02:57:58.439
something captivates their attention,
then they're thinking about that.

02:57:58.440 --> 02:58:01.380
It might be something that brings
them delight, but more often than

02:58:01.380 --> 02:58:05.520
not, it's something that brings
them either mild irritation or mild

02:58:05.520 --> 02:58:09.830
entertainment, maybe even intense
entertainment for a short while.

02:58:09.830 --> 02:58:16.040
But very quickly, minutes and hours go by
in which we are not engaging in the world

02:58:16.050 --> 02:58:18.870
unless we are posting valuable content.

02:58:20.059 --> 02:58:24.230
And so social media is a bit
of a drain on these drives.

02:58:24.230 --> 02:58:26.539
I mean, it taps into these
drives in very strong ways.

02:58:26.540 --> 02:58:29.750
And all one has to do is observe
the behavior of people in public

02:58:29.750 --> 02:58:35.090
spaces, now, in airports, on
trains, even in their cars.

02:58:35.100 --> 02:58:37.740
And people are essentially
watching TV all day long.

02:58:38.530 --> 02:58:44.910
And it does concern me, and I raise
it because I feel like it can distract

02:58:45.099 --> 02:58:50.570
from our generative drive in a way that
doesn't necessarily speak to any kind of,

02:58:50.570 --> 02:58:54.590
like, deep character flaw or any kind of
subconscious narrative, but just that.

02:58:54.650 --> 02:59:01.139
That salience cupboard, clearly
something within that salience

02:59:01.170 --> 02:59:07.150
cupboard is happening that's
unprecedented and very, very powerful

02:59:07.189 --> 02:59:09.260
and potentially quite destructive.

02:59:09.670 --> 02:59:09.890
Paul Conti: Yeah.

02:59:10.550 --> 02:59:15.279
I think to understand this,
I would cite this belief.

02:59:15.360 --> 02:59:18.770
I believe this to be true,
that human beings have a long

02:59:18.809 --> 02:59:22.869
history of underappreciating the
power of the discoveries that

02:59:22.869 --> 02:59:24.349
are then in their own hands.

02:59:24.590 --> 02:59:26.480
So we discover gunpowder.

02:59:26.720 --> 02:59:28.110
How long until we're shooting each other?

02:59:28.880 --> 02:59:30.480
We discover nuclear fission.

02:59:30.490 --> 02:59:32.529
Now, are we going to destroy the planet?

02:59:33.130 --> 02:59:35.549
So social media, in a
sense, it's a discovery.

02:59:35.559 --> 02:59:40.879
It's a thing that comes from what we've
figured out as humans that now is there in

02:59:41.070 --> 02:59:46.710
front of us, and big, powerful discoveries
deserve to be treated with respect.

02:59:47.300 --> 02:59:51.580
Gunpowder is very powerful, and if
people need to hunt in order to survive,

02:59:51.670 --> 02:59:54.970
gunpowder can help them hunt without
getting hurt, and we'd be more successful.

02:59:55.480 --> 02:59:58.959
Nuclear fission has provided
some good things to humanity,

02:59:58.959 --> 03:00:00.770
but it can also destroy humanity.

03:00:01.000 --> 03:00:04.330
So I think the same is true here,
that what you're talking about is

03:00:04.330 --> 03:00:08.730
something of immense power, and you
can see how, if it gets out of balance.

03:00:08.740 --> 03:00:10.190
So let's use the salient.

03:00:10.260 --> 03:00:14.419
So let's say the social
media is too salient.

03:00:14.940 --> 03:00:16.500
That's going to make a problem.

03:00:16.940 --> 03:00:20.490
If it's too salient in the sense that
the person is always looking at things

03:00:20.490 --> 03:00:21.840
that don't make them feel good enough.

03:00:22.490 --> 03:00:25.470
Well, that's not going to go well,
and that's going to affect what's

03:00:25.470 --> 03:00:27.650
in those other ten cupboards.

03:00:27.910 --> 03:00:29.244
And what is built on top of it.

03:00:29.300 --> 03:00:32.460
So then it gets into the unconscious
mind, like, oh, I thought I

03:00:32.460 --> 03:00:34.199
was good enough until now.

03:00:34.199 --> 03:00:36.950
I'm looking at all this social
media, and I realize I'm not.

03:00:36.950 --> 03:00:37.590
I mean, this is.

03:00:37.730 --> 03:00:42.340
People who treat teens often talk about
this, that you see something that you

03:00:42.340 --> 03:00:46.310
didn't often see before, where a person
who might have gotten through a lot

03:00:46.310 --> 03:00:51.239
of formative years thinking like, oh,
how I look is okay, for example, then

03:00:51.290 --> 03:00:54.590
is bombarding themselves with social
media that tells them how they look

03:00:54.590 --> 03:00:56.430
is not okay, and then that changes.

03:00:56.620 --> 03:00:57.230
Andrew Huberman: Absolutely.

03:00:57.280 --> 03:01:01.810
Or perhaps social media is just
simply absorbing a ton of time and

03:01:02.110 --> 03:01:06.820
energy, but mostly time that could
be devoted to a generative force.

03:01:07.090 --> 03:01:07.310
Paul Conti: Right.

03:01:07.750 --> 03:01:08.689
That's the other side of it.

03:01:08.689 --> 03:01:11.560
So think about the example of
the person who, I know it wasn't

03:01:11.560 --> 03:01:13.680
social media, but we were saying,
well, what if it were social media?

03:01:13.680 --> 03:01:18.360
That instead of 90 minutes a day, it's
8 hours because there's an analog there

03:01:18.360 --> 03:01:25.489
and we see a lot of this, then it's
taking something that can be good in

03:01:25.490 --> 03:01:27.260
sense, you could say even should be good.

03:01:27.450 --> 03:01:31.130
There's enough out there in
terms of learning and bolstering

03:01:31.130 --> 03:01:32.730
that why should it not be good.

03:01:33.290 --> 03:01:36.670
But it's not good because
the defenses then shift.

03:01:36.670 --> 03:01:40.980
Like if you're relying on it 10 hours a
day, there has to be some denial, right?

03:01:41.000 --> 03:01:43.349
Because there are other
things to do in the world.

03:01:43.360 --> 03:01:44.520
There has to be some avoidance.

03:01:44.520 --> 03:01:47.419
There has to be some rationalization,
like something is going on

03:01:47.420 --> 03:01:48.590
there that's not healthy.

03:01:48.910 --> 03:01:52.980
So if you tell me this person is utilizing
social media 10 hours a day, they're not

03:01:52.980 --> 03:01:55.760
looking at things that make them feel bad
about themselves, they're just doing it.

03:01:56.250 --> 03:02:00.099
Then I think, okay,
something is out of balance.

03:02:00.099 --> 03:02:03.899
Now it may be that that person's
defenses are out of balance.

03:02:03.900 --> 03:02:06.659
So think about the example of the
person with the job they didn't like.

03:02:06.660 --> 03:02:08.260
Then their defensive structure changes.

03:02:08.490 --> 03:02:11.760
Then the thing that was good
for them, they rely on too much.

03:02:11.760 --> 03:02:14.589
And now it becomes something
that's not good for them, right?

03:02:14.690 --> 03:02:18.709
So then you go and look at what
else is out of balance here.

03:02:19.210 --> 03:02:21.503
What else is driving this now?

03:02:21.570 --> 03:02:24.630
Maybe it's being driven by the change
in defense mechanisms, et cetera.

03:02:24.820 --> 03:02:28.539
Maybe it's the other way around that this
person just kind of habituated to doing

03:02:28.540 --> 03:02:30.290
more and more and more and more of it.

03:02:30.550 --> 03:02:34.530
And then you would come at it in a
different way of, okay, can you slowly

03:02:34.530 --> 03:02:39.580
but surely do less, replace the time
with things that were good before?

03:02:39.580 --> 03:02:42.759
Because you could then back that person
out to where they were before, but

03:02:42.760 --> 03:02:45.800
you're not going to back the person
out to where they were before if

03:02:45.800 --> 03:02:47.550
it's being driven by something else.

03:02:47.780 --> 03:02:49.940
So we again come to the curiosity.

03:02:50.160 --> 03:02:53.400
You tell me that person is on
social media 14 hours a day.

03:02:53.920 --> 03:02:54.920
I'm curious.

03:02:55.200 --> 03:02:58.849
I want to understand what is
the balance of those drives.

03:02:59.459 --> 03:03:03.270
You've just told me a very
powerful point about salience that

03:03:03.270 --> 03:03:04.440
doesn't sound like a good one.

03:03:04.440 --> 03:03:07.859
So already you're giving me clues
about where the drives are, which

03:03:07.880 --> 03:03:09.119
means, where's that person at?

03:03:09.349 --> 03:03:11.090
What's going on in all those cabinets?

03:03:11.090 --> 03:03:13.269
Then you give more information.

03:03:13.270 --> 03:03:14.630
Now sit and talk with the person.

03:03:14.630 --> 03:03:18.380
Now you're going to understand what
is the lay of the land here and how

03:03:18.380 --> 03:03:20.320
do we go about making it better.

03:03:21.360 --> 03:03:23.740
Andrew Huberman: I love the
concept of the generative drive.

03:03:24.160 --> 03:03:26.529
First of all, because it's pro social.

03:03:26.530 --> 03:03:29.650
It brings about great things
for us and for the world.

03:03:29.789 --> 03:03:33.090
And what is better than peace,
contentment and delight.

03:03:33.090 --> 03:03:39.260
Especially when we remind ourselves that
those are active phrases or those can be

03:03:39.380 --> 03:03:41.770
achieved and experienced inside of action.

03:03:41.770 --> 03:03:44.702
It's not just sitting, levitating,
navel gazing, that sort of thing.

03:03:44.702 --> 03:03:46.660
So it's not enlightenment, right.

03:03:46.660 --> 03:03:48.289
It's peace, contentment and delight.

03:03:50.219 --> 03:03:51.010
Paul Conti: Very big difference.

03:03:51.030 --> 03:03:52.150
Andrew Huberman: Very big difference.

03:03:52.150 --> 03:03:52.560
Yes.

03:03:53.100 --> 03:03:56.360
One of the other reasons I love this
concept of the generative drive so much

03:03:56.830 --> 03:03:59.730
is also because it is a verb state.

03:03:59.780 --> 03:04:04.140
It has to do with creating things in
us and in the world, in cultivating

03:04:04.450 --> 03:04:07.710
our experience of things and what
we do and what we say and how we

03:04:07.710 --> 03:04:09.680
respond to what others do and say.

03:04:10.389 --> 03:04:15.390
And I also like it because it's distinct
from the way that we're normally taught

03:04:15.470 --> 03:04:20.089
to think about psychological well being
or being a healthy individual, which

03:04:20.800 --> 03:04:27.300
usually centers around a discussion of
goals and values and what am I trying

03:04:27.300 --> 03:04:30.610
to focus on, and what sorts of people
do I want to engage with in the world?

03:04:30.610 --> 03:04:33.520
And certainly all of that is really
important goals and who you engage with.

03:04:33.850 --> 03:04:38.699
But I think for many people out there,
much of their time is spent thinking

03:04:38.700 --> 03:04:44.509
about other people, like how healthier
or unhealthy are the people they're

03:04:44.509 --> 03:04:49.440
dating or their friends, or what's going
on between two family members, which,

03:04:49.450 --> 03:04:51.070
of course, is fine to think about.

03:04:51.550 --> 03:04:55.750
But a lot of emphasis is placed
on our assessments of others

03:04:55.810 --> 03:04:57.379
and how those are impacting us.

03:04:58.399 --> 03:05:01.970
And in some cases, people default to
just thinking about others and their

03:05:01.970 --> 03:05:03.759
problems and seeing their problems.

03:05:03.759 --> 03:05:07.570
And what we're really talking about
here is a process of introspection

03:05:07.570 --> 03:05:10.040
and inquiry that's very structured.

03:05:10.340 --> 03:05:15.050
And as it's been laid out by
you, these two pillars, structure

03:05:15.050 --> 03:05:16.220
of self, function of self.

03:05:16.220 --> 03:05:19.800
With these ten cupboards, that might
sound like a lot of cupboards, but

03:05:19.800 --> 03:05:23.509
as we talked about in the first
episode, all of that flows up to these

03:05:24.190 --> 03:05:30.160
very simple ideals and concepts and
action, states and ways of being.

03:05:30.240 --> 03:05:34.350
And to me, there's nothing more
powerful than the statement

03:05:34.360 --> 03:05:38.959
that what we are all seeking are
states of agency and gratitude.

03:05:39.420 --> 03:05:42.580
Because, again, to go back to
the analogy of physical fitness,

03:05:43.330 --> 03:05:47.910
there are not an infinite number
of different physical states or

03:05:47.910 --> 03:05:51.140
states of fitness that one can seek.

03:05:51.160 --> 03:05:53.620
There's endurance, there's
strength, there's flexibility,

03:05:53.620 --> 03:05:56.950
there's dynamic movement, there's
explosiveness, there's speed.

03:05:56.950 --> 03:05:58.959
There are a bunch of subtleties to it.

03:05:59.340 --> 03:06:04.850
But here it really seems that the
psyche, ourselves and our mental

03:06:04.850 --> 03:06:09.839
health is really tractable if we
turn the lens and we look inward.

03:06:11.050 --> 03:06:14.730
Paul Conti: Yes, I think that hits
upon a very, very important point

03:06:14.730 --> 03:06:18.549
as we talk about understanding
oneself and the process of change.

03:06:19.170 --> 03:06:22.070
And I would describe that
as rational aspiration.

03:06:22.470 --> 03:06:25.530
So let's use the physical health example.

03:06:25.780 --> 03:06:31.520
If I think, okay, I want to be healthier,
I want to have more strength, I want

03:06:31.530 --> 03:06:34.900
to have more endurance, and I might
even have ideas of what that would be.

03:06:34.900 --> 03:06:37.789
I want to be able to run a certain
distance in a certain time, lift

03:06:37.790 --> 03:06:40.220
a certain amount of weight, I
have an idea of what that is it.

03:06:40.420 --> 03:06:44.000
But rational aspiration
is rooted in our present.

03:06:44.230 --> 03:06:44.596
I'm aware that there's a me
now that isn't in that state.

03:06:44.596 --> 03:06:54.039
And I'm aware that there are things
that I'm going to do to get to that

03:06:54.049 --> 03:06:57.939
state and I'm not that dreading them,
like, okay, they'll be difficult, right?

03:06:57.940 --> 03:07:00.000
But that's okay, I can do
difficult things, I can take

03:07:00.000 --> 03:07:01.400
pride in doing difficult things.

03:07:01.650 --> 03:07:03.769
And that's how we all achieve things.

03:07:04.030 --> 03:07:07.810
So I see myself in the present
because of course goals are good,

03:07:07.810 --> 03:07:12.730
and that's true as long as we're
still living our lives in the present

03:07:12.750 --> 03:07:18.410
because otherwise goals just become
fantasies or things we want to possess.

03:07:18.600 --> 03:07:22.579
So if I'm aware of the state of physical
health I'm in right now, and I'm aware

03:07:22.580 --> 03:07:25.330
of the state of physical health I want
to be in, and I know there's a bunch

03:07:25.330 --> 03:07:29.599
of pathways I could take to get there,
but I have to think about it, figure

03:07:29.600 --> 03:07:32.760
it out, do those things, and then
I'm going to navigate myself there.

03:07:32.820 --> 03:07:34.320
That's how the whole process is good.

03:07:34.790 --> 03:07:36.679
I don't feel bad about myself now.

03:07:36.910 --> 03:07:38.789
I recognize something
I would like to change.

03:07:38.790 --> 03:07:40.569
I'm not saying, oh, you're
a loser, because you don't

03:07:40.570 --> 03:07:41.630
have those things, right?

03:07:42.469 --> 03:07:43.860
I feel good about myself now.

03:07:44.120 --> 03:07:47.230
I recognize there's something I want
and there's going to be a process,

03:07:47.270 --> 03:07:51.390
a process across time, across effort
that's going to navigate me there.

03:07:51.670 --> 03:07:54.480
Then when I get there, I
feel good about being there.

03:07:55.130 --> 03:07:58.119
It's very, very different
if I think I want that.

03:07:58.300 --> 03:07:59.999
I want to possess in a sense.

03:08:00.200 --> 03:08:02.900
I want to possess the ability to run
a certain distance in a certain time.

03:08:03.009 --> 03:08:03.850
I just want the thing.

03:08:03.850 --> 03:08:04.909
I'm covetous of the thing.

03:08:05.840 --> 03:08:07.770
That is not good, right?

03:08:07.770 --> 03:08:10.140
Because a person then often
is denigrating to the self.

03:08:10.140 --> 03:08:13.369
Not always, but that's a motivation to
go out and get that thing that's better.

03:08:13.690 --> 03:08:17.550
And they're really lamenting
the process of getting.

03:08:17.790 --> 03:08:19.630
They just want something as an endpoint.

03:08:20.059 --> 03:08:24.220
And that doesn't make for happiness,
it doesn't make for even the

03:08:24.449 --> 03:08:28.740
humility and humility in action,
the gratitude, the humility is,

03:08:28.740 --> 03:08:30.070
I can't just do that overnight.

03:08:30.070 --> 03:08:31.220
I'm going to have to work hard.

03:08:31.250 --> 03:08:32.529
People have to work hard.

03:08:32.809 --> 03:08:34.189
I'm no different than anybody else.

03:08:34.210 --> 03:08:34.939
I'm not special.

03:08:34.940 --> 03:08:38.610
I got to get in there and work and use the
elbow grease, and then I'll get healthier.

03:08:38.610 --> 03:08:40.049
Like all of that is good.

03:08:40.049 --> 03:08:44.080
That I just want to possess
something is not good.

03:08:44.080 --> 03:08:48.550
And that's why people in scenarios like
this, they might go through maybe in

03:08:48.550 --> 03:08:50.880
an unthinking way or they're gutting
it out or wherever they go, and they

03:08:50.880 --> 03:08:54.509
get that thing, right, but then that t
hing is not enough and they want more.

03:08:54.509 --> 03:08:59.070
Now, there's nothing wrong with wanting
more if it's the healthy inaction of self.

03:08:59.260 --> 03:09:00.289
I'm going to now map my way.

03:09:00.289 --> 03:09:01.110
This feels better.

03:09:01.400 --> 03:09:06.010
I want to map myself from here to the
next level of better physical fitness.

03:09:06.670 --> 03:09:08.470
That's different than
I just want that thing.

03:09:08.470 --> 03:09:11.849
Because then if I get it,
it won't be good enough.

03:09:12.020 --> 03:09:13.000
It doesn't make me happy.

03:09:13.000 --> 03:09:14.340
It doesn't satisfy me.

03:09:14.620 --> 03:09:19.580
And that's the unhealthy state of
just wanting things to possess them.

03:09:19.610 --> 03:09:21.930
And then we don't feel good
about them, which is the

03:09:21.930 --> 03:09:23.210
thought of if you give people.

03:09:23.280 --> 03:09:26.110
If you give a person something,
they'll resent you for it.

03:09:26.240 --> 03:09:31.460
Again, we're painting it in a sort
of certain way, the context of that

03:09:31.509 --> 03:09:34.990
statement, which I used to hear
a lot, even when I was younger,

03:09:35.049 --> 03:09:36.250
people would say that, right.

03:09:36.250 --> 03:09:38.090
And what were they trying to get at?

03:09:38.290 --> 03:09:41.490
What they were getting at is it
doesn't feel good if you didn't

03:09:41.530 --> 03:09:42.800
work for something, right?

03:09:42.800 --> 03:09:46.080
Like if you didn't work very hard
and you got a C, but I give you

03:09:46.080 --> 03:09:47.369
an A or somebody gives me an A.

03:09:48.240 --> 03:09:50.639
I know that that's not good.

03:09:50.639 --> 03:09:52.399
I know that I got the thing.

03:09:52.410 --> 03:09:53.055
I got the A.

03:09:53.260 --> 03:09:56.520
And I might feel happy in the moment
because I wanted that thing, but

03:09:56.520 --> 03:09:59.660
there's no real pleasure in it.

03:09:59.710 --> 03:10:02.380
There's no satisfaction, there's
no contentment, there's no sense of

03:10:02.380 --> 03:10:03.560
self, there's nothing generative.

03:10:03.760 --> 03:10:05.970
I didn't work hard enough
to go from a C to an A.

03:10:08.650 --> 03:10:11.340
And that really brings us back
to the self that we're growing

03:10:11.340 --> 03:10:14.150
on top of the structure.

03:10:14.180 --> 03:10:14.490
Right.

03:10:14.700 --> 03:10:17.230
And how that self is functioning.

03:10:17.600 --> 03:10:18.580
How it's striving.

03:10:18.580 --> 03:10:20.869
Because now we're really
talking about strivings.

03:10:20.889 --> 03:10:24.690
And if I'm going to strive for something
and work hard to get it, will I get the

03:10:24.690 --> 03:10:26.280
good feeling on the other side of it.

03:10:26.280 --> 03:10:28.640
And now we're living in
the generative space.

03:10:29.430 --> 03:10:31.480
Andrew Huberman: Well, I love the
structure of what you've laid out.

03:10:31.520 --> 03:10:31.870
Again...

03:10:31.880 --> 03:10:32.270
Paul Conti: Thank you.

03:10:32.550 --> 03:10:34.789
Andrew Huberman: The pillars of
structure of self and function of

03:10:34.790 --> 03:10:39.719
self, with ten cupboards between
the two of them that, when explored,

03:10:40.230 --> 03:10:41.490
can seem a little bit complex.

03:10:41.490 --> 03:10:45.010
But they're really some very
straightforward types of inquiry that

03:10:45.190 --> 03:10:50.960
anyone can go about, about self awareness
and address potential defense mechanisms.

03:10:50.960 --> 03:10:53.550
What we're conscious of, maybe
what we're not conscious of.

03:10:53.590 --> 03:10:57.310
Look at our behaviors and our strivings
and how that flows up to these simple

03:10:57.310 --> 03:11:02.099
ideals again, of empowerment, humility,
agency and gratitude as verbs.

03:11:02.450 --> 03:11:06.200
And then from that, peace,
contentment and delight.

03:11:06.530 --> 03:11:12.400
And the generative drive, which, gosh,
if there ever was a more powerful

03:11:12.400 --> 03:11:16.960
concept and something to strive for,
I don't think it exists, because the

03:11:16.960 --> 03:11:22.010
generative drive is extraordinary in the
number of different ways it plays out,

03:11:22.010 --> 03:11:24.450
and it seems always positively right.

03:11:25.260 --> 03:11:28.789
And of course, the aggressive drive,
the pleasure drive, exists to varying

03:11:28.790 --> 03:11:34.720
extents in all of us, but cannot be
allowed to overcome the generative

03:11:34.750 --> 03:11:36.880
drive if we're going to really thrive.

03:11:37.219 --> 03:11:40.490
So thank you again so
much for this framework.

